/* AQA GCSE English Language (8700) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Sonnet 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8700', KITS: {"critical-reading":{"id":"AQA-8700","topic":"critical-reading","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because 28 of the marks across both papers — Paper 1 Question 1 (4 marks), Paper 2 Question 1 (4 marks) and Paper 1 Question 4 (20 marks) — reward nothing but careful, honest reading: finding what a text actually says, working out what it implies but does not state, testing whether its evidence can be trusted, and judging its effect on a reader with a position that can be argued rather than merely asserted. Every other reading skill in this course — analysing language, structure or comparing two writers — depends on this room's habits first, since a student who cannot locate, infer and evaluate accurately has nothing reliable to analyse.","idea":[{"h":"Identifying and interpreting information","code":"3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information","t":"Before a reader can comment on anything, they must be able to say accurately what a text states and implies. Explicit information is stated directly; implicit information has to be worked out from wording, tone or detail the writer has chosen deliberately. The point-marked tasks on both papers reward only information that is true and specific to the named section — a general summary, or a whole section copied out, shows no real identification and earns nothing, however much text it covers."},{"h":"Reading for a purpose","code":"3.1.1 reading in different ways for different purposes","t":"A reader does not read every sentence the same way: skimming for the gist, scanning for one detail, and reading closely for argument or method are different skills used for different jobs. Across a single hour, the papers move a student from a narrow four-mark search through a short section, to close reading of one extract, to judging a whole text — and a top-level answer shows that it chose the right reading for each, answering the point-marked question only from the named lines and in points of its own, and arguing the extended question from close reading of the writer's methods rather than from a general impression of the text."},{"h":"Drawing inferences and justifying them","code":"3.1.1 drawing inferences","t":"An inference is a meaning the text implies but does not state outright, reached by noticing what a choice of word, an omission or a change of behaviour suggests. The mark schemes reward inference over paraphrase at every level above the lowest: a Level 1 answer restates what the text says, while a higher-level answer says what the text's choices suggest without saying it directly, and supports that claim with the exact detail that licenses it."},{"h":"Supporting an opinion with evidence","code":"3.1.1 supporting a point of view","t":"An opinion about a text only earns credit once it is tied to specific evidence within that text — a general impression with no textual support cannot be marked, however reasonable it sounds. The strongest answers select a small number of well-chosen details rather than a long list of loosely related ones, because a single quotation closely explained shows more control than several quotations merely gathered together."},{"h":"Testing a text for bias","code":"3.1.1 identifying bias and misuse of evidence","t":"A critical reader does not simply accept what a text states; they ask who is speaking, why, and what that purpose might lead them to include, omit or phrase in a particular way. Bias does not require a lie — a text built entirely from true statements can still mislead through selective detail, one-sided emphasis or wording chosen to steer a reader's sympathy. A top-level answer never simply announces that a text is biased: it quotes the wording that does the shaping, says which view that wording serves, and weighs how far the writer's purpose accounts for what has been left out."},{"h":"Evaluating effect on the reader","code":"3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text","t":"Evaluation goes beyond noticing a method to judging what that method actually does to a reader, and how successfully it does it — not simply describing a technique, but weighing its effect and deciding how convincing or powerful that effect is. The highest-level answers evaluate critically and in detail, meaning they take a clear position on how well a text achieves its effect rather than listing features it happens to contain."},{"h":"Recognising different, equally valid responses","code":"3.1.1 recognising the possibility of different responses to a text","t":"Texts are read by different people who bring different experiences to them, so a mark scheme built on the principle that the guidance given is \"neither exhaustive nor prescriptive\" expects a range of readings, not one correct answer. What is rewarded is not agreement with any particular view but the quality of the argument a reader builds from the text's own evidence, which means a confident, well-supported disagreement can score as highly as agreement."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading for four true points, restricted to one section","setup":"The task: 'Read again the first paragraph of Passage A. List four things from this part of the text about Maya.' The paragraph reads: \"The pier lights came on twenty minutes before the storm did, which Maya took as a kind of warning. She had stood at the ticket booth since four, watching the tide drag itself back from the concrete in long, complaining ropes of foam. A gull sat on the railing without moving, as if it, too, were waiting for permission to leave. The last ferry of the season was due at six, and the harbourmaster had already told her twice that it might not come at all. Rain had been forecast since noon and had arrived exactly on time, fine at first, then heavier, until the whole bay seemed to blur at the edges like a photograph left in water. Somewhere behind her a rope slapped against a mast, over and over, patient as a clock.\"","steps":["Rule out the details that belong to the weather, the sea or the rest of the scene rather than to Maya: the storm arriving after the lights, the tide's ropes of foam, the motionless gull, the six o'clock sailing, the rain, and the rope slapping behind her.","From what is left, mark every place where the paragraph says something specific about Maya herself: what she made of the pier lights, how long she had been at the ticket booth, what she had been watching from it, and what the harbourmaster had told her.","Turn four of those marked details into one short point each, in your own words: she read the pier lights as a kind of warning; she had been at the ticket booth since four; she had been watching the tide go out; the harbourmaster had twice warned her the ferry might not come.","Stop there — four separate, true points about Maya is all this task credits, and a fifth point, or a detail about the weather instead, adds nothing."],"cues":["Which details in this paragraph are not about Maya at all?","Of the lines that are left, which ones say something specific about Maya herself?","How do you turn a marked detail into a point without copying the whole sentence out?","How many separate points does this task need, and what happens once you have that many?"]},{"title":"Drawing an inference from a single choice of word","setup":"The task: 'What can a reader infer about how certain Maya feels that the ferry will come, beyond what is directly stated?' The relevant lines: \"'They always say it might not come,' he said, to the sea rather than to her. 'It always comes.' She wanted to believe him.\"","steps":["Underline the one word in these lines that reports Maya's own mind rather than the old man's speech: \"wanted\".","Notice the gap between 'wanted to believe' and simply 'believed' — the text does not say she is convinced.","Ask what kind of person needs to want to believe something: someone who is not yet certain of it.","State the inference as a claim about her state of mind — her hope is real but not settled — rather than repeating the quotation."],"cues":["Which single word here reports what Maya herself is doing, rather than what the old man says?","What is the difference in meaning between 'she believed him' and the actual wording?","What does needing to 'want' to believe something suggest about a person's certainty?","How do you turn that gap into a stated inference, not just a repeated quotation?"]},{"title":"Spotting bias in a letter written to reassure","setup":"The task: 'Identify one place in Passage B where Edmund's own wording suggests his praise of the counting-house is not simply objective.' The body of the letter runs: \"I write in haste, for Mr Pemberton allows us but the quarter hour before the counting-house closes its doors, and I would not have you think me idle. You ask whether I am content, and I answer that I am, though I own the days are long and the ledger unforgiving of any hand that trembles. We are at our desks by seven, and there we remain, with scarce a word exchanged between one man and the next, until the lamps are lit and lit again. Mr Pemberton says that a clerk who complains of tired eyes has not yet learned to love his work, and I confess I did not care for the saying when first I heard it, though I begin now to see some wisdom in it. It is a hard school, Father, but I would rather be hardened here than softened at home, and I beg you not to write again of bringing me back before Michaelmas. Mr Pemberton has this very week praised my column of figures before the whole office, which I set down here only because I know it will please my mother.\"","steps":["Locate a moment where Edmund admits he once felt differently from how he now claims to feel.","Notice the shift in his own words — from 'did not care for the saying' to 'begin now to see some wisdom in it' — a change, not a settled view.","Locate a second moment where he states outright why he is including a detail: 'only because I know it will please my mother'.","Explain what this reveals: a letter written to reassure can shape which facts are chosen and how warmly they are phrased, even where nothing stated is false."],"cues":["Where does Edmund admit to having changed his mind, rather than simply reporting a settled belief?","What does that change of position suggest about how genuine his current praise is?","Where does Edmund tell you directly why he includes a particular detail?","What does that admitted purpose suggest about how trustworthy the letter is as a plain account of his life?"]},{"title":"Building a qualified evaluation paragraph","setup":"The task: 'Focus this part of your answer on the second part of Passage A, from \"She wasn't alone on the jetty\" to the end. A student, having read this section of the text, said: \"The writer makes the reader feel that the waiting on the jetty is full of quiet hope, not despair.\" To what extent do you agree?' Relevant lines: \"he watched the horizon the way other people watched a kettle: certain that something was about to happen, unwilling to look away in case it happened without him,\" and, at the close, \"a shape that might have been the ferry, or might only have been the storm arranging itself into a shape she wanted to see.\"","steps":["State your own impression first: the passage does lean toward hope, but the writer keeps that hope unsettled rather than triumphant.","Name the method that creates the hopeful impression: the metaphor comparing the old man's vigil to watching a kettle, which makes his certainty feel patient rather than desperate.","Judge the effect of that method on the reader: it domesticates the waiting, inviting the reader to trust his calm rather than share Maya's doubt.","Weigh that impression against the closing image, where the shape on the water is left uncertain, and reach a judgement that qualifies rather than simply agrees or disagrees."],"cues":["What is your own position on the statement, before you look at any evidence?","Which specific method creates the hopeful impression, and what kind of comparison is it?","What effect does that particular comparison have on how the reader responds, rather than just what it shows?","What does the passage's final image do to that first impression, and how should your judgement change to reflect it?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between paraphrasing a text and inferring from it?","a":"Paraphrasing restates what the text already says; inferring draws out a meaning the text implies without stating it."},{"q":"Why might a completely truthful, first-person account still be biased?","a":"Because what it selects, omits or how it phrases things can still favour one view."},{"q":"In a 'to what extent do you agree' answer, what must a top-level response do with a chosen quotation, beyond naming it?","a":"Evaluate its effect on the reader, not just identify or describe it."},{"q":"Can a confident disagreement with a given statement score as highly as agreement?","a":"Yes, provided it is argued from evidence in the text, since the mark scheme rewards the quality of the argument, not the position taken."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 1 Question 1 is 4 marks, AO1, and not levelled. Its stem is \"Read again the first part of the Source from lines 1 to 7. List four things from this part of the text about…\" — one mark per point, four points, and nothing beyond the named lines.","Paper 2 Question 1 is also 4 marks, AO1, and not levelled. Its stem is \"Read again the first part of Source A from lines 1 to 15. Choose four statements below which are TRUE\" — shade a maximum of four of the eight lettered boxes.","Paper 1 Question 4 is 20 marks, AO4, and always names its section first: \"Focus this part of your answer on the second part of the Source from line 19 to the end\", then quotes a student's statement and asks \"To what extent do you agree?\"","Both papers run 1 hour 45 minutes for 80 marks: 40 for Section A (Reading) and 40 for Section B (Writing). The printed advice is \"about 15 minutes reading through the Source\", then \"about 45 minutes\" on Section A and \"about 45 minutes\" on Section B — so inside Section A a 4-mark question is worth about 5 minutes and the 20-mark Question 4 about 22.","The only tariffs that exist on either paper are 4, 8, 12, 16, 20 and 40 marks: Paper 1 Questions 2 and 3 are 8 marks each (AO2), Paper 2 Question 2 is 8 (AO1), Question 3 is 12 (AO2), Question 4 is 16 (AO3), and Question 5 on both papers is 40 (24 for content and organisation, 16 for technical accuracy)."],"questions":[{"q":"Read again the second paragraph of Passage A, from the beginning to \"unwilling to look away in case it happened without him.\" List four things from this part of the text about the old man.","a":"Any four true points about the old man, e.g.: he wore a yellow coat; he sat on the single bench; he had a canvas holdall between his boots; he was unwilling to look away in case the thing he expected happened without him.","sol":"1. The old man was wearing a yellow coat.\n2. He was sitting on the single bench.\n3. He had a canvas holdall between his boots.\n4. He would not look away from the horizon, in case what he was waiting for happened without him.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["The section runs from Maya's discovery that she has company to the end of the sentence about the kettle; the talk that follows is out of bounds.","Sort the sentence into what belongs to the man — what he wears, where he sits, what he has with him, how he watches — and what belongs to the jetty, the horizon or Maya.","Begin at the words 'An old man' and work forwards through the sentence, stopping the moment you have four separate details about him."]},{"q":"Read again from \"When Maya finally sat beside him\" to \"She wanted to believe him.\" in Passage A. List four things from this part of the text about what the old man does and says.","a":"Any four true points about the old man himself, e.g.: he didn't turn his head when Maya sat beside him; he spoke to the sea rather than to her; he said people always say the ferry might not come; he said it always comes.","sol":"1. The old man did not turn his head when Maya sat down beside him.\n2. He spoke to the sea rather than to her.\n3. He said that people always say the ferry might not come.\n4. He said that it always comes.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 drawing inferences","3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["Only the three short sentences from Maya sitting down to her wish to believe him count; the rain inside her collar comes after them.","The focus is what the old man does and says, so his one reaction and his two sentences of speech are your material, and what Maya wants is not.","Begin with what he does as Maya sits beside him, then take his speech one sentence at a time."]},{"q":"Read again from \"Mr Pemberton says that a clerk who complains of tired eyes\" to \"because I know it will please my mother.\" in Passage B. List four things from this part of the text about how Edmund feels about staying at the counting-house.","a":"Any four true points about Edmund's own feelings, e.g.: he did not care for Mr Pemberton's saying when he first heard it; he now begins to see some wisdom in it; he calls the place a hard school; he would rather be hardened there than softened at home; he begs his father not to write again of bringing him back before Michaelmas.","sol":"1. Edmund did not care for Mr Pemberton's saying when he first heard it, and treats that dislike as something to confess.\n2. He now begins to see some wisdom in the saying, so his view of the work has shifted since then.\n3. He calls the counting-house a hard school, admitting its difficulty to his father outright.\n4. He would rather be hardened there than softened at home, and begs his father not to write again of bringing him back before Michaelmas.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 identifying bias and misuse of evidence","3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["The section starts at Mr Pemberton's saying about tired eyes and runs to the mention of Edmund's mother; the opening of the letter is outside it.","Only Edmund's own feelings count, so the saying itself, and the praise of his column of figures, give you nothing here.","Begin at the words 'and I confess', where the letter turns from Mr Pemberton's words to Edmund's own, and work forwards from there."]},{"q":"Read again the first paragraph of Passage C, from the beginning to \"what love of the work looked like.\" List four things from this part of the text about why the writer worked long hours.","a":"Any four true reasons, e.g.: the writer took long hours as proof of commitment; took them as proof of ambition, or of a seriousness that gets you noticed; stayed late to avoid being called a clock-watcher; believed the hours were simply what love of the work looked like.","sol":"1. The writer thought a long day at the office was proof of commitment.\n2. The writer thought it was proof of ambition, or of the kind of seriousness that gets you noticed.\n3. The writer stayed until seven or eight for two years simply to avoid being one of the people the first boss called the clock-watchers.\n4. The writer's own account of the hours at the time was that they were simply what love of the work looked like.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 reading in different ways for different purposes","3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["The named part ends with the first paragraph of the column, before the flu and the four days offline.","The focus is the reasons for the hours, so what the writer now thinks those hours were worth is not one of them.","Begin at the words 'proof of something' and work forwards through the paragraph, taking one reason at a time."]},{"q":"Read again the final sentence of Passage A, from \"The rain had found its way inside her collar\" to the end. List four things from this part of the text about what Maya can see.","a":"Any four true points about what she can see, e.g.: the light on the water had turned the colour of a bruise; a shape was showing out past the harbour wall; that shape was growing very slowly; it might have been the ferry, or might only have been the storm.","sol":"1. The light on the water had turned the colour of a bruise.\n2. Out past the harbour wall a shape was beginning to show.\n3. That shape was growing very slowly.\n4. She cannot tell whether the shape is the ferry or only the storm arranging itself into a shape she wanted to see.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 recognising the possibility of different responses to a text","3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["The section is the passage's final sentence only, and the focus is what Maya can see, so the rain inside her collar is not a point.","Read the sentence in the order it is written: what is on the water comes first, then what lies beyond the harbour wall, then what that might be.","Begin at the harbour wall and finish the sentence from there, keeping one point back for what Maya cannot be sure of."]},{"q":"Read again the second paragraph of Passage C, from \"It took a bout of the flu\" to the end. Choose four statements below which are TRUE. A) The writer was off work for four days with flu. B) A colleague found it difficult to cover the writer's workload during that absence. C) The writer believes the company missed nothing during that absence. D) The writer argues that people should stop working altogether. E) The writer describes a desk as being like a confessional. F) The writer says the hours worked are not a measure of a person's worth. G) The writer's old boss has stopped working long hours. H) The writer no longer envies the old boss.","a":"A, C, F, H are true.","sol":"A is true — \"four days offline\" following \"a bout of the flu\". C is true — \"nothing that the company missed\". F is true — \"the hours you put in are not a measure of your worth\". H is true — \"I no longer envy him\". B is false — a colleague \"quietly cleared my inbox without incident\". D is false — \"I am not arguing for idleness\". E is false — the text says \"a desk is not a confessional\". G is false — \"My old boss still works past nine most nights\".","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["Check each statement only against this one paragraph of Passage C.","Look for a statement that reverses what the paragraph actually says, not just one that adds new information.","Choose exactly four — no more, no fewer."]},{"q":"Read again the first paragraph of Passage A. List four things from this part of the text about the weather and the sea.","a":"Any four true points drawn only from the first paragraph, e.g.: the tide was pulling back from the concrete; the rain arrived exactly when it had been forecast; the rain grew heavier, having started fine; the bay blurred at the edges in the rain.","sol":"1. The tide was going out, dragging itself back from the concrete in long, complaining ropes of foam.\n2. The rain had been forecast since noon and arrived exactly on time.\n3. The rain grew heavier as it went on, having been fine at first.\n4. The whole bay seemed to blur at the edges, like a photograph left in water.","m":4,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["The first paragraph ends with the rope slapping against the mast; nothing from the jetty scene that follows counts.","Weather and sea only, so Maya at the ticket booth, the gull on the railing and the six o'clock sailing all have to go.","Begin at the paragraph's second sentence and work forwards, since the rain is returned to more than once and each return says something new."]},{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage B, from \"My dear Father\" to \"though I begin now to see some wisdom in it.\" Choose four statements below which are TRUE. A) Edmund works at a school. B) Edmund's employer is called Mr Pemberton. C) Edmund says the working days are short. D) Edmund's desk work begins at seven. E) Edmund says he loved the saying about tired eyes from the moment he first heard it. F) Edmund tells his father that he is content. G) Edmund says the clerks talk with one another constantly through the day. H) Edmund is writing in the quarter hour before the counting-house closes.","a":"B, D, F, H are true.","sol":"B is true — his employer is named \"Mr Pemberton\". D is true — \"We are at our desks by seven\". F is true — \"You ask whether I am content, and I answer that I am\". H is true — \"Mr Pemberton allows us but the quarter hour before the counting-house closes its doors\". A is false — he writes from a counting-house, not a school. C is false — \"I own the days are long\". E is false — he \"did not care for the saying when first I heard it\". G is false — there is \"scarce a word exchanged between one man and the next\".","m":4,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information","3.1.1 identifying bias and misuse of evidence"],"hints":["Check each statement only against the named first part of Passage B, not against the rest of the letter or general knowledge of the period.","Look for a statement that reverses what the letter actually says, not just one that adds new information.","Choose exactly four — no more, no fewer."]},{"q":"Read again the first half of Passage C, up to \"a different job.\" List four things from this part of the text about the writer's working habits at that time.","a":"Any four true points, e.g.: the writer stayed at the office until seven or eight; answered emails from bed; answered them on trains and in queues; once answered them during a leaving party for a different job.","sol":"1. The writer stayed at the office until seven or eight.\n2. The writer answered emails from bed.\n3. The writer answered them on trains and in queues.\n4. The writer once answered them during the writer's own leaving party for a different job.","m":4,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["The named half of the column stops at the leaving party, so the flu and the four days offline are outside it.","Working habits are what is asked for, not the reasons the writer gives for keeping them or the verdict passed on them later.","Begin at the sentences that start 'I answered' and work forwards one sentence at a time."]},{"q":"Read again the part of Passage C from \"I used to think a long day at the office was proof of something\" to \"simply to avoid being one.\" Choose four statements below which are TRUE. A) The writer's first boss called the people who left at half past five \"the clock-watchers\". B) The writer's first boss used that phrase approvingly. C) The writer has always believed that a long day at the office proves nothing. D) The writer suggests that long hours may be a sign of bad management. E) The writer was warned at the start what the long hours might really prove. F) The writer kept to the later hours for two years. G) The writer's first boss made the same small gesture each time that phrase was used. H) The writer left the office at half past five during those two years.","a":"A, D, F, G are true.","sol":"A is true — the first boss called the people who left at half past five \"the clock-watchers\". D is true — \"Nobody warned me that it might just be proof of bad management\". F is true — \"for two years I stayed until seven or eight\". G is true — the phrase came \"always with the same small curl of the lip\". B is false — that curl of the lip marks contempt, not approval. C is false — \"I used to think a long day at the office was proof of something\". E is false — \"Nobody warned me\". H is false — the writer stayed \"until seven or eight simply to avoid being one\" of the people who left at half past five.","m":4,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information"],"hints":["Check every statement against these three sentences only: the flu, the cleared inbox and the old boss all come later in the column.","Two statements turn on the first boss's manner rather than on anything the boss actually says, so decide what the passage shows about that manner.","Start with the statements that name half past five, test each against the hours the passage says the writer kept, and shade exactly four in the end."]},{"q":"Focus this part of your answer on the first part of Passage A, from the beginning to \"waiting for permission to leave.\" A student, having read this section of the text, said: 'The writer makes Maya's waiting feel like something she has no power to end.' To what extent do you agree?","a":"Largely agree: the section fills itself with stilled images of things waiting on someone else's permission, though Maya's own reading of the signs leaves her some power to understand, if not to end, the wait.","sol":"I largely agree, because almost everything the writer puts into this opening is either held still or waiting on something outside itself. Maya's own position is fixed first: \"She had stood at the ticket booth since four\" is a flat statement of duration that gives the wait a beginning but no ending, so the reader feels it stretching on without a limit she controls. The only real movement is given to the sea, and even that is grudging: the tide is shown to \"drag itself back from the concrete\" in \"long, complaining ropes of foam\", where the personification in \"complaining\" makes even withdrawal sound reluctant and pained, as though the water is no freer to leave than she is. The clearest support is the simile of the gull, which \"sat on the railing without moving, as if it, too, were waiting for permission to leave\": the small word \"too\" quietly folds Maya into the comparison, so the reader is shown, without being told, that she is waiting on a permission she cannot grant herself. Even the scene's timekeeping is handed to the weather: the lights come on \"twenty minutes before the storm did\", so the moment is measured against the storm rather than against anything Maya can do. Against this, that same sentence shows her interpreting rather than merely enduring, since the lights are something \"Maya took as a kind of warning\". I therefore agree strongly, while noting that the writer leaves her the power to read her wait, even as he denies her the power to end it.","m":20,"d":3,"codes":["3.1.1 supporting a point of view","3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text","3.1.1 recognising the possibility of different responses to a text"],"hints":["Decide first whether this section shows Maya able to act, or only able to watch, and keep testing that against every detail.","Look for images of things that are still, held back, or waiting on someone else's permission, and name the method each time rather than only describing it.","Start with the gull on the railing, and ask what the single word 'too' quietly does to Maya's position."]},{"q":"Focus this part of your answer on the part of Passage A from \"She wasn't alone on the jetty\" to \"unwilling to look away in case it happened without him.\" A student, having read this section of the text, said: 'The old man is a comforting presence, and the writer wants the reader to trust him completely.' To what extent do you agree?","a":"Partly agree: his ordinariness and his stillness are genuinely comforting, but the metaphor the writer chooses for his watching makes his certainty look like superstition rather than knowledge.","sol":"There is real force to this view. The first thing the writer does is take Maya's isolation away — \"She wasn't alone on the jetty\" — so the old man arrives as relief before he has been described at all, and the description that follows is reassuringly ordinary: a \"yellow coat\", \"the single bench\", \"a canvas holdall between his boots\". The yellow of his coat is the only colour named in the section, a warm one in a scene of rain and blurred grey, and the holdall between his boots quietly implies a man who expects to travel, which is exactly the belief Maya needs; his stillness then reads as calm authority, since he is \"certain that something was about to happen\" and shows no sign of doubt. The writer complicates any complete trust, however, through the metaphor he chooses for that watching: \"he watched the horizon the way other people watched a kettle\". A kettle is domestic, trivial and, crucially, something a watcher cannot hurry, so the comparison makes his vigil faintly comic rather than expert, and invites the reader to smile at him even while leaning on him. The clause that completes it turns his certainty into anxiety: he is \"unwilling to look away in case it happened without him\", and a man who fears missing the moment is not a man who knows it is coming. He is comforting, then, but comforting in the way a superstition is comforting, so I would only partly agree: the writer offers his calm as something to lean on while quietly marking it as belief rather than knowledge.","m":20,"d":3,"codes":["3.1.1 supporting a point of view","3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text"],"hints":["Weigh the evidence that supports trusting him against evidence that the writer keeps this open.","Look closely at the metaphor describing how he watches, and judge what it adds as well as what it shows.","Start with the sentence 'She wasn't alone on the jetty' and ask what it does for the reader before the man has been described at all."]},{"q":"Focus this part of your answer on the part of Passage A from \"The last ferry of the season was due at six\" to \"patient as a clock.\" A student, having read this section of the text, said: 'The writer's description of the weather here leaves the reader in no doubt that the ferry will not come.' To what extent do you agree?","a":"Only partly agree: the weather is described ominously, but the section itself keeps the outcome genuinely uncertain rather than settled.","sol":"The weather here is undeniably ominous. The rain's precision — it had been \"forecast since noon and had arrived exactly on time\" — makes the storm feel scheduled rather than accidental, so the reader is invited to treat it as something that cannot be argued with, and its progression from \"fine at first, then heavier\" mirrors a situation steadily worsening. The simile that follows goes further: the bay \"seemed to blur at the edges like a photograph left in water\" rubs out the very horizon a ferry would have to appear on, and the comparison to a ruined photograph carries a suggestion of something spoiled past mending. Even the sound behind Maya is turned against her, since the rope slapping \"over and over\" is called \"patient as a clock\", a simile that hands the measuring of her wait to the weather rather than to any timetable. Yet the section stops short of settling the ferry's fate. Its strongest statement about the crossing is reported, not narrated: the harbourmaster \"had already told her twice that it might not come at all\", a hedged possibility voiced by a character, and \"might not\" is a long way from saying it will not. Against it stands a plain fact the weather never cancels — the ferry \"was due at six\". The writer builds dread while withholding confirmation, so I would only partly agree: the description makes the reader fear the worst, but this section itself leaves the outcome genuinely open.","m":20,"d":3,"codes":["3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text","3.1.1 supporting a point of view"],"hints":["Decide first whether this section actually confirms the ferry's fate, or only creates a feeling about it.","Look for comparisons that unsettle the weather and the sea, and for language that only reports a possibility.","Start with the rain's exact timing, then weigh it against what the harbourmaster is reported to have said."]},{"q":"Focus this part of your answer on the part of Passage A from \"When Maya finally sat beside him\" to the end. A student, having read this section of the text, said: 'The writer makes the reader feel far more sympathy for Maya than for the old man.' To what extent do you agree?","a":"Largely agree: the reader is given direct access to Maya's hope and doubt, while the old man is shown only from the outside, which keeps him more distant.","sol":"There is strong support for this view. The narrative stays close to Maya's own perceptions and feelings: \"She wanted to believe him\" states her inner uncertainty outright, and the discomfort the writer gives her is physical as well as mental, since \"The rain had found its way inside her collar\" while the light on the water \"had turned the colour of a bruise\". The closing image is filtered through her as well — the shape out past the harbour wall \"might only have been the storm arranging itself into a shape she wanted to see\" — where the writer names her wanting as the thing that may have made the shape, admitting the reader into the exact place where her hope and her doubt meet. The old man, by contrast, is presented entirely through what he does and says, never through what he feels: he \"didn't turn his head\" when she sits beside him, and he speaks \"to the sea rather than to her\", so the one gesture the writer gives him is a refusal of contact. His flat repetition, \"They always say it might not come… It always comes\", leaves the reader to infer his state of mind from a form of words, which keeps him a figure to be read rather than a mind to share. Against the statement, that same repetition can be heard as a man steadying himself as much as her, which earns him a little of the sympathy Maya is given more freely. On balance I agree that the writer favours Maya, since the reader is repeatedly let inside her uncertainty while the old man's inner life stays a matter of inference.","m":20,"d":3,"codes":["3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text","3.1.1 recognising the possibility of different responses to a text"],"hints":["Think about whose thoughts and feelings the reader is actually shown, not just who does more in the passage.","Compare how directly Maya's inner feelings are stated with how the old man's are only ever implied by his actions and words.","Start with the phrase 'She wanted to believe him' and ask what the passage never quite tells you about what he feels."]}],"exit":[{"q":"What must every point in a point-marked AO1 answer be, according to the mark scheme?","a":"True, and drawn only from the named section of the text."},{"q":"What is the key difference between an inference and a paraphrase?","a":"An inference draws out a meaning the text implies; a paraphrase just restates what it already says."},{"q":"Can two readers reach different conclusions about the same text and both be given credit?","a":"Yes, provided each conclusion is argued from evidence in the text."},{"q":"What does it mean for a piece of writing to show bias, even if every individual fact in it is true?","a":"The selection or phrasing of those facts favours one view over another."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Inference","back":"A meaning a text implies without stating it outright, reached by noticing what a word choice, omission or detail suggests — e.g. 'wanted to believe him' implying doubt, not certainty.","code":"3.1.1 drawing inferences","open":false},{"front":"Bias","back":"A slant in what a text includes, omits or how it phrases things, favouring one view — possible even where every stated fact is true. Example: Edmund adding his employer's praise \"only because I know it will please my mother\".","code":"3.1.1 identifying bias and misuse of evidence","open":false},{"front":"Viewpoint","back":"The position or attitude a writer holds towards a subject, shown through choice of detail, tone and emphasis. Example: Passage C's sceptical viewpoint appears in \"Nobody warned me that it might just be proof of bad management.\"","code":"3.1.1 identifying bias and misuse of evidence","open":false},{"front":"Judicious (mark-scheme term)","back":"Chosen with good judgement — precisely selected because it best supports the point being made, not simply picked because it is available. Example: quoting only \"unwilling to look away\" rather than the whole sentence around it.","code":"3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text","open":false},{"front":"Textual reference","back":"A specific detail, quoted or precisely identified, drawn directly from the text itself, not a general impression of it. Example: quoting \"to the sea rather than to her\" to support a point about the old man.","code":"3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information","open":false},{"front":"Point-marking","back":"A marking method giving one mark per true, relevant point drawn only from the named section; copying that whole section out earns nothing.","code":"3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information","open":false},{"front":"Level of response marking","back":"A ladder method: start at the lowest level and climb until the answer's qualities match a level's descriptor, then fix the mark within it.","code":"3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text","open":false},{"front":"The 'list four things' task","back":"Point-marked, 4 marks, AO1: four true points drawn only from the named section of the text, quoted or paraphrased.","code":"3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information","open":false},{"front":"The 'choose four TRUE statements' task","back":"Point-marked, 4 marks, AO1: shade exactly four of eight lettered statements that are true of the named section.","code":"3.1.1 identifying and interpreting themes, ideas and information","open":false},{"front":"'To what extent do you agree?'","back":"The extended AO4 evaluation task: take a position on a given statement and support it with judiciously chosen textual detail.","code":"3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text","open":false},{"front":"Level 4 evaluation, key words","back":"Perceptive; detailed; judicious; convincing; critical — the words a Level 4 AO4 answer's descriptor uses.","code":"3.1.1 reflecting critically and evaluatively on text","open":false},{"front":"A way to test a claim for bias","back":"checking whether evidence is given for it; checking whether the writer ignores a view that contradicts it; checking whether the writer stands to benefit from the claim being believed","code":"3.1.1 identifying bias and misuse of evidence","open":true},{"front":"An example of an inference, not a fact","back":"that a character's calm hides real doubt; that a writer disapproves of something despite never saying so directly; that an event has happened off the page and is only implied","code":"3.1.1 drawing inferences","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The passages written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Passage A — 'The Last Sailing' (fiction, 21st-century style, an opening): The pier lights came on twenty minutes before the storm did, which Maya took as a kind of warning. She had stood at the ticket booth since four, watching the tide drag itself back from the concrete in long, complaining ropes of foam. A gull sat on the railing without moving, as if it, too, were waiting for permission to leave. The last ferry of the season was due at six, and the harbourmaster had already told her twice that it might not come at all. Rain had been forecast since noon and had arrived exactly on time, fine at first, then heavier, until the whole bay seemed to blur at the edges like a photograph left in water. Somewhere behind her a rope slapped against a mast, over and over, patient as a clock.\n\nShe wasn't alone on the jetty. An old man in a yellow coat sat on the single bench, a canvas holdall between his boots, and he watched the horizon the way other people watched a kettle: certain that something was about to happen, unwilling to look away in case it happened without him. When Maya finally sat beside him he didn't turn his head. \"They always say it might not come,\" he said, to the sea rather than to her. \"It always comes.\" She wanted to believe him. The rain had found its way inside her collar, and the light on the water had turned the colour of a bruise, and somewhere out past the harbour wall a shape that might have been the ferry, or might only have been the storm arranging itself into a shape she wanted to see, was beginning, very slowly, to grow.","Passage B — 'A Letter to My Father' (non-fiction, 19th-century voice, a letter): My dear Father,\n\nI write in haste, for Mr Pemberton allows us but the quarter hour before the counting-house closes its doors, and I would not have you think me idle. You ask whether I am content, and I answer that I am, though I own the days are long and the ledger unforgiving of any hand that trembles. We are at our desks by seven, and there we remain, with scarce a word exchanged between one man and the next, until the lamps are lit and lit again. Mr Pemberton says that a clerk who complains of tired eyes has not yet learned to love his work, and I confess I did not care for the saying when first I heard it, though I begin now to see some wisdom in it. It is a hard school, Father, but I would rather be hardened here than softened at home, and I beg you not to write again of bringing me back before Michaelmas. Mr Pemberton has this very week praised my column of figures before the whole office, which I set down here only because I know it will please my mother. I remain, Father, your dutiful and obedient son, Edmund.","Passage C — 'Nobody Warned Me About the Six A.M. Emails' (non-fiction, 21st-century style, a column): I used to think a long day at the office was proof of something: commitment, maybe, or ambition, or the kind of seriousness that gets you noticed. Nobody warned me that it might just be proof of bad management. My first boss called the people who left at half past five \"the clock-watchers\", always with the same small curl of the lip, and for two years I stayed until seven or eight simply to avoid being one. I answered emails from bed. I answered them on trains, in queues, once during my own leaving party for a different job. I told myself that this was simply what love of the work looked like.\n\nIt took a bout of the flu, and four days offline while a colleague quietly cleared my inbox without incident, to show me what the extra hours had actually been buying: nothing that the company missed, and a great deal that I did. I am not arguing for idleness. I am arguing that a desk is not a confessional, and the hours you put in are not a measure of your worth, however much a certain kind of employer would like you to believe otherwise. My old boss still works past nine most nights. I no longer envy him."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 4, on Passage A: 'The writer makes the reader feel that the waiting on the jetty is full of quiet hope, not despair.')","items":["Point: the passage builds a mood of hope straining against doubt, not despair, and the old man is central to that balance.","Evidence: \"he watched the horizon the way other people watched a kettle: certain that something was about to happen, unwilling to look away in case it happened without him\".","Analysis: the metaphor domesticates his vigil — a kettle-watch is a small, patient, almost comic wait, not a dread-filled one — while 'unwilling to look away in case it happened without him' makes his hope active rather than resigned, so the reader shares his expectancy rather than his fear.","Judgement: this is judicious evidence for the statement that the waiting is full of quiet hope rather than despair, but the writer never lets that hope harden — the simile \"the whole bay seemed to blur at the edges like a photograph left in water\" has already dissolved the scene's outlines before the old man is introduced, and the final image, \"a shape that might have been the ferry, or might only have been the storm arranging itself into a shape she wanted to see\", leaves Maya's hope visibly fragile at the very end, so a convincing answer should agree that hope dominates while showing the writer keeps it from settling into certainty.","Terminology: metaphor, simile, connotation, ambiguity."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question and statement, visibly weaker)","items":["Point: the old man is calm while he waits for the ferry.","Evidence: \"he watched the horizon the way other people watched a kettle\".","Analysis: this shows he is patient and not worried, which makes the reader feel it is a hopeful moment, not a despairing one.","Judgement: I agree with the statement because the writer shows hope rather than despair.","Level check: this meets Level 2's 'makes some evaluative comment(s) on effect(s) on the reader' and 'selects some appropriate textual reference(s)', but it misses Level 4's 'judicious range of textual detail' (only one quotation, not weighed against any other) and 'perceptive understanding of writer's methods' (the metaphor is quoted but not analysed — 'kettle' is never explained as domesticating or softening the wait), so the response stays descriptive rather than evaluative."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"The questions this room is examined through","items":["Paper 1 Question 1 — 'List four things from this part of the text about…' — 4 marks, AO1, not levelled: 'Give 1 mark for each point… responses must be true, and drawn only from' the named lines.","Paper 2 Question 1 — 'Choose four statements below which are TRUE' — 4 marks, AO1, not levelled: one mark for each of the four TRUE statements shaded, from eight lettered options.","Paper 1 Question 4 — 'To what extent do you agree?' — 20 marks, AO4, levels-marked, the paper's extended reading question, which allows students to 'provide extended responses' and 'draw together different areas of knowledge, skills and/or understanding from across a full course of study'.","Both papers advise about 45 minutes for the whole of Section A (Reading), shared across that section's questions; Paper 1 Question 4 takes by far the largest share of that time.","AO4 Level 4 (16–20 marks), 'Perceptive, detailed evaluation': evaluates critically and in detail the effect(s) on the reader; shows perceptive understanding of writer's methods; selects a judicious range of textual detail; develops a convincing and critical response to the focus of the statement.","AO4 Level 3 (11–15 marks), 'Clear, relevant evaluation': evaluates clearly the effect(s) on the reader; shows clear understanding of writer's methods; selects a range of relevant textual references; makes a clear and relevant response to the focus of the statement.","AO4 Level 2 (6–10 marks), 'Some evaluation': makes some evaluative comment(s) on effect(s) on the reader; shows some understanding of writer's methods; selects some appropriate textual reference(s); makes some response to the focus of the statement.","AO4 Level 1 (1–5 marks), 'Simple, limited evaluation': makes simple, limited evaluative comment(s) on effect(s) on reader; shows limited understanding of writer's methods; selects simple, limited textual reference(s); makes a simple, limited response to the focus of the statement.","Terminology this room rewards, with a one-line definition each: inference — a meaning the text implies without stating it outright; viewpoint — the position or attitude a writer holds towards their subject; bias — a slant in what is included or how it is phrased that favours one view; judicious — chosen with good judgement, precisely fitted to the point being made; textual reference — a specific detail, quoted or precisely identified, drawn from the text itself.","Paper 1's Source is one literature fiction text, drawn from either the 20th or 21st century, genre prose fiction — \"extracts from novels and short stories\" — matched in this room by Passage A.","Across the specification as a whole, the set texts draw on \"a wide range of texts from the 19th, 20th and 21st centuries, including literature and literary non-fiction as well as other writing such as reviews and journalism\" — matched in this room by all three passages together.","The non-fiction range studied is \"literature and extended literary non-fiction, and other writing such as essays, reviews and journalism (both printed and online)\" — matched in this room by Passage B (a 19th-century letter) and Passage C (a 21st-century column)."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 1 Question 4, 'To what extent do you agree?', on Passage A","items":["The task, in the paper's own words: 'Focus this part of your answer on the second part of Passage A, from \"She wasn't alone on the jetty\" to the end. A student, having read this section of the text, said: \"The writer makes the reader feel that the waiting on the jetty is full of quiet hope, not despair.\" To what extent do you agree?' — Paper 1 Question 4, 20 marks, AO4.","Move 1 — decode the statement: identify exactly what impression it claims (hope, not despair) since the whole answer must keep testing that specific claim, not just describe the passage.","Move 2 — plan three to four paragraphs, each built the same way: an impression → the writer's method that creates it → a judiciously chosen quotation → what that quotation does to the reader → whether it supports or complicates the statement.","Move 3 — vary the ground each paragraph covers so the answer ranges across the passage rather than repeating one point: the old man's stillness, his spoken certainty, Maya's own hope, and the passage's closing image.","Move 4 — keep returning explicitly to the statement's own wording ('hope, not despair'), so every paragraph visibly answers 'to what extent', not just 'what effect'.","Timing: about 22 minutes of the 45 advised for the whole of Section A, since this is by far its largest share.","The last paragraph must give a qualified overall judgement — agree, disagree or partly agree — that weighs the paragraphs against each other rather than simply repeating the first one, and must use the statement's own words one final time."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T10:35:30Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All ten faults from the third verdict are genuinely fixed and the rebuilt material is accurate — the seven list stems now read 'List four things from this part of the text about…' as room.facts[0] prints it, questions 11 and 12 carry the paper's Question 4 frame, the four 20-mark tasks sit on four disjoint stretches of Passage A and each new solution argues only from its own section, question 13 no longer calls a non-comparison a metaphor, worked example 0 prints the whole paragraph, cards[1] illustrates bias with 'only because I know it will please my mother', and lesson.idea[1] and [4] now end on what a top-level answer does — and on a fresh reading all 96 passage quotations (including the two long verbatim blocks in examples 0 and 2), roughly 28 mark-scheme and specification quotations and 22 countable claims check out, with every TRUE/FALSE key holding exactly four true statements; what is left is bank design, chiefly that worked example 0 now hands over question 6's answer and that question 9 has landed inside question 5."}}},"summary-synthesis":{"id":"AQA-8700","topic":"summary-synthesis","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because Paper 2 devotes two of its four reading questions to AO1 alone, and AO1 carries 10% of the whole GCSE, 7.5% of it on this paper: Question 1 (4 marks) rewards finding true statements in one source, and Question 2 (8 marks) asks students to do something no other question on either paper asks, reading two unseen sources from different centuries and writing statements that hold both texts at once, moving from identification to genuine synthesis. Without practice at spotting a shared theme, summarising a single text without retelling it, and inferring a difference across two texts, students default to paraphrase, which the mark scheme caps at Level 1 however fluently it is written.","idea":[{"h":"Finding the theme that links two texts","code":"3.1.1 identifying the main theme or themes","t":"Every summary question on Paper 2 assumes you can see what a text is actually about before you can hold it against another. A theme is the idea or subject that runs through a passage: a young person's first weeks of work away from home links Passage A and Passage B in this room, even though one is a Victorian letter and the other a modern autobiographical article. Spotting it first stops you drifting into retelling isolated details. A Level 4 answer never announces the theme as a heading, though; it uses it as a spine, so that every statement it writes is a difference inside that one shared subject, and no sentence is a fact about one text that merely happens to sit beside a fact about the other."},{"h":"Summarising one text without retelling it","code":"3.1.1 summarising ideas and information from a single text","t":"Summarising a single text means selecting its most important ideas and compressing them into a judgement, not copying it out or paraphrasing it sentence by sentence. The Paper 2 mark scheme is blunt about the difference: the Level 1 descriptor reads 'Offers paraphrase rather than inference', while Level 4 answers make 'perceptive inferences from both texts'. Passage A is printed in six paragraphs and Passage B in five, so practise reducing one paragraph at a time to a single inferred idea: Thomas's second paragraph is not 'he works long hours' but 'he accepts hard usage as the price of learning a trade'. A Level 4 answer shows that compression in every sentence, quoting three or four words and spending the rest of the sentence on what they let you conclude."},{"h":"Synthesising two texts into one answer","code":"3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","t":"Synthesis means treating two texts as one problem rather than two separate summaries placed side by side. Paper 2 Question 2 instructs, 'You need to refer to Source A and Source B for this question. Use details from both Sources', and the mark scheme rewards statements that 'show perceptive differences between texts'. A single sentence built from a detail in each source, linked with a word such as 'whereas' or 'in contrast', earns far more than two paragraphs with no cross-reference. A Level 4 answer goes one step further than the link word: having put the two details together, it says what the pairing shows that neither detail shows on its own, so the inference belongs to both texts rather than to either."},{"h":"Why AO1 rewards synthesis, not just identification","code":"4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)","t":"AO1 has two bullet points: identifying and interpreting explicit and implicit information and ideas, and selecting and synthesising evidence from different texts. On Paper 2, Question 1 assesses only the first bullet, finding and shading true statements, while Question 2 'assesses both bullet points', which is why it is worth twice as much and is marked by levels rather than one point per detail. That matters for how you write: a Level 4 answer satisfies both bullets inside the same sentence, interpreting a detail from one source and holding it against a detail from the other, so that not one sentence of the answer belongs to a single text alone."},{"h":"How the summary-of-differences question is marked","code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)","t":"Paper 2 Question 2 is worth 8 marks and climbs a four-level ladder: Level 1 (1-2 marks) 'offers paraphrase rather than inference'; Level 2 (3-4) 'attempts some inference(s) from one/both texts'; Level 3 (5-6) 'makes clear inferences from both texts' with 'clear differences between texts'; Level 4 (7-8) 'makes perceptive inferences from both texts' with 'judicious references/use of textual detail' and statements that 'show perceptive differences between texts'. Below all four sits Level 0, where a student 'will not have offered any differences'. A Level 4 answer is therefore recognisable by its shape: three or four sentences, each naming a difference, each carrying a short quotation from each source, and each ending on the inference the pair supports rather than on the quotation."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning two details into one difference statement","setup":"Paper 2 Question 2: 'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between Thomas and the narrator.' [8 marks] Passage A: 'Mr. Pemberton is a stern master, and exacting in his standards, but I believe him to be just; he corrected my first proof-sheet without a word of unkindness, though he found seventeen errors in it.' Passage B: 'My manager, Priya, is the kind of boss who will absolutely tell you when your table service is sloppy, but she also brought in a cake on my first Friday because, she said, everyone deserves cake on a Friday.' Produce one synthesising sentence about their employers, and a second sentence that infers what the pair shows.","steps":["Thomas gives an explicit verdict on his employer, 'I believe him to be just', and bases it on the manner of the correcting, 'without a word of unkindness'.","The narrator gives no verdict word at all: her approval rests on an act that the job did not require, 'she also brought in a cake on my first Friday', set against Priya naming her service 'sloppy'.","So Thomas's good opinion is earned inside the work, by fairness in correction; the narrator's is earned outside it, by a kindness nobody owed her.","The synthesising sentence: whereas Thomas trusts a master who is 'exacting in his standards' because the correction came 'without a word of unkindness', the narrator warms to a manager who tells her when her service is 'sloppy' but also brings in cake because 'everyone deserves cake on a Friday'.","The inference sentence: Thomas asks of an employer only that strictness be just, while the narrator expects hers to treat her as a person as well as a worker."],"cues":["Which phrase in Passage A states Thomas's actual verdict on Mr. Pemberton?","Passage B gives no such verdict word, so what does the narrator offer in its place?","Does each writer's good opinion rest on something inside the work or outside it?","What one sentence, using a linking word, puts those two judgements directly against each other?","What further sentence would turn that contrast into an inference about what each writer wants from a boss?"]},{"title":"From paraphrase to inference","setup":"You are preparing a summary of the differences between Thomas and the narrator. Passage B reads: 'I ring my mum most nights, usually while I'm eating cold toast standing up at the counter, and she asks the same three questions every time: am I eating, am I sleeping, and have I made any friends. I tell her yes, mostly, and she pretends to believe me.' Task: turn this into an inference about the narrator's relationship with her mother, rather than a retelling of it.","steps":["The answer 'yes, mostly' is a hedge: it concedes that some of the three questions cannot honestly be answered yes.","The clause 'she pretends to believe me' puts the mother in on it, so the hedge is not a successful lie but an agreed one.","The inference: both women keep up a comforting fiction, the daughter to spare her mother worry and the mother to spare her daughter having to explain.","Written as a statement for the summary: the narrator manages what her mother is allowed to worry about, which is a very different thing from saying that she rings her mum every night."],"cues":["What does the word 'mostly' quietly admit about the three questions?","What does 'pretends to believe me' tell you about the mother's side of the exchange?","What single idea describes the arrangement the two of them are keeping up?","How would you phrase that as a statement of inference rather than a retelling?"]},{"title":"Choosing four TRUE statements (Paper 2 Question 1)","setup":"Read again the first part of Passage A, from the opening to 'though he found seventeen errors in it.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. [4 marks] A Thomas has been at Mr. Pemberton's printing-house for six weeks. B Thomas and the other workers rise at five. C Thomas finds the work easier than he had supposed. D Thomas stops work before six in the evening. E Thomas describes Mr. Pemberton as a stern master. F Mr. Pemberton spoke unkindly when he corrected Thomas's first proof-sheet. G Mr. Pemberton found seventeen errors in Thomas's first proof-sheet. H Thomas says his father also works at the printing-house.","steps":["A is supported directly by the letter, which reads 'I have now been six weeks in Mr. Pemberton's printing-house', so A is true.","B and D are both about the working day: 'We rise at five' makes B true, while 'I do not lay down my composing-stick until past eight in the evening' contradicts D, so D is false.","C reverses the letter, which says 'the work is harder than I had supposed', so C is false.","E and F are both about the master: 'Mr. Pemberton is a stern master' makes E true, and 'he corrected my first proof-sheet without a word of unkindness' makes F false.","G matches 'though he found seventeen errors in it' and is true, while H is never stated anywhere in the named part, so it cannot be shaded: the four to shade are A, B, E and G."],"cues":["Which phrase in the named part gives Thomas's length of service?","What two times does the letter give for the start and the end of the working day?","Does Thomas call the work easier or harder than he expected?","Which words does the letter use for Mr. Pemberton's manner, and for the way he corrected the proof-sheet?","Only G and H are left: can you point to wording in the named part for each of them?"]},{"title":"Building a full synthesis paragraph","setup":"Paper 2 Question 2: 'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between Thomas and the narrator.' [8 marks] You have already noted two details. Passage A: 'I would be glad, Father, if you could send my old winter coat by the carrier, for the nights grow cold, and I have no fire in my room.' Passage B: 'My feet ache, I smell of bacon fat by ten in the morning, and I have never been so tired in my life.' Build the pair into a Level 4 paragraph about independence.","steps":["Each detail is a hardship, but the two are handled differently: Thomas sends his hardship home as a request, while the narrator reports hers to nobody but the reader.","The judicious detail on each side is therefore the smallest one: the coat asked for 'by the carrier', and the smell of 'bacon fat' she simply carries.","The synthesising sentence: whereas Thomas's discomfort becomes a favour his father is expected to grant, the narrator's is hers to absorb and even to enjoy telling.","The developing sentence, which is what lifts the paragraph to Level 4: Thomas's cold room keeps him a dependent son inside a working life, while the narrator's aching feet are the price of a life she has chosen and pays for herself."],"cues":["Both details describe physical discomfort, so what is actually different about them?","Which few words on each side prove that difference most economically?","What one sentence, using a linking word, states the difference across both texts?","What second sentence would take the same contrast from a statement to an inference?"]},{"title":"Spotting the theme that links two unseen texts","setup":"Passage A (Thomas's letter) and Passage B (the narrator's article) never mention each other, and they were written roughly a century and a half apart, but they are printed together for a summary question. Task: work out, in one sentence, the theme that links them, and say how that theme should organise an answer.","steps":["Reduce each text to its own subject first: Passage A is a young apprentice's account of his first weeks in a printing-house; Passage B is a young waitress's account of her first weeks in a diner.","Find the kind of detail that appears in a different form in both: a coat asked for 'by the carrier' in one, and 'I ring my mum most nights' in the other, both of them contact with home kept up from a distance.","The shared theme, in one sentence: both texts are about a young person's first weeks of paid work away from home, and how each stays tied to the family they have left.","Use the theme as the spine of the answer: take work, home and the future in turn, and put both writers into every one of those sentences rather than working through Passage A and then Passage B."],"cues":["What is each passage about, considered on its own?","What kind of detail turns up in a different form in both texts?","How would you state the shared theme in a single sentence?","How should knowing the theme change the order in which you write the answer?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between paraphrasing and making an inference when writing a summary?","a":"Paraphrasing restates a text's words in different words; an inference draws a conclusion from what the text implies, which is what the top level rewards."},{"q":"Why does Paper 2 Question 2 ask you to use 'details from both Sources'?","a":"Because it tests synthesis: bringing information from two texts together into linked statements of difference, not summarising each text on its own."},{"q":"What single word or phrase can open a sentence that states a difference between two texts?","a":"'Whereas', 'in contrast', 'unlike' or 'on the other hand'."},{"q":"What makes a synthesis statement 'perceptive' rather than merely 'clear'?","a":"It draws an inference from both texts and states a precise, judiciously evidenced difference between them, not just some interpretation from one text with a detail added from the other."},{"q":"What separates a Level 1 summary from a Level 0 one?","a":"A Level 1 answer still states a simple difference between the texts, even though it paraphrases; a Level 0 answer offers no difference at all."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 2 Question 1: 'Read again the first part of Source A... Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements.' 4 marks, AO1, point-marked: one mark for each of the four TRUE statements shaded, not levelled.","Paper 2 Question 2: 'You need to refer to Source A and Source B for this question. Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between...' 8 marks, AO1, marked by four levels.","AO1 ('identify and interpret explicit and implicit information and ideas; select and synthesise evidence from different texts') carries 10% of the GCSE: 7.5% on Paper 2, 2.5% on Paper 1.","The top level of the summary question needs 'perceptive inferences from both texts' with statements that 'show perceptive differences between texts'; the bottom level 'offers paraphrase rather than inference'; Level 0 is for a student who 'will not have offered any differences'.","The specimen Paper 2 advises about 15 minutes reading the Source and all five questions, about 45 minutes on Section A and about 45 minutes on Section B; it gives no time for any single question.","A synthesis statement links a detail from each source in one sentence with a word such as 'whereas', 'in contrast' or 'unlike', and then says what the pair shows, rather than summarising each source in turn."],"questions":[{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage A ('A Letter to My Father'), from the opening to 'though he found seventeen errors in it.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A Thomas hopes the harvest has been gathered in without mishap. B Thomas believes he is making steady progress. C Thomas has found the work easier than he had supposed. D Thomas's back aches from the standing. E Thomas begins his working day at eight in the morning. F Thomas says Mr. Pemberton is exacting in his standards. G Mr. Pemberton spoke unkindly when he corrected Thomas's first proof-sheet. H Thomas has been at the printing-house for six months. [4 marks]","a":"A, B, D, F","sol":"A is true: Thomas trusts that 'the harvest has been gathered in without mishap'. B is true: 'I flatter myself that I am making steady progress'. D is true: 'my back aching from the standing'. F is true: 'Mr. Pemberton is a stern master, and exacting in his standards'. C is false — the letter says 'the work is harder than I had supposed'. E is false — 'We rise at five'. G is false — 'he corrected my first proof-sheet without a word of unkindness'. H is false — 'I have now been six weeks in Mr. Pemberton's printing-house'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Everything that can make a statement true is inside the named part of Passage A; nothing you remember from Passage B counts here.","Test each statement against a phrase you can point to, and look out for the two ways a statement goes wrong: it reverses what the letter says, or it changes one of the letter's own words.","Take the statements in order and settle each one on its own evidence before moving on, rather than hunting for four that feel right."],"codes":["3.1.1 summarising ideas and information from a single text","4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)"]},{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage B ('Learning the Ropes'), from the opening to 'and laughed about most of them.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A The narrator moved into a shared flat above a bike shop three weeks ago. B The narrator's alarm goes off before it is light. C The narrator likes the job less than she expected to. D Priya tells the narrator when her table service is sloppy. E Priya brought in a cake on the narrator's first Friday. F Priya counted nineteen mistakes on the narrator's first shift. G The narrator had worked in a diner before this job. H Priya laughed at none of the narrator's mistakes. [4 marks]","a":"A, B, D, E","sol":"A is true: 'Three weeks ago I moved into a shared flat above a bike shop'. B is true: 'My alarm goes off in the dark'. D is true: Priya 'will absolutely tell you when your table service is sloppy'. E is true: 'she also brought in a cake on my first Friday'. C is false — 'I love it more than I expected to'. F is false — 'She counted nine mistakes on my first shift'. G is false — this is 'my first proper job'. H is false — she 'laughed about most of them'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Work only inside the named part of Passage B; Thomas's letter cannot make any of these statements true.","A false statement usually reverses what the passage says or alters a single word of it, so judge each one against a phrase you can put your finger on rather than against your general sense of the text.","Shade only when the phrase is in front of you: a statement you cannot match to any wording in the named part is not one of the four."],"codes":["3.1.1 summarising ideas and information from a single text","4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)"]},{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage A ('A Letter to My Father'), from the opening to 'I have written to my sister but have had no answer.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A Thomas trusts that the letter finds his father and his mother in good health. B Thomas has been at Mr. Pemberton's printing-house for six weeks. C Thomas says his fingers are black with ink. D Thomas believes Mr. Pemberton to be unjust. E Mr. Pemberton found seven errors in Thomas's first proof-sheet. F Thomas says he is often lonely in the loft above the workshop. G Thomas's sister has answered his letter. H Thomas thinks of his mother's kitchen less than he ought to own. [4 marks]","a":"A, B, C, F","sol":"A is true: 'I trust this letter finds you and my mother in good health'. B is true: 'I have now been six weeks in Mr. Pemberton's printing-house'. C is true: 'my fingers black with ink'. F is true: 'I confess I am often lonely in the loft above the workshop'. D is false — the letter says 'I believe him to be just'. E is false — 'he found seventeen errors in it', not seven. G is false — 'I have written to my sister but have had no answer'. H is false — he thinks of his mother's kitchen 'more than I ought to own'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["This named part runs further than the one in the first question, as far as the sentence about Thomas's sister, so the loft and the sister are inside it.","A statement can go wrong in two ways here: it reverses what the letter says, or it swaps one of the letter's own words for another, so read each one against the wording rather than against the gist.","Settle each statement on a phrase you can point to in the letter before you move to the next one, rather than shading the four that feel most likely."],"codes":["3.1.1 summarising ideas and information from a single text","4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between Thomas's working day and the narrator's working day. [8 marks]","a":"Thomas's day is set for him, from five in the morning until past eight at night in the printing-house; the narrator's begins in the dark at a diner that opens at six and is described through tiredness and smell rather than through hours.","sol":"Thomas measures his working day by the clock and by an authority outside himself: 'We rise at five, and I do not lay down my composing-stick until past eight in the evening', where the plural 'We' shows that even the hour of getting up belongs to the house rather than to him. The narrator measures hers by her own body: her diner 'opens at six', her 'alarm goes off in the dark and I stumble out still half asleep', and before the morning is even over 'My feet ache, I smell of bacon fat by ten in the morning'. Whereas Thomas records the hours themselves, as facts a father is entitled to be told, the narrator records what the hours do to her, as sensations offered to a reader. The difference this implies is one of ownership: Thomas's day is imposed and endured, while the narrator's, though it starts in the dark, is a day she has taken on and writes about as hers.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Find where each writer tells you when the working day starts and when it ends; one of them gives you a clock and the other gives you something else.","Put one detail from each passage into the same sentence instead of describing Thomas's day and then the narrator's.","Finish on what the pair of details implies about who decides the shape of each writer's day."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between how Thomas's mistakes and the narrator's mistakes were treated by their employers. [8 marks]","a":"Thomas's seventeen errors were corrected without a word of unkindness, as trade discipline; the narrator's nine mistakes were counted and laughed about, as an expected part of learning the job.","sol":"Both writers were counted wrong on their first attempt, but the counting means different things. Mr. Pemberton 'found seventeen errors in it' and 'corrected my first proof-sheet without a word of unkindness': the errors are many and the correction comes without reproach, so a mistake is a matter of trade discipline, to be put right rather than resented. Priya 'counted nine mistakes on my first shift and laughed about most of them': the same act of counting ends in laughter, so a mistake is a companionable part of learning the work. Whereas Thomas is grateful that his errors brought no unkindness, so that the best he can hope for from a master is that the correction is just, the narrator is not required to be grateful at all, because her mistakes were never held against her. What each writer counts as fair treatment has moved between the two texts: from the absence of cruelty to the presence of good humour.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Each passage gives both a number and a reaction; the reaction is the part that carries the difference.","Pair the two employers in one sentence, then ask what each reaction tells you about how that workplace treats a beginner.","End on what each writer seems to think a fair employer owes them, rather than on the counting itself."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between how Thomas and the narrator each describe their employer. [8 marks]","a":"Thomas presents Mr. Pemberton as a stern but just master who corrects him formally; the narrator presents Priya as a critical boss who also shows personal kindness.","sol":"Thomas and the narrator both work for demanding employers, but describe the demand differently. Thomas calls Mr. Pemberton 'a stern master, and exacting in his standards', judging him ultimately fair because 'he corrected my first proof-sheet without a word of unkindness', even though 'he found seventeen errors in it' — Thomas's employer is strict but formally, impersonally just. The narrator's Priya is also exacting — she 'will absolutely tell you when your table service is sloppy' and 'counted nine mistakes' on the narrator's first shift — but Priya's strictness is paired with an informal, personal gesture: she 'brought in a cake on my first Friday' because 'everyone deserves cake on a Friday'. Whereas Thomas experiences correction as a formal, professional relationship, the narrator experiences the same strictness inside a relationship that also has warmth and humour, suggesting a shift from Victorian professional distance to a more personal, modern workplace.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Focus only on what each writer says about the person they work for, not on the rest of the passage.","Find one moment of criticism and one moment of approval or kindness in each passage, then compare the two pairs rather than the two people.","Open your key sentence with a linking word so the two employers are held against each other in a single statement."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between how Thomas and the narrator each communicate with their family. [8 marks]","a":"Thomas writes home: a formal letter to his father asking for practical help, and an unanswered one to his sister; the narrator rings her mother most nights and manages what she tells her.","sol":"Thomas's contact with home is formal and one-directional: he writes to his father in the manner of a dutiful son, closing 'I remain, Father, your dutiful and affectionate son', and uses the letter to make a specific, practical request, 'if you could send my old winter coat by the carrier'. He has written elsewhere in the family too, but without success: 'I have written to my sister but have had no answer', so the only contact that works for him is the one that carries a duty with it. The narrator's contact is informal and frequent: she says 'I ring my mum most nights', but her honesty is managed rather than complete, since she tells her mother 'yes, mostly, and she pretends to believe me'. Thomas's letter to his father is entirely transparent about what he needs, even where that costs him some pride, though the letter he sent his sister has gone unanswered; the narrator's nightly calls are answered every time but only partly honest. The difference is not how much contact each keeps up but what contact is for: for Thomas it is the means of asking, and for the narrator it is the means of reassuring.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Look at how often each writer is in touch with home, and by what means.","Notice that one writer asks for something outright while the other holds something back; find the wording that proves each.","Build the answer around a single contrast: what the contact with home is actually for in each text."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between Thomas's and the narrator's feelings about their new working life. [8 marks]","a":"Thomas endures his new life with dutiful resignation and hope of future advancement; the narrator embraces hers despite the exhaustion, saying she loves it.","sol":"Thomas's attitude is one of endurance: his account of the physical demands of the work, 'my fingers black with ink and my back aching from the standing', is followed not by complaint but by a hope of future reward, 'to be admitted a journeyman by Michaelmas'. The narrator states her feeling directly and positively despite comparable exhaustion — 'My feet ache', 'I have never been so tired in my life', and yet 'there is something about earning your own money, however little, that makes you stand up a bit straighter' — and says outright that she loves the job 'more than I expected to'. Both writers endure real physical hardship for the sake of independence, but Thomas's satisfaction waits on a future someone else will grant him, while the narrator's is already here, in the way the work has changed how she carries herself.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Find the physical hardship each writer describes before you look at how each of them feels about it.","For each writer, ask what their feeling about the work is attached to, and settle that from their own words before you compare the two.","Use a quotation about hardship and a quotation about feeling from each passage, linked inside one sentence."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between where Thomas lives and where the narrator lives. [8 marks]","a":"Thomas lodges alone in a cold, fireless loft above his master's workshop and must have comforts sent to him; the narrator has chosen a shared flat above a bike shop, no more elegant but separate from her work.","sol":"Thomas's lodging belongs to his employer and is described almost entirely through what it lacks: he is 'often lonely in the loft above the workshop', 'the nights grow cold, and I have no fire in my room', and any comfort has to be posted to him, 'my old winter coat' sent 'by the carrier'. The narrator's home is a choice she reports as a step taken: 'Three weeks ago I moved into a shared flat above a bike shop', and although her domestic life is no more elegant than his — 'eating cold toast standing up at the counter' — she asks nobody to improve it. Both writers live above a business, but Thomas lives above the one that employs him, whereas the narrator lives above somebody else's shop and goes out to work. What this implies is that Thomas's home and his employment are a single fact he cannot separate, while the narrator keeps a life the diner does not reach into.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Each passage names the building each writer sleeps in; start by putting the two buildings side by side.","Ask what each writer's room is missing, and who is expected to supply it.","End on what the two lodgings imply about how far each writer's job reaches into the rest of their life."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between what Thomas hopes for and what the narrator hopes for. [8 marks]","a":"Thomas hopes to be admitted a journeyman by Michaelmas if he stays diligent, and owes the first proceeds to his father; the narrator expects Priya to make her a supervisor by the summer and thinks she might.","sol":"Thomas's hope is conditional, deferred and in someone else's gift: 'I hope, if I continue diligent, to be admitted a journeyman by Michaelmas', where the clause 'if I continue diligent' makes advancement a reward for conduct, the passive 'to be admitted' leaves the granting to the trade, and Michaelmas fixes it to a date in the calendar rather than to his own readiness. Even the good it will do is owed elsewhere, since he means 'to send you something towards my keep before the year is out'. The narrator's hope is nearer, plainer and half a prediction: 'I'm hoping Priya will make me a supervisor by the summer, and honestly, I think she might', where the second clause quietly converts the hope into an expectation drawn from her own reading of her manager. Whereas Thomas must earn a status that will then be conferred on him, and owes its first proceeds to his father, the narrator expects promotion from one person she believes she already understands, and owes it to nobody.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Both passages end by looking forward; begin with the sentence each writer uses to do it.","Ask, for each hope, who has the power to grant it and what has to happen first.","Close on the difference in what each writer's future depends on, not on the two jobs they hope to get."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between Thomas and the narrator. [8 marks]","a":"A full Level 4 summary links contrasts in duty, family contact and the value each writer puts on the work into inferred differences drawn from both texts, rather than two summaries placed side by side.","sol":"Thomas and the narrator both begin new working lives away from home, but where Thomas submits to duty, the narrator claims independence. Thomas's letter is built from obligation: he writes 'I remain, Father, your dutiful and affectionate son', a formal valediction that shows he sees himself as answerable to his father even in the smallest details of his life, down to asking that his old coat be sent 'by the carrier'. The narrator, by contrast, frames her calls home as something she manages rather than a duty she owes: she tells her mother 'yes, mostly' when asked about friends, and the admission that her mother 'pretends to believe me' shows the narrator controlling what her mother is allowed to know, rather than reporting to her as Thomas reports to his father. A second difference lies in how each writer values the work itself: Thomas hopes for an advancement granted from outside, 'to be admitted a journeyman by Michaelmas', while the narrator locates the value in the earning, 'there is something about earning your own money, however little, that makes you stand up a bit straighter'. Together these details suggest a shift from a relationship of formal duty to one of protective independence, inferred from the way each writer speaks rather than stated by either of them.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Do not summarise Passage A and then Passage B; every sentence should hold a detail from each.","Choose two or three points of contrast and build the whole answer on them instead of trying to use every detail you have noticed.","Make at least one sentence end on what a contrast implies about each writer's relationship with home, not on what each writer says."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)","4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between how each writer's family responds to their absence. [8 marks]","a":"Thomas's family respond with silence — the letter to his sister has had no answer — and at best with a practical duty he can only ask for; the narrator's mother responds every night with the same three questions and a shared pretence.","sol":"The only response Thomas's family actually make inside the letter is no response at all: 'I have written to my sister but have had no answer' implies a family in which absence is met with silence, and a brother left to wonder whether his one letter was read at all. What he looks for from his father is still unanswered too, since 'if you could send my old winter coat by the carrier' is a request rather than a reply, which implies that the only response Thomas can count on is a practical one, expressed in goods sent after him rather than in words returned. The narrator's mother, by contrast, responds every night and in the same form: she 'asks the same three questions every time: am I eating, am I sleeping, and have I made any friends', which implies a mother managing her own anxiety by ritual, since the questions are repeated whatever the answers were the night before; and the narrator's admission that 'she pretends to believe me' implies that the mother knows the answers are edited and chooses to let the fiction stand. Where Thomas's absence is answered by silence and, at best, by something sent, the narrator's is answered by an unfailing routine of asking and then declining to press, so that one family leaves the absent child to guess at their care and the other makes it audible every night.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Do not simply paraphrase what each family member does or says: the marks here are for inference, so make at least one from each text about what the detail implies.","Look for something a family figure does more than once, or a question they keep coming back to, as a way into inference.","For each text write the plain detail first, then a clause beginning 'which implies...', before you settle the final sentence."],"codes":["3.1.1 summarising ideas and information from a single text","3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between Thomas's attitude to his first weeks of work away from home and the narrator's attitude to hers. [8 marks]","a":"Theme: a young person's first weeks of work away from home. Thomas's attitude is dutiful endurance looking towards a future status; the narrator's is immediate pride in her own independence despite exhaustion.","sol":"The theme linking Passage A and Passage B is a young person's first weeks of work away from home. Thomas's attitude to that experience is one of dutiful endurance: his account of physical hardship, 'my fingers black with ink and my back aching from the standing', is offered without complaint and answered by a hope of future status, 'to be admitted a journeyman by Michaelmas'. The narrator's attitude to the same experience is one of immediate pride: she lists comparable hardship, 'I have never been so tired in my life', but resolves it not in a hope for later advancement but in present self-respect, since 'earning your own money, however little' is what 'makes you stand up a bit straighter'. Whereas Thomas defers his sense of achievement to a milestone that somebody else will grant him, the narrator claims hers immediately and for herself; the same theme is therefore lived as an apprenticeship in one text and as an emancipation in the other.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Work out the shared subject of the two passages, and put it into one general sentence, before you compare anything else.","For each writer, separate the hardship they describe from the attitude they take to it; they are not the same thing.","End on what each writer's sense of achievement depends on, rather than on the hardship either of them describes."],"codes":["3.1.1 identifying the main theme or themes","3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between what earning a living means to Thomas and what it means to the narrator. [8 marks]","a":"For Thomas a wage is an obligation flowing back to the household, and he cannot yet keep himself; for the narrator earning is small but her own, and its value is self-respect rather than provision.","sol":"For Thomas, a wage is an obligation flowing back to the household. He looks forward 'to send you something towards my keep before the year is out', a phrase in which his earnings are measured against what he has cost his parents rather than against what they could buy him; and in the meantime he cannot keep himself warm without asking, 'if you could send my old winter coat by the carrier'. For the narrator, earning is a private dividend whose value is not financial at all: 'there is something about earning your own money, however little, that makes you stand up a bit straighter', where 'however little' concedes that the sum is small and then dismisses the point, because what the money has bought is posture, not provision. Whereas Thomas's wage is a debt to be repaid before it is a freedom, the narrator's is a freedom before it is anything else. The same act of earning therefore binds Thomas further into his family's economy and lifts the narrator out of her mother's, which is why he must still ask for a coat and she asks for nothing at all.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Find the sentence in each passage where money or keep is actually mentioned; there is only one in each.","Ask who each writer's earnings are for, and what, if anything, each of them still has to ask somebody else for.","End on what earning changes for each writer, rather than on how much either of them is paid."],"codes":["3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)","4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the exam stem for Paper 2 Question 1, and how many marks is it worth?","a":"'Choose four statements below which are TRUE... Choose a maximum of four statements.' 4 marks, AO1, one mark for each of the four TRUE statements shaded."},{"q":"What is the exam stem for Paper 2 Question 2, and how many marks is it worth?","a":"'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between...' 8 marks, AO1, marked by levels."},{"q":"What must a Level 4 answer to the summary question make, that a Level 1 answer does not?","a":"Perceptive inferences from both texts, with statements that show perceptive differences between them, rather than paraphrase."},{"q":"How long does the specimen Paper 2 advise for the whole of Section A, and does it time any single question?","a":"About 45 minutes for Section A, after about 15 minutes reading the Source and all five questions; it never gives a time for an individual question."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Inference (in a summary answer)","back":"A conclusion drawn from what a text implies rather than states. Example: from 'she pretends to believe me' you infer that the narrator's mother knows she is not being told everything.","code":"3.1.1 summarising ideas and information from a single text","open":false},{"front":"Paraphrase","back":"Restating a text's words without interpreting them. Example: 'Thomas wants his coat sent' merely repeats 'send my old winter coat'. Level 1 'offers paraphrase rather than inference'.","code":"3.1.1 summarising ideas and information from a single text","open":false},{"front":"Synthesis","back":"Building one point from both texts at once. Example: 'Whereas Mr. Pemberton found seventeen errors, Priya counted nine mistakes and laughed about most of them.'","code":"3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","open":false},{"front":"Theme","back":"The subject that runs through a text and links it to another. Example: both passages share the theme of a first paid job away from home.","code":"3.1.1 identifying the main theme or themes","open":false},{"front":"Explicit information","back":"Information a text states directly, in so many words. Example: 'We rise at five' states the hour Thomas's household gets up.","code":"4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)","open":false},{"front":"Implicit information","back":"Information a text suggests without stating, which the reader must infer. Example: 'I have no fire in my room' implies that Thomas is cold and poorly provided for.","code":"4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)","open":false},{"front":"Give one linking word that can open a sentence synthesising a difference between two texts","back":"whereas; in contrast; unlike","code":"3.1.1 synthesising from more than one text","open":true},{"front":"Paper 2 Question 1 — stem and marks","back":"'Choose four statements below which are TRUE... Choose a maximum of four statements.' 4 marks, AO1, one mark for each TRUE statement correctly shaded.","code":"4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)","open":false},{"front":"Paper 2 Question 2 — stem and marks","back":"'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between...' 8 marks, AO1, marked by levels 1 to 4.","code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)","open":false},{"front":"Level 4 key words (summary question)","back":"'Perceptive summary': makes perceptive inferences from both texts; makes judicious references/use of textual detail; statements show perceptive differences between texts.","code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)","open":false},{"front":"Level 1 key words (summary question)","back":"'Simple, limited summary': offers paraphrase rather than inference; makes simple reference/textual details from one/both texts; statements show simple difference between texts.","code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)","open":false},{"front":"Level 0 (summary question)","back":"'Students in this band will not have offered any differences. Nothing to reward.' A paraphrase that still states one simple difference is Level 1, not Level 0.","code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)","open":false},{"front":"AO1 (this room)","back":"Identify and interpret explicit and implicit information and ideas; select and synthesise evidence from different texts. Question 1 tests the first bullet, Question 2 both.","code":"4.5 AO1 (select and synthesise evidence from different texts)","open":false},{"front":"Give one reason a summary drops from Level 4 to Level 2","back":"it infers from only one of the two texts; its detail is only 'some appropriate references'; its statements show only 'some difference(s) between texts'","code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 2 (summary of differences)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The passages written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Passage A — 'A Letter to My Father' (letter, 19th-century voice, an apprentice writing home; written for this room, not by any real author): My dear Father, I trust this letter finds you and my mother in good health, and that the harvest has been gathered in without mishap.\nI have now been six weeks in Mr. Pemberton's printing-house, and I flatter myself that I am making steady progress, though the work is harder than I had supposed. We rise at five, and I do not lay down my composing-stick until past eight in the evening, my fingers black with ink and my back aching from the standing. Mr. Pemberton is a stern master, and exacting in his standards, but I believe him to be just; he corrected my first proof-sheet without a word of unkindness, though he found seventeen errors in it.\nI confess I am often lonely in the loft above the workshop, and I think of my mother's kitchen more than I ought to own. I have written to my sister but have had no answer.\nI would be glad, Father, if you could send my old winter coat by the carrier, for the nights grow cold, and I have no fire in my room.\nI hope, if I continue diligent, to be admitted a journeyman by Michaelmas, and to send you something towards my keep before the year is out.\nI remain, Father, your dutiful and affectionate son, Thomas","Passage B — 'Learning the Ropes' (autobiographical article, 21st-century style, a first job; written for this room, not by any real author): Three weeks ago I moved into a shared flat above a bike shop and started my first proper job, waitressing at a diner that opens at six. Six! I didn't know six o'clock existed twice a day until I started this. My alarm goes off in the dark and I stumble out still half asleep, but honestly, I love it more than I expected to.\nMy manager, Priya, is the kind of boss who will absolutely tell you when your table service is sloppy, but she also brought in a cake on my first Friday because, she said, everyone deserves cake on a Friday. She counted nine mistakes on my first shift and laughed about most of them.\nI ring my mum most nights, usually while I'm eating cold toast standing up at the counter, and she asks the same three questions every time: am I eating, am I sleeping, and have I made any friends. I tell her yes, mostly, and she pretends to believe me.\nIt isn't glamorous. My feet ache, I smell of bacon fat by ten in the morning, and I have never been so tired in my life. But there is something about earning your own money, however little, that makes you stand up a bit straighter.\nI'm hoping Priya will make me a supervisor by the summer, and honestly, I think she might."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 2 Question 2, on Passage A and Passage B)","items":["Both writers are living among strangers, but only one of them says so. Thomas admits his isolation outright and treats the admission as a fault: 'I confess I am often lonely in the loft above the workshop, and I think of my mother's kitchen more than I ought to own', where the verb 'confess' and the phrase 'more than I ought to own' turn homesickness into something a dutiful son should be ashamed of, and the one other correspondent he reaches for has not replied, since 'I have written to my sister but have had no answer'. The narrator never uses the word loneliness at all; it surfaces only in the shape of her mother's third question, 'have I made any friends', and in the reply she gives to it, 'yes, mostly', exactly the kind of half-answer her mother 'pretends to believe'. Whereas Thomas is alone and puts it in writing, to a father who must be told and to a sister who has not written back, the narrator is surrounded — a shared flat, a manager who brings in cake, a counter to eat at — and conceals whatever solitude she feels. The difference is therefore not how lonely each writer is but what each of them does with the loneliness: Thomas's confession is owed upwards to a father, while the narrator's silence is a kindness she chooses to do a mother.","Why this is Level 4 ('Perceptive summary'): it makes perceptive inferences from both texts (that the real difference is confession against concealment, not the loneliness itself); it makes judicious use of textual detail, taking a handful of words at a time ('more than I ought to own', 'yes, mostly') rather than long quotations; and its statements show a perceptive difference between the texts, because every sentence holds both writers at once instead of describing them in turn."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 1 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Thomas says he is often lonely in the loft above the workshop. He also says he thinks of his mother's kitchen. He wrote to his sister and she has not written back to him. The narrator does not say that she is lonely. Her mum asks her if she has made any friends and she says 'yes, mostly'. So Thomas is lonely in his letter and the narrator is not lonely in hers.","Why this is Level 1 ('Simple, limited summary') and not Level 0: it does state one simple difference between the texts in its last sentence, which is the Level 1 descriptor 'statements show simple difference between texts', and it is safely above Level 0, where 'students in this band will not have offered any differences'. What holds it at Level 1 is the other two descriptors: it 'offers paraphrase rather than inference', because every sentence retells a detail without saying what the detail shows, and it 'makes simple reference/textual details from one/both texts', quoting only two words. It misses Level 4's 'makes perceptive inferences from both texts' and 'statements show perceptive differences between texts': it never notices that the narrator conceals exactly what Thomas confesses, and its one difference is a fact about each writer's mood rather than an inference drawn from the pair."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"The questions this room is examined through, and the words the mark scheme uses","items":["Paper 2 Question 1 — 'Read again the first part of Source A... Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements.' 4 marks, AO1 (it 'assesses the first bullet point identify and interpret explicit and implicit information and ideas'), point-marked: the mark scheme prints (T) or (F) against each of the eight lettered statements and gives one mark for each TRUE one shaded. Not levelled.","Paper 2 Question 2 — 'You need to refer to Source A and Source B for this question. Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between...' 8 marks, AO1, and it 'assesses both bullet points'. This is the only question on either paper that is marked for synthesis across two texts.","Timing: the specimen question paper advises 'about 15 minutes reading through the Source and all five questions' and 'about 45 minutes' on Section A; it never gives a time for one question, so the eight marks of Question 2 are worth roughly nine of those forty-five minutes worked out pro rata across Section A's 4 + 8 + 12 + 16 marks.","Level 4 (7-8 marks), 'Perceptive summary': 'Shows perceptive synthesis and interpretation of both texts: Makes perceptive inferences from both texts; Makes judicious references/use of textual detail relevant to the focus of the question; Statements show perceptive differences between texts.'","Level 3 (5-6 marks), 'Clear, relevant summary': 'Shows clear synthesis and interpretation of both texts: Makes clear inferences from both texts; Selects clear references/textual detail relevant to the focus of the question; Statements show clear differences between texts.'","Level 2 (3-4 marks), 'Some attempts at summary': 'Shows some interpretation from one/both texts: Attempts some inference(s) from one/both texts; Selects some appropriate references/textual detail from one/both texts; Statements show some difference(s) between texts.'","Level 1 (1-2 marks), 'Simple, limited summary': 'Shows simple awareness from one/both texts: Offers paraphrase rather than inference; Makes simple reference/textual details from one/both texts; Statements show simple difference between texts.'","Level 0: 'Students in this band will not have offered any differences. Nothing to reward.' The line between Level 0 and Level 1 is whether any difference is stated at all, not whether it is inferred.","How a mark is fixed inside a level: 'To achieve full marks in a level, students should meet all of the skills descriptors in that level. Students achieving marks at the bottom of a level will ideally have met all skills descriptors of the previous level and at least one of the skills descriptors in that level.'","Theme — the idea or subject that links two texts; identifying it is the first move before summarising or synthesising.","Summary — selecting and compressing a text's ideas, not paraphrasing or retelling it sentence by sentence.","Synthesis — combining evidence from two texts into one point, usually in a single sentence carrying both.","Inference — a conclusion drawn from what a text implies, rewarded at Levels 3 and 4; the opposite of paraphrase.","Explicit information — information a text states directly, in so many words.","Implicit information — information a text suggests without stating directly, which a reader must infer.","On the paper the two texts are called Source A and Source B; in this room they are printed as Passage A and Passage B. The specification says only that the sources will be 'drawn from the 19th century, and either the 20th or 21st century', so either of them may be Source A: on the specimen Paper 2, Source A is the 21st-century article and Source B is the 19th-century letter."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 2 Question 2: 'Write a summary of the differences between Thomas and the narrator' (8 marks)","items":["Decoded task: use details from BOTH passages; write linked statements of difference, not two separate summaries; the shared theme is a first paid job away from home.","Move 1 — name the shared theme to yourself in one sentence before writing anything, so that every statement that follows is a difference inside one subject.","Move 2 — choose three points of contrast (the employer, contact with home, what the work is worth to each writer) instead of working through either passage line by line.","Move 3 — for each point, take one short quotation from Passage A and one from Passage B, and write a single sentence that links them with 'whereas', 'in contrast' or 'unlike'.","Move 4 — for at least two of the three points, add a sentence that says what the pair of details shows, since this is where 'perceptive inferences from both texts' is earned.","Evidence worth using: 'I believe him to be just' against 'everyone deserves cake on a Friday'; 'your dutiful and affectionate son' against 'she pretends to believe me'; 'to be admitted a journeyman by Michaelmas' against 'makes you stand up a bit straighter'.","Timing: roughly nine minutes, worked out pro rata from the 45 minutes the specimen paper advises for the whole of Section A; the paper itself times no individual question.","The last sentence must state a difference, not a similarity: end on the clearest contrast between how far each writer has become independent by the close of their text, not on what the two have in common."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T10:28:50.763Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All six fixes are genuine and none has left a new fault where it was made: lesson.why now reads 'two of its four reading questions'; questions[2].hints[1] has dropped the false count for the same two-ways-a-statement-goes-wrong taxonomy as questions[0] and [1], which fits all four false statements in each of the three sets; questions[2].hints[2] no longer names statement A; questions[11].a and .sol are rebuilt on the unanswered letter to the sister with the coat correctly presented as a request; questions[6].sol is narrowed to 'Thomas's letter to his father'; and questions[7].hints[1] and [12].hints[2] now name the ground of the contrast without stating it. On a fresh reading every countable claim holds — six and five paragraphs, four true statements in each of the three TRUE-statement questions and in examples[2] (A,B,D,F / A,B,D,E / A,B,C,F / A,B,E,G), seventeen and nine and three questions, one money sentence in each passage, 8/40 of 45 minutes = nine, AO1 10%/7.5%/2.5%, and 219 and 229 words in the two passages — every tariff is 4 or 8 in the board's own Question 1 and Question 2 stems, every level label and descriptor is verbatim from levels.md (including the Paper 2 variant of the 'bottom of a level' sentence), the specimen's Source A/B order in extras[3].items[15] is right, and no quotation anywhere departs from the printed passages; what is left is a gloss in one answer key that says the master was silent when the letter says only that he was not unkind, one solution that turns a single letter into a habit, and one hint that assumes a request the second passage does not contain."}}},"language-structure":{"id":"AQA-8700","topic":"language-structure","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"AO2 is the largest reading objective on this qualification, 17.5% of the GCSE, and it is examined through exactly three tasks: the 8-mark language question and the 8-mark structure question on Paper 1, and the 12-mark language question on Paper 2. All three reward the same sequence of moves — choose a precise detail, quote it word for word, name what it is accurately, and analyse what it does to the reader — and all three punish the same failure, a feature named but never linked to an effect. Learning that sequence once earns marks on both papers, and getting a name wrong loses marks on all three.","idea":[{"h":"What a reader has to do with a writer's word and grammar choices","code":"3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","t":"No word in a printed extract is an accident, and the marks are paid for the effect, not for the spotting. In Passage A the harbour 'had already swallowed the last of the light': the harbour is given a living appetite, and what it consumes is exactly what the reader would need in order to see, so the setting is hostile before Mara does anything at all. Grammar carries effect in the same way. 'Wood, not metal, and warm even now, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands exactly like hers.' has no finite verb in it, so it reads as Mara's own noticing rather than the narrator's report, and the reader is put inside her hand. A top-level answer never stops at the choice: it says what the choice does to the person reading it."},{"h":"Why naming the feature correctly matters","code":"3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","t":"Every AO2 level has a terminology strand, and it climbs from 'simple use of subject terminology, not always appropriately' at Level 1 to 'sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology' at Level 4. Accurate is the operative word, and the commonest way to lose the strand is to reach for a term that nearly fits. A simile compares two unlike things using 'like' or 'as': Passage C's exercise book, 'so thoroughly annotated in red ink that opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident', is one, because 'like' holds a page of corrections next to a car crash. Passage A's 'its hull streaked with rust the colour of dried blood' contains neither 'like' nor 'as'; the comparison is implied, so it is a metaphor, and calling it a simile costs the strand however good the analysis around it is. A top-level answer tests the words on the page against the definition before it writes the term down."},{"h":"What structure is, and how it acts on a reader","code":"3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text","t":"Structure is not plot: it is the order and shape in which a writer releases information, and the mark scheme names the three levels it works at — whole text (beginnings, endings, perspective shifts), paragraph (topic change, aspects of cohesion) and sentence (when it contributes to the whole). Passage A is four paragraphs long and the reader's field of view narrows with each one: the harbour, then the ferry, then a paragraph made of one four-word sentence, 'She gripped the rail.', before the last paragraph widens for a single sentence to the people 'Below deck' and shuts again on Mara alone. Passage C works at paragraph level too: its second paragraph opens 'I remember no such method.', a change of topic from the daughter's present to the writer's past, and its last paragraph returns to 'the room' that the first paragraph opened in. A top-level answer names the level a feature works at and says what the arrangement does to the reader."},{"h":"The assessment objective that pays for language and structure","code":"4.5 AO2","t":"AO2 is 'Explain, comment on and analyse how writers use language and structure to achieve effects and influence readers, using relevant subject terminology to support their views', and at 17.5% it is the largest reading objective on the GCSE: 10% on Paper 1, across the language and the structure questions, and 7.5% on Paper 2. 'Effects' and 'influence readers' are the operative words in every descriptor. The ladder is a ladder of verbs — 'Offers simple comment', 'Attempts to comment', 'Explains clearly', 'Analyses' — so a paragraph that lists three devices and never says what any of them does to a reader is a Level 1 paragraph however many terms it contains. A top-level answer makes the effect the subject of the sentence and the device the explanation of it."},{"h":"How close language analysis is examined on Paper 1","code":"4.2 Paper 1 Question 2 (language)","t":"Paper 1 Question 2 prints a short, bounded extract and asks how the writer uses language inside it, for 8 marks, offering three headings: 'words and phrases; language features and techniques; sentence forms'. Two rules decide most marks. The first is scope: nothing outside the printed extract can be credited, so summarising the rest of the source is wasted time. The second is selection: 'Selects a judicious range of textual detail' means three or four details worked properly, not a list of everything present. On Passage A's ferry sentence, a top-level answer takes 'rust the colour of dried blood' and 'never quite reached the water', names each accurately, and spends its words on what each one does to the reader's trust in the vessel."},{"h":"How whole-text structure is examined on Paper 1","code":"4.2 Paper 1 Question 3 (structure)","t":"Paper 1 Question 3 is the one question on the paper that must range across the whole source, for 8 marks: 'what the writer focuses your attention on at the beginning; how and why the writer changes this focus as the Source develops; any other structural features that interest you'. Comments on language earn nothing here unless they are shown to shape the whole, so a paragraph about a good verb is a paragraph wasted. The practical method is to read only the first sentence of each paragraph and note where the writer is standing each time; on Passage A that gives the harbour, the ferry, the rail and the wood of the rail, which is the narrowing the answer then has to explain. A top-level answer names the level each feature works at and finishes on what the ending does to the reader."}],"examples":[{"title":"Worked example — Paper 1 Question 2: naming the comparison in Passage A correctly","setup":"Look in detail at this extract from Passage A: 'The ferry itself sat low against the tide, its hull streaked with rust the colour of dried blood, and along its deck a single lamp swung on its hook, throwing arcs of yellow light that never quite reached the water.' How does the writer use language here to make the ferry feel unsafe?","steps":["Commit to six words, 'rust the colour of dried blood', instead of quoting the whole sentence, because an examiner can see a choice being made but cannot reward a paraphrase.","Test the term before writing it: a simile needs 'like' or 'as', neither word is present, so the comparison is implied and the accurate name is metaphor.","Explain what the metaphor does: rust is what time does to a ship and dried blood is what violence leaves behind, so the hull reads as a wound rather than as neglect.","Add one further detail, 'never quite reached the water', and explain that light which stops short leaves the water beneath the deck permanently unlit, telling the reader exactly where they cannot see.","Weigh the answer: two details, each quoted, named accurately and explained for effect, is what 'Analyses the effects of the writer's choices of language' with 'sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology' looks like."],"cues":["Which few words would you underline, and what is wrong with quoting the whole sentence instead?","Before you may put a name to that comparison, what do you have to check it against?","What does colouring the rust that way make the hull seem to be?","Which further few words would you add, and what do they do to what the reader is allowed to see?","You now have two named, explained details — which level's wording does that shape match?"]},{"title":"Worked example — Paper 1 Question 3: the shape of Passage A","setup":"You now need to think about the whole of Passage A. It has four paragraphs: the first is the harbour, the second the ferry, the third is a single sentence, 'She gripped the rail.', and the fourth runs from 'Wood, not metal' to 'she would not be watching it return.' How has the writer structured the text to interest you as a reader?","steps":["Ask what the opening puts in front of the reader: 'The harbour had already swallowed the last of the light' offers place and dusk before it offers a person, so weather arrives before character.","Follow the focus across the first paragraph break: the harbour is dropped and one object is kept, the ferry, so the field of view has already closed in.","Say what is done with the third paragraph: four words are given a paragraph to themselves at the narrowest point of the text, and the white space around them holds the reader still at the moment the writing closes on a hand.","Track the last paragraph honestly rather than tidily: it widens for one sentence to the people 'Below deck', then shuts again on Mara alone, so the text never returns to the harbour it opened on.","Explain the ending: two reasons are offered with 'because' and the one that matters is withheld, 'without being able to say why', so the reader is left holding a question.","Weigh the answer: an opening focus, a paragraph-level narrowing, a one-sentence paragraph and a withheld ending, each explained for effect, is 'Analyses the effects of the writer's choice of structural features'."],"cues":["What does the very first sentence hand the reader — a person, or something else?","What survives the first paragraph break, and what is left behind?","Why give four words a paragraph of their own at this point rather than joining them to what follows?","Does the focus stay as tight as it has become, or does something change in the last paragraph?","What does the final sentence do with the reasons it offers, and what does that leave the reader with?","With four structural choices now explained, which level's wording does this match?"]},{"title":"Worked example — Paper 2 Question 3: where Edmund's request sits in the letter","setup":"You now need to refer only to Passage B, the letter by Edmund written to his father. Its second paragraph opens 'I write with a request which I hope you will not think unreasonable'; its third opens 'I would count it the greatest kindness in the world if you would permit me to come home'; and the letter ends 'I am, and shall always strive to be, your dutiful and affectionate son, Edmund.' How does Edmund use language to try to influence his father?","steps":["Locate the ask before analysing anything: it is announced at the top of the second paragraph and made in the third, so whatever precedes it prepares his father and whatever follows it insures the result.","Take the preparation first: 'I have laboured over it every evening by candlelight until my eyes ached' borrows a verb of manual work for a schoolboy's Latin and measures study in physical cost, which is harder for a father to dismiss than a claim of trying.","Name the vocative in 'It is not idleness, Father, whatever the report may suggest': the word 'Father' appears twice in the letter, once in the salutation and once here, inside a sentence, so the denial is aimed straight at the man holding the report.","Name the hyperbole in the request itself, 'the greatest kindness in the world', and explain that inflating a fortnight's leave into the largest favour available flatters the father's power to grant it and makes refusal feel disproportionate.","Place the closing pledge accurately: 'your dutiful and affectionate son' is the last phrase of the letter, so it follows the request and cannot reassure in advance; with 'laboured' before the ask and 'dutiful' after it, the language of duty frames the request on both sides.","Weigh the answer: three choices, each quoted, named and explained for its effect on the father as reader, is the judicious range Level 4 asks for."],"cues":["Before naming a single technique, where in the letter does the actual request live?","What is Edmund doing with the reader's sympathy in the paragraph that comes before the ask?","He names his reader inside a sentence rather than only at the top — what does that buy him at this exact moment?","How large is the favour made to sound, and what does that do to the cost of saying no?","Where does the closing pledge of duty stand in relation to the request, and what does that position let it do?","Three choices are now quoted, named and explained — what is left to judge?"]},{"title":"Worked example — telling a simile from a metaphor in Passage C","setup":"Passage C contains several comparisons. Two of them are these. The writer's old exercise book was 'so thoroughly annotated in red ink that opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident', and the phrase he repeats from his daughter's wall is 'the mitochondrion really is the powerhouse of the cell'. Which of the two is a simile and which a metaphor, and what does each do for the reader?","steps":["Apply the definition rather than the feel of the phrase: look for 'like' or 'as' holding the two halves of the comparison apart.","The exercise book carries 'like', and it sets a page of corrections beside a car crash, two entirely unlike things, so it is a simile.","The mitochondrion phrase says one thing simply is another, with neither 'like' nor 'as' anywhere in it, so it is a metaphor.","Separate the effects: the simile turns the writer's own schooling into wreckage he survived, which is comic and disarming; the metaphor is borrowed, not invented, and the joke is that he has 'just read the phrase on her wall poster'.","Link both back to the marks: the terminology strand pays for the accurate label and the analysis strand pays for the effect, so a Level 4 paragraph has to supply both."],"cues":["What test decides whether a comparison may be called a simile?","Which of the two phrases passes that test, and what two things does it set side by side?","The other phrase compares as well — what is different about how it does it?","One comparison is the writer's own and one is second-hand; what does each do to how you see him?","Why is getting the two names right worth marks on its own here?"]},{"title":"Worked example — how Passage C's paragraphs are arranged","setup":"Passage C has four paragraphs. The first ends 'forty minutes of geography I am not allowed to interrupt.' The second opens 'I remember no such method.' The fourth ends 'to be let, however briefly, into the room.' How does the writer's arrangement of these paragraphs interest the reader?","steps":["Compare the two sides of the first break: the present tense and the daughter's wall give way to the past tense and the writer's own schooldays.","Name the move and note what carries it — a topic change made across a paragraph boundary by the shortest sentence in the piece, five words long, so the break lands hard.","Explain what the pairing buys: two revision scenes are set against each other and the reader reaches the conclusion about the two generations without being told it.","Follow the last paragraph back to the first: it closes on 'the room', which is the bedroom the column opened in, so the piece shuts the door it opened.","Say what the reader is left holding: that cohesion between the first paragraph and the last turns a set of jokes about revision into a single argument about wanting to be let in."],"cues":["What changes as you cross the first paragraph break — the subject, the time, or both?","What would you call that move, and what in the writing carries it?","What is the reader now able to do that they could not do after the first paragraph alone?","Where has the reader met a room before, and what does meeting it again do to the shape of the piece?","What has the arrangement, rather than any single joke, persuaded the reader of?"]}],"check":[{"q":"At which three levels can a structural feature work, according to the mark scheme?","a":"Whole text, paragraph and sentence level."},{"q":"Is Passage A's 'rust the colour of dried blood' a simile or a metaphor, and how can you tell?","a":"A metaphor: the comparison is implied, because neither 'like' nor 'as' appears."},{"q":"Which phrase in Passage C is a simile?","a":"'opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident' — it uses 'like' to compare two unlike things."},{"q":"What must a Level 4 AO2 answer do with subject terminology?","a":"Make sophisticated and accurate use of it."}]},"room":{"facts":["Structural features work at three levels: whole text (beginnings, endings, perspective shifts), paragraph (topic change, aspects of cohesion) and sentence (when it contributes to the whole).","Every AO2 level is built from the same three strands: the verb for effect (offers simple comment, attempts to comment, explains clearly, analyses), the range of detail (simple references, some appropriate detail, a range of relevant detail, a judicious range), and the terminology (simple and not always appropriate, some and mainly appropriate, clear and accurate, sophisticated and accurate).","A simile compares two unlike things using 'like' or 'as'; a metaphor makes the comparison without either word. Test the words on the page against the definition before writing the term down.","Each point in a language or structure answer does four things in order: quote the exact words, name the feature accurately, explain the effect on the reader, and tie that effect to the focus of the question.","Paper 1 Question 2 works only inside the printed extract; Paper 1 Question 3 must range across the whole source; Paper 2 Question 3 works on one named source only, and the two questions that require both sources are Paper 2 Question 2 and Paper 2 Question 4."],"questions":[{"q":"Look in detail at this extract from Passage A: 'The harbour had already swallowed the last of the light when Mara stepped onto the jetty. Gulls wheeled once, screamed, and vanished into the grey seam where sky met water.' How does the writer use language here to create an unsettling atmosphere? You could include the writer's choice of: words and phrases; language features and techniques; sentence forms.","a":"Personification in 'swallowed the last of the light'; the three verbs 'wheeled once, screamed, and vanished'; the metaphor 'the grey seam where sky met water'.","sol":"The writer makes the place hostile before Mara has done anything in it. 'The harbour had already swallowed the last of the light' is personification: the harbour is given an appetite, and what it consumes is precisely what the reader would need in order to see, so the setting takes rather than simply exists. The verb carries as much as the figure — 'swallowed' is silent and complete, with nothing left over — and the adverb 'already' tells the reader they have arrived too late for the light. The gulls are then given three verbs in one sentence, 'wheeled once, screamed, and vanished', a list that moves from a measured action to a human-sounding cry to absence, so the only living things in the extract apart from Mara leave at the moment they are introduced; 'once' limits the wheeling to a single turn, which makes their departure feel decided rather than casual. Finally, 'the grey seam where sky met water' is a metaphor: the horizon is called a seam, a join in cloth, so the world looks stitched together and therefore capable of coming apart. A harbour that eats light, birds that scream and go, and a sky sewn to the sea build an atmosphere in which the reader expects something to give way, without the writer once naming a threat.","m":8,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the things acting in this extract are not people at all; ask what the writer has each of them doing.","Work phrase by phrase, and put every label you reach for next to its definition before you write it down.","Give each detail a short paragraph of its own, in the order quotation, name, effect, and keep every effect about the atmosphere the question names."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","4.2 Paper 1 Question 2 (language)"]},{"q":"Look in detail at this extract from Passage A: 'She counted the boats moored below: six, then five, because the smallest had slipped its rope and was nosing out alone into the swell.' How does the writer use language here to suggest that things are slipping out of Mara's control? You could include the writer's choice of: words and phrases; language features and techniques; sentence forms.","a":"The colon and the falling count 'six, then five'; the quiet verb 'had slipped its rope'; the personification 'was nosing out alone into the swell'.","sol":"The sentence lets the reader watch control leak away rather than being told about it. The colon in 'She counted the boats moored below: six, then five' holds the sentence open so that the count itself becomes the news, and the number does not hold or rise: it drops, and the drop is handed to the reader as a bare pair of figures with nothing between them but 'then'. The writer then explains the loss with a verb of accident rather than intention — the smallest boat 'had slipped its rope' — and 'slipped' makes the escape quiet, easy and already finished before Mara looked. The personification that follows gives the boat a will of its own: it 'was nosing out alone into the swell', so a thing that ought to be tied up is now exploring, and 'nosing' is the movement of an animal testing its way forward. The adverb 'alone' does double work, describing the boat and anticipating Mara, who is about to cross without anyone she knows. The grammar completes the effect: 'was nosing' is continuous, so the boat is still going as the sentence ends, and nothing in the extract stops it. Between the falling count, the accidental verb and the drifting boat, the reader is shown a place where things come untied by themselves.","m":8,"d":1,"hints":["Something changes between one half of this sentence and the other; ask what Mara has just watched happen to it.","Look at the punctuation mark that holds the sentence open, and then at what the writer lets the boat do by itself.","Two or three details is right at this length: quote, name, and make every effect about how much control is left."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","4.2 Paper 1 Question 2 (language)"]},{"q":"Look in detail at this extract from Passage A: 'Wood, not metal, and warm even now, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands exactly like hers.' How does the writer use language here to connect Mara to the people who have made this crossing before her? You could include the writer's choice of: words and phrases; language features and techniques; sentence forms.","a":"A minor sentence with no finite verb, so it reads as Mara's own noticing; the correction 'not metal'; 'worn smooth by a hundred years of hands', where the hands stand in for the people.","sol":"The sentence has no finite verb anywhere in it: 'Wood, not metal, and warm even now, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands exactly like hers.' is a minor sentence, and the missing verb is the point, because it arrives as Mara's own noticing rather than the narrator's report and puts the reader inside her hand instead of beside it. The correction 'not metal' does the next piece of work, cancelling the material a reader would assume on a working ferry and replacing it with something older and warmer; the adverbial 'even now' quietly insists that the warmth has survived the dusk established in the first paragraph. The strongest choice is 'worn smooth by a hundred years of hands', where the hands stand in for the people themselves, so a century of passengers enters the sentence without one of them being described, and the passive construction puts the wear first and the people second — which is exactly the order in which Mara meets them, through the surface they left behind. It is worth naming what this is not: 'exactly like hers' contains the word 'like', but it compares hands with hands, things of the same kind, so it is a literal comparison and not a simile. Naming the minor sentence accurately, and refusing a label that does not fit, is what the terminology strand pays for.","m":8,"d":1,"hints":["Read the extract aloud and ask what an ordinary sentence has that this one is missing.","Check what you find against the definition of a sentence before you name it, then ask why a writer would leave that out at this moment.","Two details is enough here: quote, name, effect, and make each effect about Mara's link to people she never met."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","4.2 Paper 1 Question 2 (language)"]},{"q":"Look in detail at this extract from Passage A: 'Below deck, she knew, the other passengers would already be seated: the farmer with his crates of hens, the woman who never spoke, the twins who were not, strictly, twins at all.' How does the writer use language here to interest the reader in the other passengers? You could include the writer's choice of: words and phrases; language features and techniques; sentence forms.","a":"A colon opening a three-part list of noun phrases, each portrait withholding its explanation, with the strangest item placed last and the interruption 'strictly' implying a truth being kept back.","sol":"The colon after 'would already be seated' opens the sentence into a three-part list, and each part is a portrait that stops early. 'the farmer with his crates of hens' supplies an occupation and a cargo but no face; 'the woman who never spoke' supplies a habit and withholds its cause, so the reader is handed a peculiarity with no way to solve it; and 'the twins who were not, strictly, twins at all' is placed last, where the weight of a list falls, so the sentence closes on its own contradiction. The pattern is deliberate, each item odder than the one before it, and the writer lets the reader feel that escalation rather than announcing it. The interruption 'strictly' is the sharpest touch, because it is the language of someone being careful with the truth, which implies there is a truth being kept back; set beside 'she knew', which tells the reader that Mara has crossed often enough to expect these people, it creates a world that is fully furnished offstage. The reader becomes interested in the other passengers precisely because the sentence declines to finish any of them, and curiosity is produced by what is withheld rather than by what is described.","m":8,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what each portrait leaves out rather than at what it tells you.","Begin with the punctuation mark that opens the list, then look at the pattern the items make and at where the oddest of them sits.","Build two points — the shape of the sentence, then the effect of what it refuses to explain — and anchor each on an exact quotation."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","4.2 Paper 1 Question 2 (language)"]},{"q":"Look in detail at this extract from Passage A: 'But Mara stayed on deck as the engine coughed awake, because she wanted to watch the island go, and because some part of her understood, without being able to say why, that she would not be watching it return.' How does the writer use language here to make the reader uneasy about Mara's journey? You could include the writer's choice of: words and phrases; language features and techniques; sentence forms.","a":"Personification in 'the engine coughed awake'; the repeated conjunction 'because' setting an ordinary reason beside a strange one; the withheld explanation 'without being able to say why'.","sol":"The sentence opens with 'But', a coordinating conjunction placed where a sentence begins, so the reader feels Mara resisting something before any reason is given. The engine is then personified: it 'coughed awake', a verb borrowed from illness and from sleep, so the machine that has to carry her across is introduced as ailing and only half-conscious — the reader's unease is attached to the vessel before it is attached to the crossing. The reasons that follow are held in a repeated conjunction, 'because she wanted to watch the island go, and because some part of her understood', and the repetition is what makes the second one land: the first reason is ordinary and the second is not, but putting them in the same grammatical frame makes the strange reason sound as reasonable as the plain one. The writer then removes the explanation the reader wants, 'without being able to say why', so the knowledge is presented as real and unaccountable at once, and the final clause, 'that she would not be watching it return', is set in the future, where nothing in the passage can yet contradict it. The effect is unease rather than fear: the reader has been given a certainty and denied its cause in the same breath.","m":8,"d":1,"hints":["The sentence offers reasons; ask whether the second one really explains anything.","Look at the word the sentence begins with and at the word each reason begins with, then ask what repeating it achieves.","Two or three points, each quoted and named, and finish on the effect of what the sentence refuses to say."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","4.2 Paper 1 Question 2 (language)"]},{"q":"Look in detail at this extract from Passage A: 'The ferry itself sat low against the tide, its hull streaked with rust the colour of dried blood, and along its deck a single lamp swung on its hook, throwing arcs of yellow light that never quite reached the water. Mara's father had told her the crossing took forty minutes in calm weather and considerably longer in anything else, and tonight the sea offered nothing that could be called calm.' How does the writer use language here to make the crossing feel dangerous? You could include the writer's choice of: words and phrases; language features and techniques; sentence forms.","a":"The metaphor 'rust the colour of dried blood', which contains neither 'like' nor 'as'; the incomplete light that 'never quite reached the water'; and the negation in 'nothing that could be called calm'.","sol":"The writer makes the crossing dangerous before it has begun, and the strongest choice is also the easiest to misname. 'its hull streaked with rust the colour of dried blood' contains neither 'like' nor 'as', so it is not a simile; the comparison is implied, which makes it a metaphor, and naming it correctly is itself part of what the terminology strand rewards. The image works by laying decay over violence: rust is what time does to a ship and dried blood is what violence leaves behind, and the two are held inside one noun phrase so that the hull reads as a wound rather than as neglect. The reader therefore distrusts the vessel on sight, before a single event has taken place aboard it. The lamp extends that distrust. Its light is 'throwing arcs of yellow light that never quite reached the water', and 'never quite' does the damage: the light almost arrives and then stops, leaving the water below the deck permanently unlit, so the reader is shown exactly where they will not be able to see. The last choice is grammatical rather than figurative. 'tonight the sea offered nothing that could be called calm' defines the sea by negation, refusing the reader a positive description and leaving only the absence of calm; the phrasing 'that could be called' also makes calm a matter of what anyone is willing to say aloud, which quietly concedes that the truth is worse than the sentence states. A wounded hull, a light that stops short and a sea described only by what it is not accumulate into a danger the passage never has to name.","m":8,"d":1,"hints":["Before anything happens on board, the writer has already told the reader how to feel about this crossing; find the words doing that work.","For each choice, set the words beside the definition of the term you want to use, and write the term only if it fits.","Three points is the right number at this length: run each as quotation, name, effect, and let the effects accumulate instead of repeating."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","4.5 AO2","4.2 Paper 1 Question 2 (language)"]},{"q":"You now need to think about the whole of Passage A. This text is from the opening of a novel. How has the writer structured the text to interest you as a reader? You could write about: what the writer focuses your attention on at the beginning; how and why the writer changes this focus as the Source develops; any other structural features that interest you.","a":"A paragraph-by-paragraph narrowing from the harbour, to the ferry, to the one-sentence paragraph 'She gripped the rail.'; then a last paragraph that widens for a single sentence to the passengers 'Below deck' before closing on Mara alone with the reason withheld.","sol":"Passage A is built in four paragraphs, and the reader's field of view narrows with each one. The first gives the place before the person: 'The harbour had already swallowed the last of the light' puts weather and dusk in front of the reader, and the paragraph stays wide, taking in the gulls, the boats and a shutter somewhere behind Mara. The second paragraph drops the harbour and keeps one object, the ferry, so across a single paragraph break the reader has been moved from a whole scene to one vessel. The third paragraph is both the shortest and the narrowest point of the text: four words, 'She gripped the rail.', given a paragraph to themselves, so the white space around them holds the reader still at the exact moment the writing closes on a hand. That is a choice working at two levels at once, the paragraph and the sentence, and it slows the reader to the speed of the gesture. The fourth paragraph then behaves less simply than the first three. It opens on the wood of the rail, widens for one sentence to the people 'Below deck', and shuts again on Mara alone as 'the engine coughed awake' — so the text does not return to the harbour it opened on; it ends nearer in than it began. The final sentence completes the design by withholding: two reasons are offered with 'because', and then Mara is said to have understood 'without being able to say why', so an opening that promised a scene closes on a piece of knowledge the reader is not allowed to share. The interest lies in that shape — wide, narrower, narrowest, briefly wide again, and then a door shut.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Read only the first sentence of each paragraph and ask where the writer is standing each time.","One paragraph is far shorter than the others; ask what the writer gains by giving it a paragraph at all rather than what it says.","Take the paragraphs in order, say what each does to the reader's field of view, and finish on what the last sentence chooses not to explain."],"codes":["3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text","4.5 AO2","4.2 Paper 1 Question 3 (structure)"]},{"q":"You now need to refer only to Passage B, the letter by Edmund written to his father. How does Edmund use language to try to influence his father?","a":"Physical evidence of effort before the request ('I have laboured over it every evening by candlelight until my eyes ached'); the vocative 'Father' moved inside a sentence; the hyperbole 'the greatest kindness in the world'; and the closing pledge 'your dutiful and affectionate son', which follows the request rather than preceding it.","sol":"Edmund's letter is arranged so that its request is protected on both sides, and his language does the protecting. The request is announced at the start of the second paragraph, 'I write with a request which I hope you will not think unreasonable', and made in the third, 'I would count it the greatest kindness in the world if you would permit me to come home'; everything before those sentences prepares his father, and the last line insures the result. Before the request, Edmund builds the case for his own effort. 'I have laboured over it every evening by candlelight until my eyes ached' chooses a verb of manual work for a schoolboy's Latin, and the candle and the aching eyes turn study into physical cost, which is far harder for a father to dismiss than a claim of having tried. He then denies the charge outright and does it to his father's face: 'It is not idleness, Father, whatever the report may suggest' moves the vocative into the middle of the sentence, where the salutation has already used it once, so the denial is spoken directly at the man holding the report rather than merely written to him. The request itself is carried on hyperbole. Calling a fortnight at home 'the greatest kindness in the world' inflates an ordinary permission into the largest favour available, which flatters his father's power to grant it and makes refusing feel out of all proportion to what is being refused. What follows the request is insurance: the letter closes 'I am, and shall always strive to be, your dutiful and affectionate son'. Because this is the last phrase of the letter and stands after the ask, it cannot reassure his father in advance; what it does is close the frame, with 'laboured' before the request and 'dutiful' after it, so the language of duty stands on both sides and the father is left with a son who has already paid and already promised.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Read the letter first for where its request begins and ends; everything else is doing a job around that.","Name each choice by its definition, then ask what it is designed to make the father feel rather than think.","Three choices, each quoted and explained for its effect on the father, and a last sentence that judges which of them works hardest."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","4.5 AO2"]},{"q":"You now need to refer only to Passage C, the newspaper column about a daughter's revision. How does the writer use language to try to influence the reader's view of his own part in his daughter's revision?","a":"The simile 'opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident'; the repeated 'four' in the video-call sentence set against the irony of 'somehow this counts as being alone'; and the lift of register at the close, 'a great deal of retrospective sympathy'.","sol":"The writer's method is to make himself the least impressive person in the room and let the reader like him for it. His own schooldays arrive through a simile: an exercise book 'so thoroughly annotated in red ink that opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident'. The comparison is held by 'like', and it sets a page of corrections beside a car crash, two entirely unlike things, which is what makes it a simile rather than a metaphor; the effect is to turn his failure into something comic and safe, so the reader has laughed at him before deciding anything about him. The same self-deprecation runs through 'I say yes with the confidence of a man who has just read the phrase on her wall poster and nowhere else in forty years', where the admission is buried inside the boast. The centre of the column works by repetition. 'four laptops open, four spreadsheets glowing in four separate bedrooms' repeats the number three times across two verbless phrases appended to the clause 'my daughter revises with three friends on a video call', piling up equipment and people until the scene feels crowded, and then the clause that follows, 'and somehow this counts as being alone', withdraws all of it. The gap between what the list describes and what the sentence calls it is irony, and it carries the writer's whole case: he is telling the reader that the word 'alone' has changed meaning without once telling the reader what to think about that. By the close the register lifts. 'a great deal of retrospective sympathy for my own father' is formal, almost official vocabulary, and it sits immediately after the domestic image of being 'left holding a cup of tea'; the mismatch lets him be sincere without becoming solemn. The reader is influenced by being made to feel, in turn, amused, then complicit, then quietly moved, and never once instructed.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The writer keeps making himself the joke; ask what the joke is buying from the reader.","Test each comparison against the definitions before you name it, and look hard at what is repeated in the sentence about the video call.","Three choices, quoted and named, and a last sentence saying what the writer has persuaded the reader to feel by the end."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","4.5 AO2"]},{"q":"Focus this part of your answer on the second part of Passage A, from 'She gripped the rail.' to the end. A student, having read this section of the text, said: 'The writer makes Mara's fear completely convincing here. You feel the crossing through her hands rather than being told about it.' To what extent do you agree? In your response, you could: write about your own impressions of the characters; evaluate how the writer has created these impressions; support your opinions with references to the text.","a":"Agree with the method and qualify the word: the one-sentence paragraph and the verbless sentence do deliver the crossing through touch, but the section never names fear, and the closing sentence replaces fear with something Mara cannot account for.","sol":"I agree with the student's second sentence more than the first. The crossing is unquestionably delivered through Mara's hands, but 'fear' is the student's word, not the writer's, and this part of the passage is careful to give the reader something less nameable than that. The tactile method is exactly as the student describes it. The section opens with a paragraph made of one sentence, 'She gripped the rail.', and the decision to give four words a paragraph of their own forces a pause the reader feels as a grip before any explanation arrives. What follows is sensation rather than description: 'Wood, not metal, and warm even now, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands exactly like hers.' has no finite verb, so it reads as Mara's own noticing rather than the narrator's account, and the reader is inside the hand instead of watching it. The wear in 'worn smooth by a hundred years of hands' is the most convincing touch of all, because the earlier passengers reach Mara only through the surface they left behind; a century of people is made available to the reader without a single face being described. Where I part from the student is over 'fear'. The word never appears, and the evidence points elsewhere. The other passengers are handled with dry amusement — 'the twins who were not, strictly, twins at all' is the voice of someone entertained rather than frightened — and Mara's decision to stay on deck is given as a preference, 'because she wanted to watch the island go'. The final sentence then replaces fear with something stranger: 'some part of her understood, without being able to say why, that she would not be watching it return.' Fear has a cause, and this deliberately does not; the writer withholds the explanation, and that withholding is the real source of the section's effect, which is unease rather than terror. So the student is right about the method and imprecise about the feeling. The crossing does reach the reader through Mara's hands, and the one-sentence paragraph and the verbless sentence are what make that happen; but the section's most convincing achievement is the calm certainty of a last sentence that refuses to explain itself, and 'fear' is too ordinary a word for it.","m":20,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first whether the word the student has chosen is the right word for what this part of the passage actually gives you.","Evaluate the method, not the events: for every impression you record, name what the writer did to produce it.","Three or four paragraphs, each holding an impression, its evidence and a judgement, and a close that answers 'to what extent' with a position rather than a summary."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text"]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage C, together with Passage B, Edmund's letter to his father. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to studying alone. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Edmund asks for solitude as the condition of doing well; the columnist had that solitude and did not value it, and now watches his daughter study in company that still 'counts as being alone'. Methods: a private letter arguing by accumulation against a public column arguing by irony.","sol":"Both writers are interested in who else is in the room while a young person studies, and they take opposite views of it. Edmund wants solitude and treats it as the condition of doing well. He asks for leave 'that I might study in peace and without the noise of forty other boys about me', where 'noise' reduces his classmates to an obstacle and the plain number of them makes the school sound like a crowd rather than a community. The columnist's own schooldays were exactly the solitude Edmund is asking for, and he does not remember them warmly: 'Nobody sat with me while I copied out irregular French verbs' is flat and unadorned, and its bareness is the point, while 'copied out' makes the work mechanical and unshared. What Edmund asks for is what the columnist had. The methods differ with the form, and the forms differ because the readers do. Edmund is writing privately to the one person who can grant what he wants, so his solitude is argued for: the letter's third paragraph is a single sixty-four-word sentence that accumulates clause after clause — peace, the absence of noise, his mother, the dogs — until the request sounds reasonable by sheer weight. The columnist is writing publicly, for readers who can grant him nothing, so his case is made by irony instead: 'four laptops open, four spreadsheets glowing in four separate bedrooms, and somehow this counts as being alone' repeats the number until the scene is crowded and then lets a single word take it all back. The attitudes finally diverge over what solitude is worth. For Edmund it is the thing that will rescue his Latin, and he underwrites the request with obedience, closing as 'your dutiful and affectionate son'. For the columnist, his daughter's company-in-isolation is a change he cannot share, and his column ends not with a wish to be alone but with its opposite, 'to be let, however briefly, into the room'. One writer wants the door shut; the other wants it opened.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Both writers want to be somewhere they are not; ask where each of them is standing as he writes, and who is allowed to answer.","Pair the texts idea by idea rather than writing one and then the other, and give each pair a named method from both sides.","Three paired paragraphs and a close that states the difference in attitude plainly, with both texts quoted in every paragraph."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately"]},{"q":"Focus this part of your answer on the second part of Passage A, from 'She gripped the rail.' to the end. A student, having read this section of the text, said: 'The other passengers are the most interesting thing in this part of the passage. The writer makes them far more vivid than Mara herself.' To what extent do you agree? In your response, you could: write about your own impressions of the characters; evaluate how the writer has created these impressions; support your opinions with references to the text.","a":"Disagree, while conceding the appeal: the list is memorable because each portrait is unfinished, but the passengers are held at a distance by 'Below deck, she knew', while Mara is given the touch of the rail and the withheld knowledge of the last sentence.","sol":"I disagree with the student, although the reason the claim is tempting is itself worth examining. The other passengers are certainly memorable, and the writer has made them so with great economy. They arrive in a single sentence, after a colon, as a three-part list: 'the farmer with his crates of hens, the woman who never spoke, the twins who were not, strictly, twins at all'. Each portrait withholds more than it supplies, and the strangest is placed last, where the weight of a list falls, so the sentence ends on an unexplained contradiction. That is why they stay with the reader — not because they are vivid, but because they are unfinished, and an unfinished thing is hard to put down. Vividness, though, is a different quality, and this part of the passage gives it to Mara. She has the only physical experience in the section: 'Wood, not metal, and warm even now, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands exactly like hers.' The sentence has no finite verb, which makes it read as her own thought rather than the narrator's report, so the reader receives the rail through her palm. The passengers, by contrast, are held at a distance by the phrase that introduces them, 'Below deck, she knew', which places them out of sight and inside Mara's expectation rather than in the scene at all. The closing sentence settles it. Mara is given an interior life the passengers are never allowed: 'some part of her understood, without being able to say why, that she would not be watching it return.' The reader is admitted to a knowledge Mara herself cannot explain, which is as close as this section comes to intimacy, and none of it is available for the farmer, the silent woman or the twins. So the student has mistaken curiosity for vividness. The passengers are the passage's most intriguing detail, and their incompleteness is deliberate; but Mara is the only person here the reader is allowed to be inside, and that, not the list, is where the writing does its most convincing work.","m":20,"d":3,"hints":["Ask how much of this part of the passage each side is actually given, and from how far away.","Vividness is made by a method: for each character, name what the writer did and what it leaves the reader knowing.","Take a side, concede the strongest point against you, and let the final paragraph settle the comparison rather than repeat it."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text"]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage C, together with Passage B, Edmund's letter to his father. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to the demands that schoolwork makes on a young person. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Edmund presents the demand as a moral test he is failing and asks to be excused it; the columnist presents it as a system mastered without him and stands outside it. Methods: a semantic field of labour and obedience against deflating lists and a self-mocking simile.","sol":"For Edmund, schoolwork is a moral test he is failing; for the columnist, it is a system that has been mastered by somebody else. Edmund's language turns study into labour and then into obedience. 'I have laboured over it every evening by candlelight until my eyes ached and would give me no more service' takes its verb from the fields and its detail from the body, so the demand is measured in exhaustion rather than in hours. He then personifies the Latin itself: 'I cannot make the words obey me as the other boys make them obey.' Giving the words a will of their own excuses him, because the failure becomes a refusal on the part of the language rather than a fault in the pupil, and repeating 'obey' makes the whole business sound like a contest of authority he has lost. The letter's closing pledge, 'I am, and shall always strive to be, your dutiful and affectionate son', carries that vocabulary of obedience out of the Latin and into the family, so schoolwork and filial duty become the same demand made twice. The columnist meets the demand from outside it, and his method is the deflating list. His daughter's wall is 'a spreadsheet of coloured squares', and 'every square, apparently, represents forty minutes of geography I am not allowed to interrupt', where 'apparently' concedes that the system is not his to understand. His own schooldays return as a comic inventory — 'a satchel, a dread of Mondays that started somewhere around Saturday lunchtime, and an exercise book so thoroughly annotated in red ink that opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident' — and that simile turns the demand into wreckage he survived rather than a duty he owed. The difference is one of position as much as of period. Edmund writes privately, to the single reader who can lighten the demand, and so presents himself as its victim; the columnist writes publicly, about a demand being met perfectly well without him, and so presents himself as its bystander. Edmund asks to be excused. The columnist asks only to be let in.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what each writer thinks schoolwork is demanding, and of whom, before you look at how either of them says it.","For every attitude you identify, take a named method from each text rather than describing one text and then the other.","Three paired paragraphs and a close that names the difference in position, not just in period, with both texts quoted throughout."],"codes":["3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text","3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately"]}],"exit":[{"q":"At which three levels can a structural feature work?","a":"Whole text, paragraph and sentence."},{"q":"Which two words must a comparison contain before you may call it a simile?","a":"'Like' or 'as'."},{"q":"Is Passage A's 'rust the colour of dried blood' a simile or a metaphor?","a":"A metaphor — the comparison is implied, with no 'like' or 'as'."},{"q":"What does the top AO2 level require of subject terminology?","a":"Sophisticated and accurate use of it."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Simile","back":"A comparison of two unlike things using 'like' or 'as', e.g. Passage C's 'opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident'.","code":"3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","open":false},{"front":"Metaphor","back":"A comparison made without 'like' or 'as', one thing given as another, e.g. Passage A's 'rust the colour of dried blood'.","code":"3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","open":false},{"front":"Personification","back":"Giving a non-human thing a living action, e.g. Passage A's 'The harbour had already swallowed the last of the light'.","code":"3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","open":false},{"front":"Semantic field","back":"A group of words across a text linked by one idea, e.g. Passage B's field of duty and effort: 'laboured', 'obey', 'dutiful'.","code":"3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","open":false},{"front":"Vocative","back":"A name or title used to address someone inside a sentence, e.g. Passage B's 'It is not idleness, Father, whatever the report may suggest'.","code":"3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","open":false},{"front":"Hyperbole","back":"Deliberate exaggeration for effect rather than literal truth, e.g. Passage B's 'the greatest kindness in the world' for a fortnight's leave.","code":"3.1.1 using linguistic and literary terminology accurately","open":false},{"front":"Minor sentence","back":"A sentence with no finite verb, e.g. Passage A's 'Wood, not metal, and warm even now, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands exactly like hers.'","code":"3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","open":false},{"front":"Compound and complex sentences","back":"Compound: two main clauses joined by a conjunction. Complex: a main clause plus a finite subordinate clause. Passage A's ferry sentence is compound-complex: two main clauses plus the relative clause 'that never quite reached the water'.","code":"3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","open":false},{"front":"One-sentence paragraph","back":"A paragraph made of a single sentence, which halts the reader, e.g. Passage A's third paragraph, 'She gripped the rail.'","code":"3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text","open":false},{"front":"Topic change at paragraph level","back":"A shift of subject across a paragraph boundary, e.g. Passage C's second paragraph opening 'I remember no such method.' after the daughter's wall.","code":"3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text","open":false},{"front":"Aspects of cohesion","back":"The links that hold a text together, e.g. Passage C closing on 'the room', the bedroom its first paragraph opened in.","code":"3.1.1 analysing and evaluating how form and structure contribute to the effectiveness and impact of a text","open":false},{"front":"Level 4 AO2 language descriptor","back":"Analyses the effects of the writer's choices of language; selects a judicious range of textual detail; makes sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology.","code":"4.2 Paper 1 Question 2 (language)","open":false},{"front":"Level 4 AO2 structure descriptor","back":"Analyses the effects of the writer's choice of structural features; selects a judicious range of examples; makes sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology.","code":"4.2 Paper 1 Question 3 (structure)","open":false},{"front":"Paper 1 Question 2 and Question 3 — the two scopes","back":"Question 2 is language only, inside the printed extract. Question 3 is structure only, across the whole source. Both are 8 marks and AO2; a comment on language earns nothing on Question 3.","code":"4.5 AO2","open":false},{"front":"An example of an implied comparison in Passage A","back":"rust the colour of dried blood; the grey seam where sky met water; the engine coughed awake","code":"3.1.1 explaining and illustrating how vocabulary and grammar contribute to effectiveness and impact","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The passages written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Passage A — 'The Last Ferry' (prose fiction in a 21st-century style, an opening; written for this room):\nThe harbour had already swallowed the last of the light when Mara stepped onto the jetty. Gulls wheeled once, screamed, and vanished into the grey seam where sky met water. She counted the boats moored below: six, then five, because the smallest had slipped its rope and was nosing out alone into the swell. Somewhere behind her a shutter banged twice, then found its latch and settled.\nThe ferry itself sat low against the tide, its hull streaked with rust the colour of dried blood, and along its deck a single lamp swung on its hook, throwing arcs of yellow light that never quite reached the water. Mara's father had told her the crossing took forty minutes in calm weather and considerably longer in anything else, and tonight the sea offered nothing that could be called calm.\nShe gripped the rail.\nWood, not metal, and warm even now, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands exactly like hers. Below deck, she knew, the other passengers would already be seated: the farmer with his crates of hens, the woman who never spoke, the twins who were not, strictly, twins at all. But Mara stayed on deck as the engine coughed awake, because she wanted to watch the island go, and because some part of her understood, without being able to say why, that she would not be watching it return.","Passage B — 'A Pupil's Letter Home' (non-fiction in a 19th-century voice, a letter; written for this room):\nMy dear Father, I trust this finds you and my mother in good health, and that the harvest has been kinder to you this year than the weather here has been to us.\nI write with a request which I hope you will not think unreasonable, coming as it does so soon after the last. Mr Pemberton has again remarked upon my construing of the Latin, and I confess the fault is mine, though I have laboured over it every evening by candlelight until my eyes ached and would give me no more service. It is not idleness, Father, whatever the report may suggest; it is only that I cannot make the words obey me as the other boys make them obey.\nI would count it the greatest kindness in the world if you would permit me to come home for a fortnight before the examinations, that I might study in peace and without the noise of forty other boys about me, and that I might see you and my mother and the dogs, of whom I confess I think more often than a scholar ought.\nI am, and shall always strive to be, your dutiful and affectionate son, Edmund.","Passage C — 'The Revision Timetable' (non-fiction in a 21st-century voice, a newspaper column; written for this room):\nMy daughter has covered her bedroom wall in a spreadsheet of coloured squares, and every square, apparently, represents forty minutes of geography I am not allowed to interrupt.\nI remember no such method. I remember a satchel, a dread of Mondays that started somewhere around Saturday lunchtime, and an exercise book so thoroughly annotated in red ink that opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident. When she asks me, mid-revision, whether the mitochondrion really is the powerhouse of the cell, I say yes with the confidence of a man who has just read the phrase on her wall poster and nowhere else in forty years.\nWhat has changed, I think, is not the fear, she has plenty of that, generously self-administered, but the company. Nobody sat with me while I copied out irregular French verbs; my daughter revises with three friends on a video call, four laptops open, four spreadsheets glowing in four separate bedrooms, and somehow this counts as being alone.\nI offer to test her on capital cities. She declines, kindly, the way you decline a grandparent's help with a phone. I am left holding a cup of tea and a great deal of retrospective sympathy for my own father, who probably wanted, all those years ago, exactly what I want now: to be let, however briefly, into the room."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 2, on Passage A)","items":["The question: 'Look in detail at this extract from Passage A: The ferry itself sat low against the tide, its hull streaked with rust the colour of dried blood, and along its deck a single lamp swung on its hook, throwing arcs of yellow light that never quite reached the water. How does the writer use language here to make the ferry feel unsafe?' [8 marks, AO2]","Point: the writer condemns the ferry before anyone boards it, by describing its damage in the vocabulary of injury.","Evidence: 'its hull streaked with rust the colour of dried blood'.","Method: the phrase contains neither 'like' nor 'as', so the comparison is implied and the accurate name is metaphor; it lays a field of decay ('rust', 'streaked') over a field of violence ('dried blood') inside a single noun phrase.","Effect on the reader: rust is what time does to a ship and dried blood is what violence leaves behind, so the hull reads as a wound rather than as neglect, and the reader distrusts the vessel on sight, before the narrative has stated any danger at all.","Development: the same distrust is extended by 'never quite reached the water' — the light almost arrives and then stops, so the reader is shown precisely where they will not be able to see.","Terminology: naming this a metaphor rather than a simile is the whole of the terminology strand here, because 'simile' would be inaccurate, and an inaccurate term holds that strand down at 'simple use of subject terminology, not always appropriately' however good the analysis around it is.","Judgement: of the two details the metaphor does the heavier work, because it makes the ferry itself the source of the threat rather than the weather around it.","Meets: Level 4 — 'Analyses the effects of the writer's choices of language'; 'Selects a judicious range of textual detail'; 'Makes sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology'."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["The same question, answered at Level 2, on the same quotation, so that only the quality of the writing differs.","Point: the writer describes the ferry to show that it is old and a bit run down.","Evidence: 'its hull streaked with rust the colour of dried blood'.","Method: this is imagery, because the rust is described using a colour.","Effect on the reader: it makes the ferry seem scary and makes you not want to get on it.","Terminology: 'imagery' is used, and used appropriately, but it is the widest term available and it is never narrowed, so the answer never has to decide what kind of comparison this is.","Meets: Level 2 — 'Attempts to comment on the effect of language'; 'Selects some appropriate textual detail'; 'Makes some use of subject terminology, mainly appropriately'.","Misses Level 3's 'Explains clearly the effects of the writer's choices of language', because 'scary' names a feeling without saying what produces it: the paragraph never notices that decay and violence are being laid over each other.","Misses Level 3's 'Makes clear and accurate use of subject terminology', because 'imagery' is too wide to be precise; the Level 4 paragraph earns the strand by naming the metaphor and showing why 'simile' would be wrong.","Misses Level 3's 'Selects a range of relevant textual detail', because one quotation is used and nothing else in the printed extract is touched."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the questions this room is examined through","items":["Paper 1 Question 2: 'Look in detail at this extract from lines 8 to 18 of the Source… How does the writer use language here to describe the effects of the weather? You could include the writer's choice of: words and phrases; language features and techniques; sentence forms.' 8 marks, AO2, about 9 minutes pro rata within the 45 the specimen paper advises for Section A, working only inside the printed extract.","Paper 1 Question 2, Level 4 (7–8 marks): 'Shows detailed and perceptive understanding of language: Analyses the effects of the writer's choices of language; Selects a judicious range of textual detail; Makes sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology.'","Paper 1 Question 2, Level 3 (5–6 marks): 'Shows clear understanding of language: Explains clearly the effects of the writer's choices of language; Selects a range of relevant textual detail; Makes clear and accurate use of subject terminology.'","Paper 1 Question 2, Level 2 (3–4 marks): 'Shows some understanding of language: Attempts to comment on the effect of language; Selects some appropriate textual detail; Makes some use of subject terminology, mainly appropriately.'","Paper 1 Question 2, Level 1 (1–2 marks): 'Shows simple awareness of language: Offers simple comment on the effect of language; Selects simple references or textual details; Makes simple use of subject terminology, not always appropriately.' Level 0: 'No comments offered on the use of language. Nothing to reward.'","Paper 1 Question 3: 'You now need to think about the whole of the Source… How has the writer structured the text to interest you as a reader? You could write about: what the writer focuses your attention on at the beginning; how and why the writer changes this focus as the Source develops; any other structural features that interest you.' 8 marks, AO2, about 9 minutes pro rata within the 45 advised for Section A, ranging across the whole source.","Paper 1 Question 3, Level 4 (7–8 marks): 'Shows detailed and perceptive understanding of structural features: Analyses the effects of the writer's choice of structural features; Selects a judicious range of examples; Makes sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology.'","Paper 1 Question 3, Level 3 (5–6 marks): 'Shows clear understanding of structural features: Explains clearly the effects of the writer's choice of structural features; Selects a range of relevant examples; Makes clear and accurate use of subject terminology.'","Paper 1 Question 3, Level 2 (3–4 marks): 'Shows some understanding of structural features: Attempts to comment on the effect of structural features; Selects some appropriate examples; Makes some use of subject terminology, mainly appropriately.'","Paper 1 Question 3, Level 1 (1–2 marks): 'Shows simple awareness of structural features: Offers simple comment on the effect of structural features; Selects simple references or examples; Makes simple use of subject terminology, not always appropriately.' Level 0: 'No comments offered on the use of structure. Nothing to reward.'","Paper 1 Question 3 defines the field: 'Structural features can be: at a whole text level eg. beginnings / endings / perspective shifts; at a paragraph level eg. topic change / aspects of cohesion; and at a sentence level when judged to contribute to whole structure.'","Paper 2 Question 3: 'You now need to refer only to Source B, the letter by Henry written to his father. How does Henry use language to try to influence his father?' 12 marks, AO2, about 13 minutes pro rata within the 45 advised for Section A, working on one named source only.","Paper 2 Question 3, Level 4 (10–12 marks): 'Shows detailed and perceptive understanding of language: Analyses the effects of the writer's choices of language; Selects a judicious range of textual detail; Makes sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology.'","Paper 2 Question 3, Level 3 (7–9 marks): 'Shows clear understanding of language: Explains clearly the effects of the writer's choices of language; Selects a range of relevant textual detail; Makes clear and accurate use of subject terminology.'","Paper 2 Question 3, Level 2 (4–6 marks): 'Shows some understanding of language: Attempts to comment on the effect of language; Selects some appropriate textual detail; Makes some use of subject terminology, mainly appropriately.'","Paper 2 Question 3, Level 1 (1–3 marks): 'Shows simple awareness of language: Offers simple comment on the effects of language; Selects simple references or textual details; Makes simple use of subject terminology, not always appropriately.' Level 0: 'No comments offered on the use of language. Nothing to reward.'","Paper 1 Question 4: 'Focus this part of your answer on the second part of the Source from line 19 to the end. A student, having read this section of the text said: … To what extent do you agree? In your response, you could: write about your own impressions of the characters; evaluate how the writer has created these impressions; support your opinions with references to the text.' 20 marks, AO4 — 'Evaluate texts critically and support this with appropriate textual references' — about 22 minutes pro rata within the 45 the specimen paper advises for Section A, working on the named second part of one fiction text.","Paper 1 Question 4, Level 4 (16–20 marks), 'Perceptive, detailed evaluation': 'Shows perceptive and detailed evaluation: Evaluates critically and in detail the effect(s) on the reader; Shows perceptive understanding of writer's methods; Selects a judicious range of textual detail; Develops a convincing and critical response to the focus of the statement.'","Paper 1 Question 4, Level 3 (11–15 marks): 'Shows clear and relevant evaluation: Evaluates clearly the effect(s) on the reader; Shows clear understanding of writer's methods; Selects a range of relevant textual references; Makes a clear and relevant response to the focus of the statement.'","Paper 1 Question 4, Level 2 (6–10 marks): 'Shows some attempts at evaluation: Makes some evaluative comment(s) on effect(s) on the reader; Shows some understanding of writer's methods; Selects some appropriate textual reference(s); Makes some response to the focus of the statement.'","Paper 1 Question 4, Level 1 (1–5 marks): 'Shows simple, limited evaluation: Makes simple, limited evaluative comment(s) on effect(s) on reader; Shows limited understanding of writer's methods; Selects simple, limited textual reference(s); Makes a simple, limited response to the focus of the statement.' Level 0: 'No relevant comments offered in response to the statement, no impressions, no evaluation.'","Paper 2 Question 4: 'For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Source A, together with Source B, the father's letter to a family friend. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to parenting and education. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.' 16 marks, AO3 — 'Compare writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed, across two or more texts' — about 18 minutes pro rata within the 45 advised for Section A, working across both sources.","Paper 2 Question 4, Level 4 (13–16 marks), 'Perceptive, detailed': 'Compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way; Analyses how writers' methods are used; Selects a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts; Shows a detailed understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.'","Paper 2 Question 4, Level 3 (9–12 marks): 'Compares ideas and perspectives in a clear and relevant way; Explains clearly how writers' methods are used; Selects relevant detail to support from both texts; Shows a clear understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.'","Paper 2 Question 4, Level 2 (5–8 marks): 'Attempts to compare ideas and perspectives; Makes some comment on how writers' methods are used; Selects some appropriate textual detail/references, not always supporting from one or both texts; Identifies some different ideas and perspectives.'","Paper 2 Question 4, Level 1 (1–4 marks): 'Makes simple cross reference of ideas and perspectives; Makes simple identification of writers' methods; Makes simple references/textual details from one or both texts; Shows simple awareness of ideas and/or perspectives.' Level 0: 'No ideas offered about the differences. Nothing to reward.'","Terminology — simile: a comparison of two unlike things using 'like' or 'as'. In this room: Passage C's 'opening it felt like witnessing a minor road accident'.","Terminology — metaphor: a comparison made without 'like' or 'as', in which one thing is given as another. In this room: Passage A's 'rust the colour of dried blood', which is not a simile because neither word appears in it.","Terminology — personification: giving a non-human thing a living or human action. In this room: Passage A's 'The harbour had already swallowed the last of the light'.","Terminology — semantic field: a group of words across a text linked by a single idea. In this room: duty and effort in Passage B — 'laboured', 'idleness', 'obey', 'dutiful'.","Terminology — vocative: a name or title used to address someone inside a sentence. In this room: 'It is not idleness, Father, whatever the report may suggest'. 'Father' appears twice in Passage B: once in the salutation 'My dear Father' and once here.","Terminology — hyperbole: deliberate exaggeration used for effect rather than literal truth. In this room: 'the greatest kindness in the world' for a fortnight at home.","Terminology — irony: saying something the surrounding words show to be untrue. In this room: 'four laptops open, four spreadsheets glowing in four separate bedrooms, and somehow this counts as being alone'.","Terminology — negation: describing something by what it is not. In this room: 'tonight the sea offered nothing that could be called calm'.","Terminology — minor sentence: a sentence with no finite verb. In this room: 'Wood, not metal, and warm even now, worn smooth by a hundred years of hands exactly like hers.'","Terminology — compound and complex sentences: a compound sentence joins two main clauses with a conjunction; a complex sentence adds a finite subordinate clause to a main clause, as in 'The harbour had already swallowed the last of the light when Mara stepped onto the jetty.' Passage A's ferry sentence is both at once: it joins 'The ferry itself sat low against the tide' to 'and along its deck a single lamp swung on its hook', and it also contains the finite relative clause 'that never quite reached the water', so it is compound-complex. The participial phrases 'streaked with rust' and 'throwing arcs of yellow light' are not what makes it complex; that finite clause is.","Terminology — structural feature: a choice about the order or shape of a text, working at whole-text, paragraph or sentence level. In this room: Passage A's four paragraphs narrowing from harbour to hand, and Passage C's return to 'the room'."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 2 Question 3, on Passage B ('A Pupil's Letter Home')","items":["Decoded task: 'You now need to refer only to Passage B, the letter by Edmund written to his father. How does Edmund use language to try to influence his father?' [12 marks, AO2]. Language only, and one source only: no reference to Passage A or Passage C will be credited here.","Before planning, find the request. It is announced at the start of the second paragraph, 'I write with a request which I hope you will not think unreasonable', and made in the third, 'I would count it the greatest kindness in the world if you would permit me to come home'. Everything else in the letter is positioned around those two sentences.","Paragraph 1 — the evidence of effort, which comes before the request: 'I have laboured over it every evening by candlelight until my eyes ached'. Name the verb choice and explain that it measures study in physical cost, which is harder for a father to dismiss than a claim of having tried.","Paragraph 2 — the vocative, also before the request: 'It is not idleness, Father, whatever the report may suggest'. 'Father' appears twice in the letter, once in the salutation 'My dear Father' and once here, inside a sentence; explain that moving it into the middle of a denial aims that denial straight at the man holding the report.","Paragraph 3 — the hyperbole of the request itself: 'the greatest kindness in the world'. Explain that inflating a fortnight's leave into the largest favour available flatters the father's power to grant it and makes refusal feel disproportionate.","Paragraph 4 — the pledge that comes after the request: the letter closes 'I am, and shall always strive to be, your dutiful and affectionate son'. This is the last phrase of the letter, so it cannot reassure the father in advance of the ask; what it does is close a frame, with 'laboured' before the request and 'dutiful' after it, so the language of duty stands on both sides of it.","Evidence rule: quote the exact words before naming the technique, and name nothing you have not quoted.","Timing: about 13 minutes, pro rata within the 45 minutes the specimen paper advises for Section A, leaving time to check each quotation against the letter.","What the last paragraph must do: judge which choice works hardest on the father — the physical evidence of effort, or the flattery in the hyperbole — and say why, rather than adding a fourth technique."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 1 Question 3, on Passage A ('The Last Ferry')","items":["Decoded task: 'You now need to think about the whole of Passage A… How has the writer structured the text to interest you as a reader?' [8 marks, AO2]. Structure only: a comment on a word choice earns nothing here unless it is shown to shape the whole.","Before writing, read only the first sentence of each of the four paragraphs and note where the writer is standing each time: the harbour, the ferry, the rail, the wood of the rail.","Paragraph 1 — the beginning: 'The harbour had already swallowed the last of the light' gives place and time before it gives a person, so the reader is settled in weather and dusk before meeting Mara.","Paragraph 2 — the change of focus: the second paragraph drops the harbour and keeps one object, the ferry, so the field of view closes in across a paragraph break.","Paragraph 3 — the narrowest point: 'She gripped the rail.' is four words with a paragraph to itself. Say what the white space around it does to the reader's pace, and name it at both the paragraph and the sentence level.","Paragraph 4 — the shape of the ending: the last paragraph widens for one sentence to the people 'Below deck' and then closes on Mara alone, so the text ends nearer in than it began and never returns to the harbour.","The final move: the closing sentence offers two reasons with 'because' and then withholds the one that matters, 'without being able to say why', so the reader is left holding a question rather than an answer.","Timing: about 9 minutes, pro rata within the 45 minutes the specimen paper advises for Section A.","What the last paragraph must do: decide which structural choice does most to interest the reader — the one-sentence paragraph or the withheld ending — and justify the choice, rather than listing a further feature."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T10:29:36Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"All eight logged fixes are genuine — the ferry sentence is now correctly called compound-complex on the strength of the relative clause 'that never quite reached the water' and the complex-sentence example is a whole sentence, facts[4] now names Paper 2 Questions 2 and 4 as the cross-source pair, the Passage C comparison count no longer denies the others, questions[2].sol drops the unestablished 'cold', the false '4.5 AO2' tags are gone from both 20-mark tasks and the factfile now prints the Paper 1 Question 4 AO4 grid and the Paper 2 Question 4 AO3 grid in the specimen schemes' words, the two leaking cues have been rewritten, questions[5] is retiered to d=1, and questions[8].sol now describes the two verbless phrases accurately — and on a fresh reading every quotation, count and grammatical label in the kit holds except one overstated clause in questions[0].sol."}}},"comparing-texts":{"id":"AQA-8700","topic":"comparing-texts","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Comparing texts is the one skill in this course examined nowhere except a single question, so getting it right here recovers marks nowhere else can. The 16-mark comparison is the only place AO3 is assessed, and it carries 10% of the whole GCSE, awarded not for spotting facts in either source alone but for holding two writers' attitudes and the methods that convey them together in the same sentence. The 8-mark summary of differences trains the same synthesising habit at a smaller scale, on the same pair of sources. A student who has practised writing about two texts at once, rather than two texts in turn, is the only one who can reach the top level on either question.","idea":[{"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","h":"What 'comparing texts' actually asks for","t":"The specification's own words are 'comparing two or more texts critically with respect to the above' — 'the above' being the whole of the critical reading bullet: interpretation, inference, evidence, bias, critical reflection, the evaluation of vocabulary, form, grammar and structure. Comparing is not a separate skill bolted on afterwards; it is every reading skill you already have, aimed at two writers at once instead of one, which is why the comparison question's levels reward both the ideas you find and the methods you notice. A top-level answer therefore never reports one text and then the other: it makes a claim about both writers in one sentence and proves it with a quotation from each."},{"code":"3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts","h":"Comparison runs through the whole course","t":"The specification lists 'read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts' among the aims for the whole qualification, not just for one paper: the same section states that students should 'read critically, and use knowledge gained from wide reading to inform and improve their own writing.' Paper 2 as a whole 'looks at how different writers present a similar topic over time', so every reading skill practised on a single source in this course is also being built towards the moment it has to work on two sources side by side. A top-level answer shows that transfer: the close reading of word choice you would do on one text is done on both, and then turned into a difference."},{"code":"4.5 AO3","h":"Where the marks for comparing sit","t":"'AO3: Compare writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed, across two or more texts.' AO3 is worth 10% of the GCSE, and the weighting table prints 'N/A' against Paper 1 for it, which means every AO3 mark in the entire qualification sits inside one 16-mark question. Losing marks here cannot be made up anywhere else on the paper, so the levels reward two things at once — the ideas and perspectives compared, and the methods that convey them. A top-level answer does both in the same paragraph, naming the method that carries the idea rather than listing devices after the comparison is over."},{"code":"4.3 the two linked sources","h":"The two texts you will always be given","t":"'In section A, reading two linked sources from different time periods and genres in order to consider how each presents a perspective or viewpoint to influence the reader.' The sources 'will be drawn from the 19th century, and either the 20th or 21st century', always 'non-fiction and literary non-fiction texts', chosen so the pairing 'will always provide students with an opportunity to consider viewpoints and perspectives over time.' This room's own two passages follow that pattern: a 21st-century newspaper column and a father's letter written in 1854, both on the same question of how much freedom a child should be given. A top-level answer treats the gap in time as evidence, explaining what each period's form and manners let its writer say."},{"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","h":"How the 16-mark comparison question is marked","t":"The question always has the same shape: 'compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.' Level 4, 13 to 16 marks, needs a response that 'compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way' and 'analyses how writers' methods are used', selecting 'a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts'; a Level 1 answer, 1 to 4 marks, only 'makes simple cross reference of ideas and perspectives' and 'makes simple identification of writers' methods'. The gap between those two is the gap between summarising each text in turn and holding both in one sentence, which is why a top-level answer is built as three developed comparison paragraphs and a closing paragraph that judges."}],"examples":[{"title":"Locating a first point of comparison","setup":"The task: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence.' Passage A's writer says: 'I told her the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all: the walk only works if nobody is checking.' Passage B's writer, Josiah Fenwick, says: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it.'","steps":["Passage A's sentence trusts the child completely: no phone, no check, 'the walk only works if nobody is checking' — freedom has to be total or it is not freedom at all.","Fenwick's sentence answers the same question with the opposite rule: a boy given his 'own direction' does not learn independence, he only learns 'the taste for it' — an appetite, not a skill.","Both sentences are short, unqualified declarative statements of principle, not hedged opinions — each writer states his rule as if it needs no further argument.","That shared confidence of tone is itself a point of comparison: two writers using the same declarative certainty to argue for opposite conclusions about a child's freedom."],"cues":["What does Passage A's sentence rule out completely?","What does Fenwick's sentence claim a boy 'learns' instead of independence?","Read the two sentences one after the other: what do their length and grammar have in common?","If both writers state their rule with the same flat confidence, what does that tell you about their methods, not just their ideas?"]},{"title":"Comparing form: public sphere versus private sphere","setup":"The task: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to their children's upbringing.' Passage A opens as a newspaper column: 'Every morning for a fortnight, my daughter Nell has walked the ninety-three steps from our gate to the corner shop and back, alone, to buy a pint of milk.' Passage B opens as a private letter, and its first three lines are separate: the dateline 'Broomhill, the 3rd of March, 1854', then the salutation 'Dear Madam,', then the letter's first sentence, 'I have your letter of the 20th ult., and I confess it has occasioned me some disquiet.'","steps":["Passage A addresses a general readership who were not there and do not know Nell — the writer has to set the whole scene, the gate, the shop, the milk, before he can make his point.","Passage B's dateline and salutation fix one place, one date and one reader addressed directly before a word of argument, and that reader already knows the facts — Fenwick can open on his reaction, 'some disquiet', rather than on the events.","That difference is the public sphere versus the private sphere: an article has to persuade strangers, a letter can simply instruct someone the writer already has power over.","The form a writer chooses is therefore itself a method: Passage A's column has to win the reader round, while Passage B's letter, 'I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege', can simply issue the request."],"cues":["Who is Passage A's writer talking to, and what do they not yet know?","Before Fenwick argues anything, what three things have the first lines of his letter already fixed?","What do we call a text addressed to a general readership, and one addressed to a single reader?","If a letter-writer already has authority over his reader, what can his sentences do that a columnist's cannot?"]},{"title":"Comparing register and what it reveals about attitude","setup":"The task: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to trusting a child's own judgement.' Passage A writes: 'standing at the window with my heart going like a drum, before I made myself sit down.' Passage B writes: 'kindness misapplied is scarcely to be distinguished from neglect.'","steps":["Passage A's phrase is a simile — the heart is compared to a drum with 'like' — and it is physical and informal, so the writer is admitting his own anxiety rather than hiding it.","Passage B's phrase is abstract and formal, an antithesis that balances 'kindness' against 'neglect', so it argues a general moral rule instead of admitting a personal feeling.","The informal register lets Passage A's writer seem honest about his fear and trust the reader with it; the formal register lets Fenwick sound authoritative rather than anxious, even though both men are worried about their child.","So the same underlying anxiety about a child's independence is conveyed through opposite registers, and that choice of register is itself part of each writer's argument."],"cues":["Which single word in Passage A's phrase makes the link between the heart and the drum?","Passage B's phrase names two things a father might be accused of: what are they?","Which register — informal or formal — lets a writer admit feeling, and which lets him claim authority?","If both men are anxious underneath, what does the choice of register do to how that anxiety reads to us?"]},{"title":"Selecting evidence for a summary of differences","setup":"The task: 'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between the two fathers' attitudes to how much freedom a child should be given.' Passage A: 'A child who knows she is being watched has not really been given anything.' Passage B: 'his hours accounted for from rising to retiring, and his company chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it.'","steps":["Passage A's sentence is a definition: freedom that is monitored does not count as freedom at all.","Passage B's phrase describes total supervision, hour by hour, from waking to sleeping — the opposite of an unwatched walk.","A Level 1 summary would only paraphrase each quotation in turn; a top-level summary infers from the pair: Passage A defines freedom as the absence of watching, while Passage B defines good care as watching that never stops.","That inferred contrast, not the two quotations alone, is the 'statement' the mark scheme rewards for showing 'perceptive differences between texts'."],"cues":["What does Passage A's sentence say true freedom cannot include?","How much of Edmund's day does Passage B's phrase claim should be accounted for?","What is the difference between paraphrasing each quotation and inferring what the pair together shows?","What single sentence could hold both writers' definitions of good care at once?"]},{"title":"Writing the closing paragraph that judges","setup":"The task: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence.' You have already compared the two fathers' rules and the two forms they write in, and you are writing the closing paragraph. Passage A closes: 'Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this: small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself.' Fenwick closes his argument: 'I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege until such time as I may judge him equal to it, and to acquaint me by return of any other indulgences you have thought proper to allow.'","steps":["In Passage A the father has disappeared from his own ending: the last clause belongs to 'the child', and what she arrives at is the end of having to 'ask permission'.","Fenwick keeps the ending for himself: 'until such time as I may judge him equal to it' makes his own judgement the only condition, and 'acquaint me by return' adds a fresh demand instead of an end point.","Set the two endings in one sentence to compare them: Passage A conveys trust by writing himself out of his final clause, while Fenwick conveys distrust by writing himself into his twice.","Then judge, which is what the closing paragraph is for: Fenwick's method conveys his attitude more completely, because a condition only he can declare met enacts the withholding it describes."],"cues":["Who is the subject of the last clause of Passage A's closing sentence?","Whose decision does Fenwick's closing sentence make the condition of Edmund's freedom?","What has to happen to two endings before a paragraph can compare rather than describe them?","A closing paragraph must do one thing a body paragraph need not — what is it?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What are the two things a full-mark comparison answer must do together, not one after the other?","a":"Compare the writers' ideas and perspectives, and analyse the methods that convey them, in the same sentence."},{"q":"Which two time periods will the two linked sources always come from?","a":"One from the 19th century, and one from either the 20th or 21st century."},{"q":"What is the difference between a Level 1 summary of differences and a Level 4 one?","a":"Level 1 offers paraphrase; Level 4 makes perceptive inferences from both texts."},{"q":"Should a comparison answer be structured text by text or idea by idea?","a":"Idea by idea — each paragraph should hold both writers on the same point."},{"q":"What shape does a 16-mark comparison answer need?","a":"Three developed comparison paragraphs, each on one point across both texts, and a closing paragraph that judges."}]},"room":{"facts":["AO3 rewards comparing writers' ideas and perspectives, and the methods that convey them, across two or more texts.","Every AO3 mark in the GCSE sits in one 16-mark comparison question, marked in four levels: Level 4 13-16, Level 3 9-12, Level 2 5-8, Level 1 1-4.","The two stems: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to...' (16 marks, AO3) and 'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between...' (8 marks, AO1); about 18 minutes and about 9 minutes respectively, pro rata within the 45 minutes advised for Section A.","The two linked sources are always non-fiction or literary non-fiction, one from the 19th century and one from the 20th or 21st century, on a linked theme.","A comparison paragraph is built by idea, not by text: each paragraph puts both writers on the same point, quotes both, and names how each writer's method conveys it. Three such paragraphs and a closing paragraph that judges is the shape a 16-mark answer needs.","The methods the mark scheme names for comparison: article form (public sphere) against letter form (private sphere); different tone, register, humour or seriousness; contemporary references against traditional values; language differences that reflect different times, modes and purposes.","A top-level summary of differences makes inferences from both texts; a Level 1 summary only offers paraphrase."],"questions":[{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage A, from 'Every morning for a fortnight' to 'before I made myself sit down.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A Nell walks to the corner shop and back on her own. B Nell is six years old. C Nell buys a pint of milk. D The writer counted the distance twice. E Nell has made the walk only once. F The writer measured the distance in metres. G Nell's father walks to the shop with her. H The writer felt calm as he counted the distance.","a":"A, B, C and D are true.","sol":"A is true: Nell walks 'from our gate to the corner shop and back, alone'. B is true: 'She is six.' C is true: she goes 'to buy a pint of milk'. D is true: 'I counted it, twice'. E is false, because the walk has happened 'Every morning for a fortnight'. F is false: the distance is given in steps, 'the ninety-three steps', not in metres. G is false: the same word 'alone' rules it out. H is false: the writer counted 'with my heart going like a drum'. The four details you shade are the raw material a summary of differences later has to set against Passage B, so read them for what they fix — who walks, how far, how often — rather than for atmosphere.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["4.3 the two linked sources","3.1.1 comparing texts"],"hints":["Every statement has to be true of the words on the page, not of what you would expect to be true of a six-year-old's errand.","Take the eight statements one at a time and try to put your finger on the words that make each one true; a statement that needs you to assume anything is false.","Begin with the first sentence of the passage: it settles more than one of the statements on its own."]},{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage A, from 'Every morning for a fortnight' to 'before I made myself sit down', and the first part of Passage B, from 'I have your letter of the 20th ult.' to 'I must beg leave to differ.' Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between the walk Nell is allowed and the walk Edmund is to be permitted.","a":"Nell's walk is short, measured, daily and already happening with her father's consent; Edmund's is unmeasured, occasional, still only proposed by somebody else, and refused by his father.","sol":"Nell's walk is already a settled routine, and her father's own measurement keeps it small: 'Every morning for a fortnight' makes it a habit rather than an event, and 'the ninety-three steps from our gate to the corner shop' fixes a distance short enough to be counted, so the freedom has been limited before it was given. Edmund's walk is still hypothetical and, by comparison, unmeasured: 'Edmund is to be permitted, upon fine afternoons, to walk unaccompanied as far as the mill and back' reports a plan rather than something that has happened, 'upon fine afternoons' makes it occasional where Nell's is daily, and 'as far as the mill' carries no number at all. The deeper difference is in who granted the liberty: Nell's father designed her walk himself and counted its limits, which is why he can bear it, whereas Edmund's was granted by someone else, and Fenwick's reply, 'I must beg leave to differ', treats a liberty he did not design as one he cannot accept.","m":8,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Both sections describe a child going out and coming back; ask what is different about how far, how often, and whose idea it was.","Set one detail from each named section beside the other and write the inference the pair supports, rather than describing each section in turn.","Look first at the words each father uses to fix how far the child goes, and ask how precisely each of them measures it."]},{"q":"Read again the part of Passage A from 'Friends look faintly appalled' to 'has not really been given anything', and the part of Passage B from 'A boy left to his own direction' to 'the worse for the restraint.' Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between what each father thinks being watched does to a child.","a":"Passage A's writer thinks being watched cancels the freedom given; Fenwick thinks constant watching is the only thing that forms a boy.","sol":"For Passage A's writer, watching destroys the very thing it is meant to protect: 'A child who knows she is being watched has not really been given anything', so for him a monitored freedom is not a smaller gift but no gift at all, and his refusal of the 'tracking app' shows he would rather give up the reassurance than give up the effect. For Fenwick, being watched is not a subtraction but the whole of a boy's education: 'the constant correction of an elder' is what a boy 'requires', and the three items that follow — his lessons 'heard daily', his 'hours accounted for from rising to retiring', his 'company chosen for him' — leave no part of the day in which being unwatched could teach him anything. The difference is therefore about what an unwatched hour contains: for one father it is the only time in which anything can really be given, for the other it is empty of everything except 'the taste' for liberty.","m":8,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts"],"hints":["Each section makes a claim about what happens to a child in the hours when an adult is not looking.","Pair one detail from each named section and write a single statement of difference that covers both, instead of summarising each father in turn.","Start from the sentence in Passage A that defines what a watched child has been given, and set it against what Fenwick says a boy 'requires'."]},{"q":"Read again the last part of Passage A, from 'I still watch from the window' to the end, and the last part of Passage B, from 'I do not doubt your kindness' to 'thought proper to allow.' Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between what each father says his approach will produce.","a":"Passage A's writer says his produces confidence and a child who no longer needs permission; Fenwick says his protects the boy's future character, and it produces two further demands on the schoolmistress.","sol":"Passage A's writer describes an outcome that ends in the child's own hands: 'Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this: small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself' makes the aim the disappearance of permission itself, so what he claims to produce is a child who no longer needs his approval for what she does. Fenwick describes an outcome that stays in his own hands: a father 'does not purchase the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character', so the good he claims for his approach is something Edmund cannot feel now and cannot be consulted about. The clearest difference is in what each father asks for next: Passage A's writer asks for nothing, having 'simply stopped needing her to see me do it', while Fenwick asks for two things — that the schoolmistress 'withdraw the privilege' and that she 'acquaint me by return of any other indulgences you have thought proper to allow' — so his approach produces, above all, more reporting to himself.","m":8,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Each ending names the thing the father believes he is protecting or building; find that noun in each.","Then ask what each father does with his reader at the end — one asks for nothing, the other asks for something.","Begin by asking, of each ending in turn, who is left holding the outcome."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between the two fathers' attitudes to how much freedom a child should be given.","a":"Passage A's writer wants his daughter to have real unsupervised freedom; Passage B's writer wants his son under constant, hour-by-hour supervision.","sol":"The writer of Passage A defines freedom as the absence of watching: 'the walk only works if nobody is checking' shows that any supervision, even from a distance, would undo the point of the exercise, so his underlying belief is that a child only grows in confidence when genuinely left alone. Fenwick, in Passage B, defines good care as the opposite: 'his hours accounted for from rising to retiring' shows a child watched over every waking hour, and he states his rule directly — 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it' — so independence is a danger to be prevented rather than a skill to be built. Where Passage A's writer measures success by how little supervision is needed, Passage B's writer measures good parenting by how completely it is maintained.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Think about what each writer believes freedom actually requires, or prevents.","Look for one sentence in each passage that states, rather than just shows, the writer's rule about supervision.","Write your first statement so that it covers both texts at once, instead of describing Passage A and then Passage B."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between how the writer of Passage A and Fenwick in Passage B regard their child's own judgement.","a":"Passage A trusts Nell's judgement enough to test it now; Fenwick does not trust Edmund's judgement until he himself decides it is fit.","sol":"The writer of Passage A treats his daughter's judgement as already sound enough to be tested: sending her 'ninety-three steps' alone assumes she can manage whatever she meets on the way, and he removes the phone so that the judgement exercised is genuinely hers. Fenwick, by contrast, treats Edmund's judgement as not yet formed at all, insisting his 'company' be 'chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it' — a fitness Fenwick expects to arrive only gradually, and only under correction. The two fathers therefore disagree about when a child's judgement can be relied on: Passage A trusts it now, in a small test whose limits he has set himself; Passage B defers trusting it 'until such time as I may judge him equal to it', which leaves the decision entirely with the father and gives the boy nothing to work towards.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.5 AO3"],"hints":["Ask whether each father treats the child's judgement as ready now, or as something still being built.","Look for a phrase about who chooses the child's company or route, and who chooses for themselves.","Begin from the question of timing: ask each passage when the child's judgement is to be relied on, and support the answer from both."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between the kind of supervision Passage A and Passage B think a child needs.","a":"Passage A thinks a child needs supervision the child cannot feel; Passage B thinks a child needs supervision that is constant, itemised and impossible to miss.","sol":"Passage A's writer removes supervision deliberately: 'I still watch from the window. I have simply stopped needing her to see me do it', so what remains is invisible to the child by design and exists for the father's benefit rather than hers. Fenwick wants supervision that is neither invisible nor occasional but total, covering 'his hours accounted for from rising to retiring' and even his lessons, 'his Latin conjugations heard daily and not merely set as an exercise'. The difference is not just how much supervision each father wants, but what kind: Passage A wants supervision the child cannot feel, so that the freedom counts, while Passage B wants supervision the child is meant to feel at every hour, so that the correction counts.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Ask whether each father wants supervision the child can sense, or supervision hidden from the child.","Look for a phrase about watching that the child is not meant to notice, and a phrase about hours 'accounted for'.","Make your statement about the kind of supervision, not the amount, and prove it from both passages."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between the reasons each father gives for his view on children's independence.","a":"Passage A's writer reasons that freedom builds confidence; Fenwick reasons that supervision protects the boy's future character.","sol":"The writer of Passage A justifies his position with an outcome he wants for his daughter, confidence, built from 'small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself', so his reason is something that has to be accumulated and can only be accumulated out of sight. Fenwick justifies his position with an outcome he fears losing, character, warning that a father 'does not purchase the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character', so his reason is something that can only be protected, never built. Both fathers look to the child's future rather than the present moment, but Passage A's future is made by removing supervision now, while Fenwick's is defended by extending it.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","4.5 AO3"],"hints":["Each father names the long-term thing he is building or defending; find the noun in each passage.","Notice that both reasons are about the future, and make that the hinge of your statement.","Set the sentence about 'unwatched successes' against the sentence about what a father does not purchase."]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between what each writer implies about his own childhood.","a":"Passage A's writer had more freedom than he now allows and treats that as a loss; Fenwick had less and treats it as proof that restriction does no harm.","sol":"The writer of Passage A looks back on a childhood freer than the one he is providing: 'My own mother let me roam a whole estate at that age, further than I would dare send Nell now', so his memory works against his own present caution rather than against the freedom he gives Nell. Fenwick looks back on a restricted childhood and offers it as evidence for his case: 'I was not permitted beyond the garden wall unescorted until my thirteenth year, and I have never considered myself the worse for the restraint.' The difference is in what each memory is made to do: Passage A's writer uses his childhood to question the limits he imposes now, admitting he has 'spent thirty years wondering what we traded away', while Fenwick uses his to close the question, since the only witness to the restraint's harmlessness is Fenwick himself.","m":8,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.1 comparing texts","3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts"],"hints":["Ask whether each man's memory of his own childhood supports, or questions, the rule he now applies.","Find the sentence in each passage where the writer sets his own childhood beside the child's.","Your statement should be about what the memory is used for, not just about what each childhood was like."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Both convey their attitude through flat, unqualified declaratives, but Passage A pairs his with admitted feeling to license freedom, while Fenwick pairs his with formal authority to withdraw it; the closing nouns, confidence against character, measure the distance between them.","sol":"Both writers state their attitude to a child's independence in short, unqualified declarative sentences, and both use that flat certainty to shut the argument down — but they shut it down on opposite sides. Passage A's writer allows no exception: 'I told her the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all: the walk only works if nobody is checking', where the colon turns the second clause into a rule rather than a preference, and 'only' makes any supervision fatal to the whole experiment. Fenwick answers the same question with equal certainty and the opposite conclusion: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it' generalises Edmund's single case into a law about boys, so that his refusal sounds like settled wisdom rather than one father's anxiety. The method is shared — the unqualified declarative — but in Passage A it licenses a child's freedom and in Passage B it withdraws it.\nThe two writers convey those attitudes through opposite relationships with their own feelings. Passage A's writer puts his anxiety on the page in a simile, 'my heart going like a drum', and then shows himself overruling it, 'before I made myself sit down', so the reader watches trust being paid for rather than simply asserted. Fenwick converts the same anxiety into formal authority: 'I confess it has occasioned me some disquiet' names the feeling only in order to make it evidence of the schoolmistress's error, and 'I must beg leave to differ' uses the courtesy of the letter to close the question rather than open it. Passage A therefore conveys independence as something a father must overcome himself to give; Passage B conveys it as something a father has a duty to refuse.\nEach writer ends by naming what his approach is for, and the nouns they choose measure the distance between them. Passage A builds towards 'Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this: small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself', where the metaphor of building and the plural 'successes' make independence cumulative, something the child assembles for herself. Fenwick builds towards the antithesis of 'the boy's present contentment' against 'his future character', in which independence is not gained at all but paid for, so that a liberty allowed now looks like theft from the man Edmund will become.\nJudged as methods, Fenwick's is the more controlling and for that reason the more revealing: his sentences enact the supervision they argue for, accumulating clause upon clause until no hour of Edmund's day is left unaccounted. Passage A's writer proves his attitude differently, by writing himself out of his own last clause and leaving 'the child' as its subject. Both convey a father's anxiety; only Passage A's conveys a father willing to let that anxiety cost him something.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","3.1.1 comparing texts"],"hints":["Ask whether each writer is more confident about giving freedom or about withholding it, and what he wants the reader to feel about that confidence.","Compare the sentence forms first: find a short, unqualified sentence in each passage, then ask what each writer's form — column or letter — lets him do with it.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges, and put a quotation from each passage into every one of the three."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to a child's need for supervision. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Passage A conveys that supervision should shrink until the child cannot feel it, by a method of subtraction; Passage B conveys that it should be constant and itemised, by a method of accumulation.","sol":"Passage A conveys its attitude to supervision by describing its own removal. 'I still watch from the window. I have simply stopped needing her to see me do it' sets two short sentences side by side so that the second withdraws what the first admits: the watching goes on, but everything the child could feel has been stripped out of it. Fenwick conveys the opposite attitude by itemising supervision rather than hiding it: 'his Latin conjugations heard daily and not merely set as an exercise, his hours accounted for from rising to retiring, and his company chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it' is a list of three parallel noun phrases, each opening with the same word, 'his', and the repetition makes the supervision sound total before any argument for it has been made. One writer's method is subtraction, the other's accumulation.\nThe two also differ over whom the supervision is meant to serve, and both methods give that away. In Passage A it is admitted to be for the father: 'I have simply stopped needing her to see me do it' concedes that the need is his, and the earlier detail 'I know the distance because I counted it, twice' shows the same anxiety measured out in a number he keeps to himself. Fenwick never allows that the need might be his own. 'What he requires, at this age above all others, is the constant correction of an elder' places the requirement inside the boy, and the impersonal 'an elder', which names nobody and least of all himself, dresses one man's preference as a general law of upbringing.\nBehind the two methods lie opposite beliefs about what an unwatched hour contains. Passage A's writer frames it as the only place anything can be given: 'A child who knows she is being watched has not really been given anything' is written as a definition, so supervision does not reduce the gift, it cancels it. Fenwick frames the unwatched hour as the source of the danger: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it' makes the appetite for liberty the only thing liberty teaches, and 'nothing but' cuts off every other outcome.\nThe sharpest difference is therefore not the quantity of supervision but its visibility, and each writer's method carries it. Passage A wants a supervision the child cannot feel and admits it is there for the parent; Passage B wants a supervision the child feels at every hour and insists it is there for the boy. Fenwick's list conveys his attitude the more completely, because the accumulating phrases leave the reader, like Edmund, with no hour unaccounted for: the shape of the sentence does the arguing.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","3.1.1 comparing texts"],"hints":["Both fathers argue through the shape of their sentences as well as through what they say; read each passage again for that shape before you compare.","Compare a sentence in Passage A about watching at a distance with the long list in Passage B, and name what each structure does to the reader.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges; let the first compare how each passage is built, not just what it says."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to their children's upbringing. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Passage A uses the public form of a newspaper column, an informal register and admitted doubt; Passage B uses the private form of a letter, a formal register and a politeness that delivers instructions.","sol":"The forms the two writers choose are themselves the first statement of their attitudes. Passage A is a newspaper column, a public-sphere text written for readers who were not there and have never met Nell, so the writer must build the scene before he can build the argument: 'Every morning for a fortnight, my daughter Nell has walked the ninety-three steps from our gate to the corner shop and back, alone, to buy a pint of milk' hands strangers the distance, the destination and the errand in one sentence and invites them to judge it for themselves. Passage B is a letter, a private-sphere text addressed to a single reader who already knows the facts, so Fenwick can open on his reaction instead: 'I have your letter of the 20th ult., and I confess it has occasioned me some disquiet' begins at the point of disagreement, because the schoolmistress needs no telling what Edmund has been allowed to do.\nThe forms then decide what each man's register can do. Passage A's writer keeps the column's informality and a sense of company — 'Friends look faintly appalled when I mention it' — which lets him sound like one parent persuading others rather than a father laying down a rule, and even his concession, 'I still watch from the window', is offered as an admission between equals. Fenwick's letter is formal throughout, and its deference is a method rather than a courtesy: 'I do not doubt your kindness towards the boy, Madam' concedes nothing of substance before the reversal, 'but kindness misapplied is scarcely to be distinguished from neglect', and 'I remain, Madam, your obedient servant' signs off with a politeness that sits oddly against an instruction the reader is not free to refuse.\nBecause of those forms, the same subject is argued for in entirely different moods. Passage A can only offer: 'Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this' hedges with 'I am fairly sure' and leaves the reader free to disagree. Fenwick can command: 'I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege until such time as I may judge him equal to it, and to acquaint me by return of any other indulgences you have thought proper to allow' wraps two demands — withdraw the privilege, and report anything else — inside the polite conditional 'I would beg you', while 'by return' fixes even the schoolmistress's timetable.\nJudged side by side, form does more of the work here than vocabulary does. Both men are anxious fathers, but Passage A's public form obliges its writer to expose his doubt in order to win agreement, while Passage B's private form lets Fenwick keep his authority and conceal any doubt he may have. The letter conveys its attitude to upbringing most completely in that last request, where the demand for a report shows that Fenwick's idea of raising a child extends to supervising the person who supervises him.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","4.3 the two linked sources"],"hints":["Ask who each writer is imagining as he writes, and what that reader already knows, before you look at word choice.","Compare the two openings as forms — one sets a scene for strangers, one replies to a reader who was there — and then compare what each form lets the writer ask for.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges; the first can begin from the greeting and signed closing of Passage B."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to trusting a child's own judgement. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Passage A conveys trust by making it a small, measured test taken now; Passage B conveys distrust by deferring trust to a condition only Fenwick can declare met, with no date set.","sol":"Passage A conveys trust in a child's judgement by making it a test small enough to be survived. The precise measure 'ninety-three steps' is a distance the father has already judged safe, and when the number returns in 'It is ninety-three steps of a child discovering that she can manage a small piece of the world' the errand becomes evidence: trust is extended in an amount that can be checked. Fenwick conveys distrust by naming no amount and no date. Edmund's 'company' is to be 'chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it', where 'until' promises an end without setting one, since the only person who can declare the judgement 'fit' is Fenwick himself.\nThe two writers also differ over who holds the decision, and the grammar of their closing clauses shows it. Passage A's writer hands it, by degrees, to Nell: his last clause runs 'until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself', so the subject of the final clause is the child, not the father, and the goal is the end of permission itself. Fenwick keeps the decision entirely: 'until such time as I may judge him equal to it' plants his own first-person judgement inside the very clause that is supposed to describe Edmund's readiness, and the modal 'may' loosens it further — he does not undertake to judge, only to be the one who might.\nEach father then measures the child's judgement against his own past and reaches the opposite verdict. Passage A's writer remembers being trusted further than he now dares trust Nell, 'My own mother let me roam a whole estate at that age', and treats the gap as a loss still under examination, 'wondering what we traded away'. Fenwick remembers restraint and treats it as proof: 'I was not permitted beyond the garden wall unescorted until my thirteenth year, and I have never considered myself the worse for the restraint' — and the one moment of release he is willing to date belongs to his own childhood, not to Edmund's.\nThe comparison is finally one of timing, and the methods make it. Passage A conveys trust as something already under way, in the present perfect 'has walked' and in a number of steps anybody could count; Fenwick conveys it as something permanently ahead, in clauses that open with 'until' and end in his own judgement. His method is the harder to argue with, and that is its point: a father who never states the condition for trusting his son can never be shown to have withheld the trust unfairly.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","3.1.1 comparing texts"],"hints":["Find in each passage the words that deal with when a child's judgement may be trusted, and compare how each writer's phrasing treats that moment.","Compare a precise detail of scale in Passage A with a phrase about timing or readiness in Passage B, and ask what each implies about when trust begins.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges; the first can start from what each passage does, or refuses to do, with a number."]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B, the father's letter to the schoolmistress. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to the childhoods they themselves had. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","a":"Passage A's writer remembers more freedom than he now allows and keeps the question open; Fenwick remembers restraint, declares it harmless and closes the question, since he is his own only witness.","sol":"Both writers reach back to their own childhoods, and both offer the memory as evidence, but for opposite verdicts. Passage A's writer remembers a freedom he no longer dares repeat: 'My own mother let me roam a whole estate at that age, further than I would dare send Nell now', where the comparative 'further' measures his own practice against his mother's and finds it wanting. Fenwick remembers restraint and finds it vindicated: 'I was not permitted beyond the garden wall unescorted until my thirteenth year, and I have never considered myself the worse for the restraint.' The passive 'was not permitted' leaves the boy he was without any agency at all, and that is precisely what he offers as the proof of his case.\nThe methods differ as much as the verdicts. Passage A's writer turns his memory into a question that has outlasted the childhood itself: 'I have spent thirty years wondering what we traded away when we swapped that freedom for door-to-door supervision', where 'wondering' leaves the question open and the language of trading admits that something was given up on both sides. Fenwick turns his memory into a closed proof: the negative construction 'never considered myself the worse for the restraint' allows no cost whatever, and since the only witness called is Fenwick himself, the evidence cannot be tested by the reader he is writing to.\nEach man then applies his own childhood to the child in front of him, and the application reverses. Passage A's writer uses his freer past to indict his own caution rather than Nell's walk: what he cannot justify is that he 'would dare send Nell' no further than a corner shop. Fenwick uses his restricted past to restrict Edmund further, asking the schoolmistress 'to withdraw the privilege' of a walk 'as far as the mill and back' that she had thought 'wholesome to a boy of nine'.\nThe two memories are put to work in opposite directions: Passage A's loosens the writer's grip on the present, Fenwick's tightens it. Within his own letter Fenwick's method is the more effective, because a life offered as evidence cannot be contradicted by the reader it is offered to; but it is Passage A's unfinished 'wondering' that conveys the more honest attitude to his own childhood, since it admits that the memory may be judging him rather than the other way round.","m":16,"d":3,"codes":["4.5 AO3","4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts"],"hints":["Both writers tell you what they were allowed as boys; ask what each memory is being used to prove.","Compare the verbs: one writer is still wondering, the other has long since concluded, and that difference is a method.","Plan three paragraphs across both texts and a fourth that judges; the first can set the two sentences about their own boyhoods side by side."]}],"exit":[{"q":"Should a comparison answer be structured text by text, or idea by idea?","a":"Idea by idea, with both writers in every paragraph."},{"q":"What two things must a top-level comparison do together in the same paragraph?","a":"Compare the writers' ideas and perspectives, and analyse how their methods convey them."},{"q":"What does a Level 1 summary of differences offer instead of inference?","a":"Paraphrase."},{"q":"Which two time periods do the two linked sources come from?","a":"One from the 19th century; one from the 20th or 21st century."}]},"cards":[{"front":"AO3","open":false,"code":"4.5 AO3","back":"Compare writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed, across two or more texts."},{"front":"Perspective","open":false,"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","back":"The position or viewpoint from which a writer presents their subject, shaped by when and why they write."},{"front":"Attitude","open":false,"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","back":"A writer's expressed feeling towards their subject, shown through the words and judgements they choose."},{"front":"A public-sphere text (example)","open":true,"code":"4.3 the two linked sources","back":"A newspaper article; a magazine column; a speech."},{"front":"A private-sphere text (example)","open":true,"code":"4.3 the two linked sources","back":"A letter; a diary entry; a personal account."},{"front":"Synthesis","open":false,"code":"3 read and evaluate texts critically and make comparisons between texts","back":"Drawing details from two texts together into one statement that compares them, rather than reporting each text in turn."},{"front":"The comparison question's two required elements","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"Compare the writers' ideas or attitudes, and compare the methods they use to convey them, each supported with reference to both texts."},{"front":"Level 4 comparison key words","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"Compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way; analyses how writers' methods are used; selects a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts."},{"front":"Level 1 comparison warning","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"Makes only simple cross reference of ideas and perspectives, and simple identification of writers' methods, from one or both texts."},{"front":"Inference, not paraphrase","open":false,"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","back":"A top-level summary of differences makes inferences from both texts; a Level 1 summary only restates what each text says."},{"front":"The two linked sources (time periods)","open":false,"code":"4.3 the two linked sources","back":"One 19th-century text and one 20th- or 21st-century text, always non-fiction or literary non-fiction, on a linked theme."},{"front":"Register","open":false,"code":"3.1.1 comparing texts","back":"How formal or informal a writer's word choice and sentence patterns are, shaped by their audience."},{"front":"Declarative sentence","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"A sentence that states something as fact, ending in a full stop: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it.'"},{"front":"Antithesis","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"Two opposed ideas balanced in one structure: 'the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character'."},{"front":"Anaphora","open":false,"code":"4.3 Paper 2 Question 4 (compare)","back":"The same word repeated at the start of successive clauses or phrases: Fenwick's 'his Latin conjugations', 'his hours', 'his company'."}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The passages written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Passage A — 'The Corner-Shop Test' (non-fiction, 21st-century newspaper column, a father's account of giving his daughter independence): Every morning for a fortnight, my daughter Nell has walked the ninety-three steps from our gate to the corner shop and back, alone, to buy a pint of milk. She is six. I know the distance because I counted it, twice, standing at the window with my heart going like a drum, before I made myself sit down.\nFriends look faintly appalled when I mention it. One asked whether I had installed a tracking app on a phone I could watch from my desk. I told her the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all: the walk only works if nobody is checking. A child who knows she is being watched has not really been given anything.\nMy own mother let me roam a whole estate at that age, further than I would dare send Nell now, and I have spent thirty years wondering what we traded away when we swapped that freedom for door-to-door supervision. The corner shop is not a dare. It is ninety-three steps of a child discovering that she can manage a small piece of the world without a grown-up's hand on her shoulder, and coming home a little taller for it.\nI still watch from the window. I have simply stopped needing her to see me do it. Confidence, I am fairly sure, is built out of exactly this: small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself.","Passage B — A Father's Letter to the Schoolmistress (non-fiction, 19th-century letter, a father's instructions about his son's supervision at school): Broomhill, the 3rd of March, 1854\nDear Madam,\nI have your letter of the 20th ult., and I confess it has occasioned me some disquiet. You write that Edmund is to be permitted, upon fine afternoons, to walk unaccompanied as far as the mill and back, and that you consider such liberty wholesome to a boy of nine. I must beg leave to differ.\nA boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it. What he requires, at this age above all others, is the constant correction of an elder: his Latin conjugations heard daily and not merely set as an exercise, his hours accounted for from rising to retiring, and his company chosen for him until his own judgement is fit to choose it. I was not permitted beyond the garden wall unescorted until my thirteenth year, and I have never considered myself the worse for the restraint.\nI do not doubt your kindness towards the boy, Madam, but kindness misapplied is scarcely to be distinguished from neglect. A father who loves his son does not purchase the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character. I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege until such time as I may judge him equal to it, and to acquaint me by return of any other indulgences you have thought proper to allow.\nI remain, Madam, your obedient servant,\nJosiah Fenwick"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 2 Question 4: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence.', on Passage A and Passage B)","items":["Both writers state a clear position on how much freedom a child should be allowed, but they arrive at it by opposite routes. The writer of Passage A insists that 'the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all: the walk only works if nobody is checking', a blunt declarative statement that puts the reader on the side of trust before any argument is made; the short main clauses read like a manifesto, refusing qualification. Josiah Fenwick, writing for a private reader rather than a public one, reaches the opposite conclusion through the same confident, declarative register: 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it' generalises a single case, Edmund's, into a rule about all boys, so that his refusal sounds like settled wisdom rather than one father's anxiety. Where Passage A's writer uses the first person and a simile to model the feeling he wants the reader to share — 'my heart going like a drum' — Fenwick uses the formal address of the letter, 'I must beg leave to differ', to assert an authority the informal column never claims. The two writers therefore convey their different attitudes to independence through the same tool, the unqualified declarative sentence, put to opposite persuasive uses: one to license a child's freedom, one to withdraw it.","Meets Level 4: compares ideas and methods together in every sentence, analyses precisely how the declarative sentence works for each writer, and selects judicious detail — the simile, the form of address — from both texts rather than just quoting them."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["In Passage A the writer lets his daughter walk to the shop by herself and says 'the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all', which shows he trusts her. He writes in an informal way about his own feelings. In Passage B, however, Fenwick does not want Edmund to walk to the mill alone and says 'I must beg leave to differ', which shows he does not agree with giving children freedom. He writes in a much more formal letter. So the two writers have different attitudes to how much freedom a child should have, and they write in different styles.","Meets Level 2: attempts a direct comparison, linked by 'however', and names each style as informal or formal — some comment on how the writers' methods are used. Misses Level 3: does not explain clearly how the declarative sentence or the formal address works on the reader, and treats the two texts as two summaries placed side by side rather than one integrated argument, so the understanding stays 'some' rather than 'clear'."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Paper 2 Question 4 (comparison) and Question 2 (summary of differences)","items":["Paper 2, Question 4: 'For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Source A, together with Source B... Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to [theme].' 16 marks, AO3, about 18 minutes (pro rata within the 45 minutes advised for Section A). The three bullets printed under it: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","AO3: 'Compare writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed, across two or more texts.'","Question 4, Level 4 (13-16 marks), Perceptive, detailed: Compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way; Analyses how writers' methods are used; Selects a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts; Shows a detailed understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.","Question 4, Level 3 (9-12 marks), Clear, relevant: Compares ideas and perspectives in a clear and relevant way; Explains clearly how writers' methods are used; Selects relevant detail to support from both texts; Shows a clear understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.","Question 4, Level 2 (5-8 marks), Some, attempts: Attempts to compare ideas and perspectives; Makes some comment on how writers' methods are used; Selects some appropriate textual detail/references, not always supporting from one or both texts; Identifies some different ideas and perspectives.","Question 4, Level 1 (1-4 marks), Simple, limited: Makes simple cross reference of ideas and perspectives; Makes simple identification of writers' methods; Makes simple references/textual details from one or both texts; Shows simple awareness of ideas and/or perspectives.","Question 4, Level 0: No ideas offered about the differences. Nothing to reward.","The methods the AO3 mark scheme itself lists for comparison: 'use of article form (public sphere)/use of letter form (private sphere); use of different tone/register/humour/seriousness; use of contemporary references (Pac-Men/Google/Skype)/traditional values; language differences to reflect different times/modes/purposes.'","Those methods in this room's pair: Passage A is the article form, a newspaper column addressed to a general readership (public sphere); Passage B is the letter form, addressed to a single reader, 'Dear Madam,' and signed 'I remain, Madam, your obedient servant,' (private sphere). Tone and register: Passage A informal and confiding, 'Friends look faintly appalled when I mention it'; Passage B formal and deferential, 'I must beg leave to differ.' Contemporary reference against traditional values: 'a tracking app on a phone' against 'his Latin conjugations heard daily'. Different times and purposes: '20th ult.' and 'your obedient servant' belong to the letter-writing manners of 1854, and the column's short sentences to a newspaper page today.","The pair itself: 'two linked sources from different time periods and genres', always non-fiction or literary non-fiction, one from the 19th century and one from the 20th or 21st century, chosen to 'provide students with an opportunity to consider viewpoints and perspectives over time'. In this room, a 21st-century column and a letter dated 'the 3rd of March, 1854'.","Paper 2, Question 2: 'You need to refer to Source A and Source B for this question. Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between [X] and [Y].' 8 marks, AO1, about 9 minutes (pro rata within the 45 minutes advised for Section A). It assesses both bullet points of AO1, and no comment on method is rewarded.","Question 2, Level 4 (7-8 marks), Perceptive summary — shows perceptive synthesis and interpretation of both texts: Makes perceptive inferences from both texts; Makes judicious references/use of textual detail relevant to the focus of the question; Statements show perceptive differences between texts.","Question 2, Level 3 (5-6 marks), Clear, relevant summary — shows clear synthesis and interpretation of both texts: Makes clear inferences from both texts; Selects clear references/textual detail relevant to the focus of the question; Statements show clear differences between texts.","Question 2, Level 2 (3-4 marks), Some attempts at summary — shows some interpretation from one/both texts: Attempts some inference(s) from one/both texts; Selects some appropriate references/textual detail from one/both texts; Statements show some difference(s) between texts.","Question 2, Level 1 (1-2 marks), Simple, limited summary — shows simple awareness from one/both texts: Offers paraphrase rather than inference; Makes simple reference/textual details from one/both texts; Statements show simple difference between texts.","Question 2, Level 0: Students in this band will not have offered any differences. Nothing to reward.","Terminology — Perspective: the position or viewpoint from which a writer presents a subject, shaped by when and why they are writing.","Terminology — Attitude: a writer's expressed feeling towards their subject, shown through the words and judgements they choose.","Terminology — Public sphere / private sphere: whether a text addresses a general readership (public, for example an article) or one particular reader (private, for example a letter).","Terminology — Register: how formal or informal a writer's word choice and sentence patterns are for their audience.","Terminology — Synthesis: drawing separate details from two texts together into one statement that compares them, rather than reporting each text in turn.","Terminology — Declarative sentence: a sentence that states something as fact, such as 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it.'","Terminology — Antithesis: two opposed ideas balanced in one grammatical structure, such as 'the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character'.","Terminology — Anaphora: the same word repeated at the start of successive clauses or phrases, such as the three noun phrases in Fenwick's list, 'his Latin conjugations', 'his hours', 'his company'.","Terminology — Simile: a comparison made with 'like' or 'as', such as 'my heart going like a drum'; without one of those two words the comparison is a metaphor, such as 'coming home a little taller for it'."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 2 Question 4: 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to children's independence.' (16 marks)","items":["Decode the task: the stem's three bullets — compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts — mean every paragraph must do three things together: state the idea, name the method, and quote both writers, never one text summarised and then the other.","Structure by idea, not by text: three points of contrast, each with its own paragraph across both passages, and then a fourth, closing paragraph that judges — four paragraphs in all.","Paragraph 1 — trust versus distrust of the child's own judgement: Passage A, 'the whole point was that Nell carries no phone at all'; Passage B, 'A boy left to his own direction learns nothing but the taste for it.' Name the method both share, the short unqualified declarative, and say what each writer does with it.","Paragraph 2 — the form each writer chooses and what it lets him do: Passage A's newspaper column speaks to a public of parents ('Friends look faintly appalled when I mention it'); Passage B's private letter speaks to a single reader and can command her directly ('I would beg you, therefore, to withdraw the privilege'). Public sphere against private sphere is the mark scheme's own example of a method.","Paragraph 3 — the reason each writer gives for his view: Passage A links freedom to confidence ('small, unwatched successes, repeated until the child stops needing to ask permission to trust herself'); Passage B links supervision to character ('does not purchase the boy's present contentment at the price of his future character').","Time: about 18 minutes for 16 marks — enough for three developed comparison paragraphs and a short closing paragraph, and no more; keep every quotation short so there is room to compare rather than describe.","The closing paragraph must still compare, not summarise each writer in turn: end on the sharpest point of contrast and judge which writer's method conveys his attitude the more completely, so that the final sentence holds both texts at once."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T10:08:37Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All ten faults from the first verdict are genuinely fixed - the Question 4 stems are now the board's own, the tariffs are 4, 8 and 16 throughout, every 16-mark solution runs to three comparison paragraphs and a judging close, and the fact file prints all five levels of both grids plus the AO3 mark scheme's own list of methods - and on a fresh reading the 180 passage quotations are all verbatim, every level descriptor matches levels.md word for word, and what remains is a handful of hints that give too much away and one loose grammatical label."}}},"clear-coherent-text":{"id":"AQA-8700","topic":"clear-coherent-text","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Paper 1 Question 5 is the single biggest question on either paper: one task, 40 marks — half of Paper 1's 80 marks and a quarter of the whole GCSE, which is what the specification means by \"Writing (40 marks) (25%)\" — marked on two grids at once, AO5 Content and Organisation out of 24 and AO6 Technical Accuracy out of 16. Unlike the reading questions there is no passage to analyse and no indicative content to recall: the only preparation that pays off is rehearsed control — of purpose, of a governing structural shape, of a single sustained point of view, and of sentence-level accuracy — because the levels reward a piece that holds all of this together for several hundred words, not one that shows a flash of a single skill and then loses it.","idea":[{"code":"3.1.2 writing effectively for different purposes and audiences","h":"Writing for a purpose, not just a topic","t":"The specification names six purposes for controlled writing — \"to describe, narrate, explain, instruct, give and respond to information, and argue\" — and Paper 1 Section B always sets the first two of these together: \"descriptive or narrative writing\", chosen from \"a choice of scenario, written prompt or visual image\" with its own audience built in. Every choice in the piece, from the first word to the last, has to serve that named purpose and nothing else: a description is not a story with the plot removed, and a story opening is not a description that happens to have a character standing in it."},{"code":"3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","h":"Choosing, not just using, vocabulary, grammar, form and structure","t":"\"Judiciously\" is the specification's own word, and it is exactly what the AO5 grid marks: Content descriptors reward register matched to audience, vocabulary and \"linguistic devices\", while Organisation descriptors reward structural features, the range of ideas, and paragraphing built with discourse markers. A word, a sentence length or a paragraph break earns marks only when it is a deliberate choice serving the task — a long cumulative sentence used exactly where the pace should slow, a short one exactly where it should not."},{"code":"3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively","h":"Imaginative and creative language","t":"AO5 opens with \"communicate clearly, effectively and imaginatively\", and the paper's own aim is to \"inspire\" students \"to write creatively themselves\". Imaginative language is not decoration bolted onto plain sentences afterwards: it is a governing image, chosen early, that the whole piece can return to and develop — a single simile or piece of personification reused and varied does more for Level 4 (\"extensive and ambitious vocabulary with sustained crafting of linguistic devices\") than five different unconnected devices tried once each."},{"code":"3.1.2 using information provided by others to write in different forms","h":"Writing from what you have read","t":"The specification frames both papers as \"two equally-balanced papers, relating reading sources to the topic and theme of writing tasks\", with reading acting as stimulus that gives students \"a clear route through each paper\", and the course aims ask students to \"use knowledge gained from wide reading to inform and improve their own writing\". In practice this means borrowing structural moves, governing images and sentence patterns noticed in other writing — including the model passages in this room — and reworking them into an original piece of your own, never copying them."},{"code":"3.1.2 maintaining a consistent point of view","h":"One point of view, held all the way through","t":"A point of view, once chosen — first person or third, one character's perspective or an unattached narrator's — has to survive every paragraph. The AO5 levels grade exactly this as register and purpose sustained: Level 3 wants register \"consistently matched to audience\" and \"consistently matched to purpose\", against only an \"occasional sense of audience\" and \"occasional sense of purpose\" at lower Level 1. A piece that drifts into a second character's private thoughts, or slips from describing to narrating without deciding to, loses control of its point of view and its level together."},{"code":"3.1.2 maintaining coherence and consistency across a text","h":"Coherence: what holds a text together","t":"AO5's second sentence is this idea stated directly: \"organise information and ideas, using structural and grammatical features to support coherence and cohesion of texts.\" The Organisation descriptors grade it in visible steps: \"no paragraphs\" and \"one or two unlinked ideas\" at lower Level 1, \"random paragraph structure\" at upper Level 1, \"usually coherent paragraphs with range of discourse markers\" at lower Level 3, up to \"fluently linked paragraphs with seamlessly integrated discourse markers\" at upper Level 4. Coherence is built sentence by sentence, not fixed afterwards by inserting a few linking words."},{"code":"4.5 AO5","h":"AO5: the 24 marks for content and organisation","t":"\"AO5: Communicate clearly, effectively and imaginatively, selecting and adapting tone, style and register for different forms, purposes and audiences. Organise information and ideas, using structural and grammatical features to support coherence and cohesion of texts.\" AO5 is the single largest assessment objective in the whole GCSE at 30%, split 15% to each paper, and on Paper 1 Question 5 it is worth 24 of the 40 marks, marked on a grid whose four levels are each split into an upper and a lower range, so within Level 4 alone a response can sit anywhere from 19 to 24. What lifts a piece to the top of that range is not extra length but satisfying both columns in the same sentences: the Content column wants \"sustained crafting of linguistic devices\" while the Organisation column wants \"inventive use of structural features\" and \"seamlessly integrated discourse markers\", so a top-level answer settles its shape and its governing image before it drafts, and then writes the imagery into that shape instead of laying it on top."},{"code":"4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","h":"The task itself: Paper 1 Question 5","t":"\"Section B: Writing – descriptive or narrative writing… Writing (40 marks) (25%): 1 extended writing question (24 marks for content, 16 marks for technical accuracy).\" The specimen task sets a creative writing competition \"judged by a panel of people of your own age\" and offers a choice: \"Write a description suggested by this picture\" or \"Write the opening part of a story about a place that is severely affected by the weather.\" Every skill in this room — purpose, judicious choices, imaginative language, using reading as a source, a consistent point of view, coherence — is practised so that it survives this one 45-minute task intact."}],"examples":[{"title":"Opening with an image, not a weather report","setup":"Task: 'Write a description suggested by this scenario: a coastal town in the hour before a storm arrives.' Passage A, 'The Last Ferry', opens: \"The forecast had promised nothing worse than showers, so nobody on the harbour wall believed it until the sky over Gull Point turned the colour of a healing bruise.\" Draft an opening sentence for the new scenario that does what Passage A's first sentence does: state an ordinary, reassuring fact and then undercut it with one concrete image, rather than announcing the weather outright.","steps":["Draft: 'Nobody on Pel Sands believed the shipping forecast until the gulls went quiet.'","Name the method: an ordinary, unremarkable fact (a shipping forecast) is undercut by one concrete, sensory image (silent gulls) — the same move Passage A makes by pairing 'nothing worse than showers' with a sky 'the colour of a healing bruise'.","Explain the effect: withholding a direct statement of the coming storm and giving the reader only its first symptom builds tension before anything has actually happened, which suits a description task better than announcing the weather outright.","Link to the task: the register stays controlled rather than alarmed, matching an audience of peers judging a competition entry and a purpose that is description, not warning.","Judge: this sentence reaches for AO5's 'register is convincing and compelling for audience' because it commits to a single governing image instead of listing several unconnected details."],"cues":["What single sentence would open this description?","Which two things does that sentence pair together, and in what order?","What does withholding the storm itself do to the reader, before it arrives?","Is the register calm and controlled, or alarmed — and which one suits a competition description?","Which AO5 Level 4 phrase does this opening earn, and why?"]},{"title":"Structuring a piece: wide, then narrow, then narrower still","setup":"Task: 'Write a description suggested by this scenario: a town on the morning after a storm has passed.' Passage C, 'Harbour Light', is a story ending, and across its three paragraphs it narrows twice: from the whole town — \"the town had the scoured, astonished look of somewhere that had survived something\" — in to a sound whose source Amir cannot even see — \"Someone somewhere was already sweeping\" — and in again, in its last line, to one pair of hands: \"He picked up the broom leaning against his own doorframe\". Plan the same narrowing movement for the new scenario before drafting a paragraph.","steps":["Draft the wide opening: 'By nine the square had the flat, wrung-out look of a room after a party nobody wanted.'","Draft the narrow middle: 'One woman was already out on her step with a dustpan, working at a drift of roof-slate as if it were nothing more than sand.'","Draft the narrowest close: 'Her hands went on working for a while after she had stopped looking at what they were doing.'","Name the method: the paragraph narrows twice, from the whole square to one named action and then to a single pair of hands, mirroring the two-stage narrowing Passage C makes from 'the town' to 'someone... sweeping' and then to the broom in Amir's own hands.","Explain the effect: narrowing by stages like this lets the reader feel recovery as something small and specific rather than an abstract idea, and gives the piece a clear direction of travel rather than a list of storm damage.","Judge: this earns AO5's 'writing is compelling, incorporating a range of convincing and complex ideas' because the structure itself, not just the imagery, is doing the work of interesting the reader."],"cues":["What does the very first sentence show the reader — the whole scene, or one detail?","What is the one specific action the focus narrows down to?","The focus has reached one person — what is left to narrow to after that?","Which structural movement does this paragraph borrow from Passage C?","Why does narrowing by stages affect the reader more than listing several kinds of damage?","Which AO5 Level 4 phrase does this structural choice earn?"]},{"title":"Extending one image instead of collecting several","setup":"Task: 'Write a description suggested by this scenario: the last hour before a storm reaches an isolated house.' Passage B, 'Low Water', gives the storm agency of its own: \"it took whatever was loose and decided, all on its own, where it belonged.\" Build one paragraph that extends a single personified image rather than introducing several different devices.","steps":["Draft: 'The wind went through the yard deciding, without asking, which chair belonged where and which one belonged in the ditch.'","Extend the same image: 'By the time it reached the gate it had made up its mind about that too, and left it swinging on one hinge like a door that had lost an argument.'","Name the method: personification — the wind is given intention and judgement, and the same 'deciding' idea from Passage B is developed across two sentences instead of being used once and abandoned.","Explain the effect: sustaining one device, rather than adding a simile in the next sentence and a metaphor in the one after that, makes the image feel controlled and the writing feel crafted rather than assembled from separate ideas.","Judge: sustained development of a single device like this is what AO5's 'sustained crafting of linguistic devices' (Upper Level 4) actually asks for, as opposed to a wider but shallower range of devices tried once each."],"cues":["What quality does the wind get given, beyond simply blowing?","Does the next sentence introduce a new device, or develop the same one further?","Which device is being used, and which phrase in Passage B does it borrow the idea from?","Why might one sustained device score higher than three different ones used once each?","Which AO5 phrase specifically rewards this kind of sustained development?"]},{"title":"Keeping one point of view all the way through","setup":"Task: 'Write the opening part of a story about a man waiting for news during a storm.' Passage B, 'Low Water', does not begin inside anybody: its first paragraph is village-wide narration — \"the whole village arranged itself around that single fact\" — with no access to any one person's thoughts. It narrows at the start of its second paragraph, \"Inside the end cottage, none of that mattered to Thomas Pryce\", and from there it enters nobody else — what follows is what Thomas does, notices and will not do: \"He sat at the table with his hands flat on either side of an unopened letter\". Draft a paragraph of your own that is inside one man's perspective from its first word, and then check it for drift.","steps":["Draft: 'Owen kept his back to the window on purpose, because turning round would have meant admitting he was watching the lane for somebody else's headlights.'","Check for drift: the sentence states nothing that anyone else in the house knows, thinks or feels — only what Owen does, what he avoids doing, and what he will not admit.","Name the method: close third-person narration, often called limited perspective, which restricts the reader to one character's knowledge and feeling.","Explain the effect: a reader trusts a narrator who keeps their own rule, and one sudden glimpse of a second character's private thoughts breaks it — which is why Passage B waits for its second paragraph to enter Thomas at all, rather than dipping in and out of him.","Judge: held for a whole paragraph without a lapse, this keeps register \"consistently matched to audience\" and \"consistently matched to purpose\" at Level 3 or above, instead of dropping to \"occasional sense of audience\" at lower Level 1."],"cues":["What one sentence would open this paragraph, keeping the reader inside this man alone?","Does the sentence you have drafted give away anything a second person is thinking?","What is this kind of narration called?","Why does Passage B wait until its second paragraph before entering Thomas at all?","Which AO5 wording is put at risk by even one lapse in point of view?"]},{"title":"Linking two paragraphs so the second grows out of the first","setup":"Task: 'Write a description suggested by this scenario: a harbour in the last calm hour before a storm.' Two of this room's passages show the cohesive work you are about to do, in different places. Passage B links across a paragraph break by pointing back at the whole of its first paragraph: \"Inside the end cottage, none of that mattered to Thomas Pryce.\" Passage C has no paragraph in front of its first sentence, but that sentence opens the whole text with the very marker you are about to borrow: \"By the time the last of the water drained from the square, the town had the scoured, astonished look of somewhere that had survived something.\" Draft two linked paragraphs of your own, using a discourse marker of time to open the second.","steps":["Draft the end of paragraph one: '...and by five the whole harbour had gone quieter than a Sunday.'","Draft the opening of paragraph two: 'By the time the first boat swung loose of its mooring, nobody was still pretending the forecast had got it wrong.'","Name the method: 'By the time' is a discourse marker of time, and it opens paragraph two by dating it against something paragraph one has already put in place — the same cohesive job Passage B does by reference (\"none of that\") and Passage C does with this very marker in its opening sentence.","Explain the effect: paragraph two now reads as a consequence of paragraph one rather than a fresh, unconnected idea, which is what AO5's \"fluently linked paragraphs with seamlessly integrated discourse markers\" (Upper Level 4) describes.","Judge: start paragraph two cold, with no marker and nothing pointing back, and the connection has to be inferred instead of shown, which pulls the piece down towards \"usually coherent paragraphs\" at best."],"cues":["What moment should paragraph one end on, so that paragraph two has something to date itself against?","Which words will open paragraph two, and what do they refer back to?","What is that kind of opening called, and which passage in this room opens with the same one?","What relationship does the marker create between the two paragraphs — sequence, or two separate ideas?","What would be lost if the marker were taken out?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What must a description or narrative opening keep sustained from its first sentence to its last, according to AO5's Level 3 wording?","a":"Register and purpose 'consistently matched' to audience and purpose."},{"q":"Which AO5 skill is being tested when a piece develops one image across several sentences rather than trying several different devices once each?","a":"'Sustained crafting of linguistic devices', the Upper Level 4 vocabulary descriptor."},{"q":"What is lost if a close third-person narration suddenly reveals a second character's private thoughts?","a":"Consistency of point of view, which risks dropping the register and purpose marks towards Level 1's 'occasional sense of audience'."}]},"room":{"facts":["AO5 rewards register, purpose, vocabulary and crafted linguistic devices (Content) together with structural features, the range of ideas and paragraph cohesion (Organisation), marked as one grid out of 24.","AO6 Technical Accuracy rewards sentence demarcation and punctuation, a range of sentence forms used for effect, control of Standard English grammar, and accurate spelling and vocabulary, marked out of 16.","A consistent point of view means the same narrative perspective, register and purpose sustained from the first sentence to the last, not just decided at the start.","Coherence is built through paragraphing and discourse markers that link ideas; a text with 'no paragraphs' or 'one or two unlinked ideas' cannot reach beyond Level 1.","A deliberate structural movement — such as wide to narrow, or outside to inside — is an organisational choice that earns marks in its own right, separate from any single sentence's imagery."],"questions":[{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: dawn breaking over a coastline the morning after a violent storm. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a lifeboat crew arriving home after a night spent searching for a missing yacht. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A single governing image — what the tide has laid out and what it will take back — developed and returned to, with a structural movement from the wide beach down to one small object and back out again: the narrowing movement Passage C makes, reworked into a new scene rather than copied.","sol":"Dawn arrived over Pel Sands without any ceremony, a grey light that seemed to have been switched on rather than to have risen. The tide had gone out further than the oldest man on the front could remember, and everything the sea had finished with was laid out along the length of the beach in one long, glittering line, sorted by nothing more deliberate than the tide: a fishing crate split clean down one side, a child's sandal, a coil of blue rope set down so neatly that it looked chosen rather than lost.\n\nThe gulls were back before anybody else. They worked the tideline with the brisk indifference of people who had done this job before and expected to do it again — lifting, dropping, arguing briefly over a rope end, moving on. Further down, where the boat shed had been, a fisherman stood with one hand on a doorframe that had outlasted its own roof, and said nothing to anyone. The beach was saying it for him: two boats over on their sides, a third simply missing, a long ragged bite out of the harbour wall.\n\nNearest the steps, though, was the sandal. It stood upright in the sand, filled to the strap, still buckled, pointing inland. Somebody would come down for it before the day was out, or nobody would, and either way the sea had set it in the same line as the crate and the rope, at the same height, with no more interest in it than in anything else it had carried up the beach in the dark.\n\nBy seven the light had reached the cottages above the tideline — curtains still drawn, one chimney beginning to smoke — and the water had turned. It came back up the sand unhurried, with the whole morning in front of it.","m":40,"d":1,"hints":["Decide, before you draft a single sentence, what one governing image the whole piece will keep returning to.","Plan the structural movement across your paragraphs — where the reader's focus starts, and where it ends up.","Draft your opening sentence, and check that it gives an image rather than a plain statement of the weather."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively","3.1.2 using information provided by others to write in different forms"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a harbour town on the last evening before a long-forecast storm arrives. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a family boarding up their windows as a storm forecast worsens. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"Anticipation built through ordinary routines shown changing, rather than through the storm itself, with sensory detail chosen judiciously.","sol":"By six the harbour had emptied, and it had emptied in the wrong order. The boats came in first, hauled up the slip and lashed down by four. Then the kiosk, its grille coming down with a clatter that carried the length of the front. Then the benches, folded and chained together in a row, which had not happened before, not even in February.\n\nThe sky gave nothing away. It hung flat and ordinary grey, and the sea went on doing its usual small business against the harbour steps, so that a visitor arriving that evening without having heard the forecast might have put the whole thing down to an early closing day. The smell was the tell. There was a mineral edge to the air, sharp at the back of the throat, that had the dogs on the front pulling at their leads and the gulls, for once, sitting in long rows along the wall instead of shouting at one another, every one of them facing the same way.\n\nBehind the front, a shop door had been left to swing. Its bell rang at odd intervals, later each time, and then stopped altogether. In the lit windows above the shops, families were doing what families do the evening before: filling baths in case the water went off, taping crosses onto the glass because their grandparents had taped crosses onto glass, putting phones on charge that were already charged.\n\nBy nine the town was as ready as it knew how to be, and nothing had happened yet. The lamps came on along the front in their usual order, one after another out to the end of the wall, and in the last lit window above the chandler's a woman counted the crosses on her own glass twice over, to be sure of them.","m":40,"d":1,"hints":["Decide what ordinary evening routine you can show being disrupted, without describing the storm arriving.","Plan a structural movement across the town — for example, from the harbour itself to the streets behind it.","Draft an opening sentence that establishes the time of day and one changed detail together."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","4.5 AO5"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a bus shelter filling up as an unexpected downpour starts. Or: Write the opening part of a story about two strangers who take shelter together from a storm they did not expect. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A consistent close third-person perspective inside one of the two characters, never entering the other's thoughts, with dialogue used to shift the distance between them.","sol":"Neither of them had planned on the bus shelter. Priya had planned on being home twenty minutes ago. The boy in the wet school blazer had planned, from the look of him, on getting from one building to another without an umbrella, and the sky had made short work of that.\n\nFor the first few minutes they stood at opposite ends of the shelter, the way strangers do, watching the same rain come down at the same furious angle. Priya kept checking the sky for a break in it. Every time she looked up it seemed to come harder, until the open end of the shelter was less a window than a wall of moving grey.\n\n'You could stay there,' the boy said eventually, not quite looking at her, 'or you could come and stand where it isn't actually coming in sideways.'\n\nPriya considered pointing out that she had been perfectly dry where she was thirty seconds ago. She moved anyway. Up close he smelled of wet wool and, faintly, of chips that were never now going to be eaten hot.\n\n'Bad day for it,' she said, which was not remotely what she meant, and he laughed properly — a short sound, over almost before it had started.\n\nShe asked him which bus he wanted. He said any of them, at this point. She said that was the correct answer, and he grinned out at the rain instead of at her, and after that neither of them looked at the timetable again.\n\nOutside, the storm carried on exactly as it had been carrying on. Inside the shelter, Priya noticed that she had stopped minding about the twenty minutes, and decided not to examine that too closely while he was standing there.","m":40,"d":1,"hints":["Decide which one of the two characters the whole opening will stay inside, and what that means the reader can never be told about the other.","Plan how the dialogue will change the distance between the two characters across the extract.","Draft an opening line that places both characters in the same space before either one speaks."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 maintaining a consistent point of view","3.1.2 writing effectively for different purposes and audiences"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a market town square flooding as dusk falls. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a shopkeeper closing up for the night as a storm arrives early. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A single setting observed through a sequence of small, specific details, building an unsettling calm rather than drama.","sol":"By five the market square had stopped being a square. It was a shallow, moving sheet of brown water that held the last of the daylight better than the cobbles underneath it ever had, and it was still coming up, an inch at a time, against the steps of the bank.\n\nThe stallholders had gone hours before. Their pitches were marked now by a scatter of empty crates, light enough to float, that had drifted down against the war memorial and knocked there, one at a time, in no particular hurry.\n\nA café sign, unplugged and abandoned, drifted the whole length of the square at walking pace. Two dogs, tethered outside a shuttered shop, stood on the raised step of the doorway and watched the water come towards them with expressions somewhere between offence and professional interest.\n\nEvery so often something new dropped in — a loose slate, a branch, once an orange traffic cone that arrived from nowhere and sailed past the bus stop like a small determined boat. Wherever it landed, the rings went out from that point in slow, widening circles until they broke against the railings on the far side and came back weaker.\n\nSound behaved oddly too. The square had always thrown noise back off its four walls of shopfronts, and what it threw back now was a different noise altogether: no footsteps, no trolleys, no delivery van reversing into the loading bay, only a soft, continuous slapping along the line where the water was still finding new kerb to cover.\n\nBehind the shuttered windows the lights came on early against the dark. Between one slate and the next the square held their reflections completely still, long enough to look solid, and then let them go.","m":40,"d":1,"hints":["List three ordinary objects in the setting, and decide which one becomes the focus of the piece.","Plan an order for these details that builds rather than repeats the same idea.","Draft the sentence that first shows the square is no longer behaving like a square."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively","3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: the aftermath of a storm seen from a departing ferry. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a character who returns to a place changed by a storm. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A narrative opening with a consistent point of view, a clear situation established early, and register and purpose sustained throughout.","sol":"I had pictured the walk home a hundred times on the ferry over, and none of the hundred had looked like this. The lane still bent the same way past the chapel. But the hedge that used to run its whole length was gone, snapped off at knee height, and through the gap I could see clean across two fields that had never once been visible from the road in my lifetime.\n\nI had known, of course. Mum had told me on the phone, in the careful voice she keeps for bad news, that the storm had taken a few trees and that the Hallorans' barn had come off worst. What she had not managed to say was that the shape of the place would be different — that I would walk the last half-mile and not be sure, for three or four unsteady seconds, which gate belonged to which house.\n\nThe barn had not come off worst. It had come off entirely. Where it used to stand there was a flat rectangle of churned mud with one roof beam still upright in the middle of it, leaning at an angle nothing is supposed to lean at. Somebody had propped a hand-painted sign against the beam: KEEP OUT, the letters run a little in yesterday's rain, and underneath, smaller, WE'RE OKAY.\n\nI stood at the gap in the hedge for longer than I meant to. Past the bend I could see smoke coming off our own chimney, thin and ordinary, exactly as it does every morning of the year, and after a while I picked up my bag and walked towards it.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Decide whose perspective the whole opening will stay inside, before drafting a single line.","Plan what the character expects to find, so the reader can feel the gap between expectation and what is actually there.","Draft an opening line that places the character in motion towards the place, rather than already arrived."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 maintaining a consistent point of view","4.5 AO5"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a rescue boat crew returning to harbour after searching through a storm. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a coastguard's first day on the job during a storm. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A description built on what is withheld (the outcome of the search) as much as what is shown, with structural and sensory detail judiciously chosen.","sol":"The lifeboat came round the harbour wall at first light, low in the water, moving without any of its usual hurry. Nobody on the quay said much. They had been out since ten the night before, in weather that only a crew on call would have gone out in, and the seven hours since had taught the town the difference between hoping and waiting.\n\nSal Osei stood nearest the steps with a blanket folded over one arm. She had stood in that spot a dozen times before. She watched the crew come up one at a time: soaked through, moving stiffly, saying almost nothing to the crowd that had gathered without quite deciding to. The last one off looked straight at her and shook his head, once. That was the whole report anybody was getting for now.\n\nThe boat told the rest of it. The rail was buckled on one side where something heavier than water had hit it. A length of orange rope, cut rather than untied, still trailed from a cleat near the stern. Somewhere out past the headland there was still a yacht that had not been found, and a family who did not yet know that the search had come back without them.\n\nSal put the blanket round the nearest shoulder anyway, because that was the job. Behind her the crew stood about on the quay in their wet gear with their hands in their armpits, listening to the engine tick as it cooled, and not one of them went home until the boat had been made fast.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Decide what the reader is allowed to know by the end, and what is deliberately left unanswered.","Plan a structural movement from the wide scene (the boat, the quay) to one specific physical detail.","Draft an opening sentence that shows the boat's movement rather than stating how the crew feel."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","4.5 AO5"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: the moment the wind changes direction on a headland, sensed before it is seen. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a walker realising, too late, that the weather has turned. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A description organised around the senses in a deliberate order — the change felt on the skin first, then seen, then pinned down in counted, concrete detail — and named only after it has been registered.","sol":"Idris feels it before he sees anything at all. A coolness arrives against the left side of his face, from the sea side rather than the land side, and after nine summers on this path his body works out what that means a good second before his mind agrees to it: the whole system offshore has swung round out where he cannot see it. He stops walking.\n\nThen the hillside shows it. The gorse goes over all at once, every yellow flower along the path leaning the opposite way to the one it has leaned all morning. Three gulls riding the updraught above the cliff edge tip sideways without moving their wings and have to work, visibly, to get back to where they were. Below them the water has not changed colour yet — it is the same flat pewter it has been since dawn — but a darker seam is moving across it from the horizon inward.\n\nSo he counts the boats still out, because counting is what he does instead of worrying. Four. Then three, as one turns for home while he watches, its wake set wrong against the swell. By the time he has counted down to two he is already moving, not running exactly, but walking the way you walk when some older, wordless part of you has decided the day is over and is waiting for the rest of you to catch up. The rain reaches him before the stile does, fine and almost polite, coming in level off the sea, and he does not stop to put his hood up.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Decide which sense (touch, sound, sight) will register the change first, and which will register it last.","Plan how the piece moves from a general feeling to one counted, specific detail.","Draft the sentence that shows the very first physical sign of the change, before naming what has changed."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively","3.1.2 maintaining coherence and consistency across a text"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a post office preparing to close early as a storm approaches. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a postal worker delivering the last letter before the storm cuts the road. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A narrative opening built around one small, deliberate decision, with a consistent perspective and coherent paragraphing linking cause to consequence.","sol":"Denny had four letters left and one road that the radio had just finished explaining would not be a road, in any useful sense, within the hour. He did the arithmetic he always did: four letters, one road, one hour, and a wife at home who had already rung twice to ask, in the careful voice she used instead of the words don't be an idiot, whether he was nearly done.\n\nHe was not nearly done. The Hendersons he could manage; their gate was still this side of the bridge. The Pryce place beyond it was the problem. The low stretch of lane before their turning was already carrying more water than a lane ought to hold, brown and moving with a purpose he did not much like the look of.\n\nSo he sat at the top of the dip for a minute with the engine running. The envelope on the passenger seat was addressed in handwriting he half recognised, careful and upright, the sort of hand that suggested whatever was inside had taken some deciding to write. It was only a letter. It could wait a day. Nothing about a delayed letter had ever actually hurt anybody, and there was a form for exactly this, and the form would be on his side.\n\nHe looked at the water a while longer. Then at the envelope. Then he put the van into gear and went down into the dip anyway, slowly, the wheels finding less road than he would have liked, because nineteen years on this round had taught him that the letters people spend a long time deciding to write are not, on the whole, improved by arriving a day late.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Decide on the one small decision the character has to make, and keep the whole extract focused on it.","Plan how the paragraphs link cause to consequence, using a discourse marker to open at least one of them.","Draft an opening line that states the practical problem the character faces, in their own terms."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 maintaining coherence and consistency across a text","3.1.2 maintaining a consistent point of view"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a village hall being turned into a shelter on the night of a storm. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a stranger who arrives at a village hall to help on the worst night of a storm. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A structural move noticed in reading — a wide view narrowing to one small, particular action and then opening out again — reworked into a new setting, with one first-person-plural narrator held throughout.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Look back at how one of this room's passages moves the reader's focus about, and decide which scene of your own could carry the same movement.","Plan the one small, particular action your middle section narrows to, and the wider view the piece opens back out to at the end.","Draft an opening sentence that establishes who 'we' are and what they are doing, before any weather is described."],"codes":["3.1.2 using information provided by others to write in different forms","3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","3.1.2 maintaining a consistent point of view"],"sol":"We had the hall open by seven, because that is what the hall is for on a night like that one. The long tables came down off their trolleys, the tea urn went on, and somebody found the box of blankets that had been in the cupboard under the stage since the floods before last. By eight there were forty people in a room built for a hundred, and the noise of them was the ordinary noise of a village doing something together: scraped chairs, kettles, three separate conversations about the state of the bridge.\n\nOutside, the wind got on with it. It took the noticeboard off the wall by the door and put it in the car park. It worked at the roof felt above the kitchen until a thin brown line of water came down the inside wall, and then somebody put a bucket under it, and then somebody else set a second bucket beside the first, and after that nobody mentioned the roof again.\n\nThe thing we kept coming back to afterwards was Mrs Ifill's daughter, who was six, going round the tables with a plate of biscuits and asking everybody, very seriously, whether they were all right. She did three circuits of the room. She ran out of biscuits on the second.\n\nBy midnight most people were asleep, or doing a convincing impression of it, in rows of chairs pushed together along the wall. Two of us sat by the door with the urn and the last of the tea, listening to the roof. At about two the wind dropped for long enough that we could hear the buckets."},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a fishing village the morning after its worst storm in a generation. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a child returning to find their street unrecognisable after a storm. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A frame structure moving from a wide view to a single small object and back out again, with the object carrying the piece's emotional weight.","sol":"From the headland the village looked, for one confusing moment, almost unchanged: the same crescent of rooftops, the same grey harbour wall curving out into the same grey sea. It was only down in the streets that the damage declared itself — roof tiles scattered across the war memorial, a car folded round a lamppost that had folded round it, a washing line wrapped three times about a chimney stack with the sheets still pegged to it.\n\nOutside the last cottage before the harbour, half buried in a drift of storm rubbish, lay a child's bicycle. One wheel had been torn off and had come to rest a few feet further on. The frame itself, small and red and entirely ordinary, had been driven at an angle into a flowerbed with nothing left standing in it.\n\nIt was a small thing to fix on, in a village with roofs off and the harbour wall cracked through, but it was the bicycle that made the storm real in a way the wrecked boats had not. Boats are built with the losing of them in mind; that risk is written into them from the keel up. A child's bicycle driven sideways into a flowerbed by nothing but wind belongs to no story anybody agreed to in advance.\n\nAn hour later, back up on the headland, the village had arranged itself into something like its old shape again: tarpaulins going up, a generator coughing into life near the harbour, two men arguing good-naturedly about the best way to right an upturned boat. From that distance it looked almost unchanged for the second time that morning. It did not look that way to anybody who had walked the streets first.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Decide on the single small object your middle paragraph will focus on before you draft your opening.","Plan how the final paragraph returns to the wide view — what has changed in how the reader now sees it, compared with the opening?","Draft the sentence that first introduces the small object, making sure it is as concrete and specific as possible."],"codes":["4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a house the morning a storm warning is issued. Or: Write the opening part of a story about a family divided by how to respond to a storm bearing down on their home. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A single consistent narrator whose attention moves between two other characters without the point of view itself ever shifting to either of them.","sol":"I was small enough that nobody thought to lower their voices round me, which is how I came to hear the whole argument twice: once through the kitchen wall while I was supposed to be finding my wellingtons, and once properly, standing in the doorway with one boot on, when neither of them noticed I had come back in.\n\nDad wanted to board the windows and stay. He said it the way he said most things, flat, as if the decision had been taken somewhere else and he was only passing it on. The house had stood through worse. Running from every forecast that used the word severe was no way to live near a coast.\n\nMum wanted the car packed by six. She did not say it flat. She said it the way she said things when, as far as I could ever tell, she had already decided and wanted him to agree out loud first, which in our house meant she was frightened and did not want to be the only one admitting it.\n\nI watched Dad's hands while he talked, because his hands always told me more than his voice did, and they were doing the thing they did when he was not as certain as he sounded: turning the roll of tape over and over without once using it. Then I watched Mum's face go into the particular stillness it went into just before she said something she had rehearsed on the way down the stairs.\n\nSo I stayed where I was, one welly on and the other held in both hands, and I did not say out loud that this had stopped being an argument about boards some time ago, because I was nine, and there was no version of that sentence that ended well for me.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Decide where your narrator is standing, and how much of the argument someone in that position could plausibly see, hear and understand.","Plan how the narrator's attention moves between the two parents without the perspective itself ever becoming either parent's.","Draft an opening sentence that places your narrator physically where both parents can be observed at once."],"codes":["3.1.2 maintaining a consistent point of view","3.1.2 writing effectively for different purposes and audiences","4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: the moment a lighthouse keeper realises a storm will not pass before dawn. Or: Write the opening part of a story about the last hours before a lighthouse is automated. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"Feeling conveyed entirely through a sequence of physical, sensory changes in one setting, with no emotion named directly.","sol":"The kettle went first. It had been singing to itself on the stove for the better part of an hour, the small domestic noise June usually left running underneath everything else in the room, and somewhere around three she realised she could no longer hear it at all under the wind, which had found new notes in the gallery stairwell above her head.\n\nThen the lamp. The paraffin lamp on the table had been guttering since midnight, throwing her shadow long and short and long again across the logbook she had given up trying to write in, and at some point she caught herself watching the flame instead of the page, with the fixed attention of someone waiting to see something fail.\n\nThe chart came off the wall on its own, though she could have sworn she had pinned it properly. It lay face up on the floor for a while before she picked it up, and she noticed, for what felt like the first time, how many crossings-out there already were along that stretch of coast: how many other keepers, on other nights, had drawn a line through their own careful predictions.\n\nShe made a second pot of tea she did not drink, because making it gave her hands ten minutes of something to do that was not listening. She checked the great lamp's mechanism twice, having already checked it twice before that, and found herself talking to it, quietly, the way you talk to a machine you are relying on to outlast something bigger than both of you.\n\nAt twenty past four the window rattled hard enough to make her put the cup down. She wrote the time in the logbook, and then the wind speed, and then nothing else, and she did not look at the clock again until it was light.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["List three ordinary objects or sounds in the room, and decide how each one changes across the piece.","Plan the order these changes appear in, so the piece builds rather than repeats the same idea.","Draft the first change you will describe, and check that no sentence in it names an emotion."],"codes":["3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively","3.1.2 maintaining coherence and consistency across a text","4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)"]},{"q":"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: two people waiting out a storm in a stalled car. Or: Write the opening part of a story about two siblings whose argument stops mattering when the storm outside gets worse. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"Subtext carried through action rather than statement: the change in the relationship is shown mainly through what the characters do and notice, and never through what they say about it.","sol":"Nadia and Cal had been not speaking to each other for a day and a half, which in the history of their arguments was close to a record, when the first slate came off the roof and hit the greenhouse hard enough that they both came out of their separate rooms at the same moment, for the same reason, without either of them planning to.\n\n'That's the greenhouse,' Cal said, which was obvious, and which was also the first thing either of them had said to the other since the argument about the argument about whose turn it had been to feed the dog.\n\nNadia did not answer. She was already halfway down the stairs, already reaching for the torch that lived by the back door for exactly this kind of night. Cal followed her without deciding to. Outside, the garden had stopped looking like their garden and started looking like somewhere being tested: the trampoline on its side against the fence, the greenhouse glass catching what little light there was in jagged, wrong-shaped pieces.\n\n'Bins,' Nadia said. 'Before they go through next door's car.'\n\nAnd Cal, a day and a half deep into being furious with her about something that now felt, in the actual wind, faintly stupid, went and got the bins.\n\nThey worked without discussing it, hauling in what could be hauled in and leaving what could not, and it was only afterwards, soaked through, standing in the kitchen and not looking at each other, that Nadia noticed neither of them had mentioned the dog. Cal filled the kettle. He took two mugs down instead of one, set them on the counter, and went upstairs to find a dry jumper.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what the characters do, physically, instead of continuing the argument, once the storm forces their hand.","Plan the piece so that no line of dialogue states that the argument no longer matters — the action must show it.","Draft the sentence that interrupts the argument, making clear something outside it now needs an answer first."],"codes":["3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","3.1.2 maintaining coherence and consistency across a text","4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does Paper 1 Question 5 carry in total, and how is that split between content and organisation and technical accuracy?","a":"40 marks in total: 24 for content and organisation (AO5), 16 for technical accuracy (AO6)."},{"q":"What two task forms can Paper 1 Question 5 set?","a":"Either a description suggested by a scenario, prompt or image, or the opening part of a story."},{"q":"About how long is Section B advised to take?","a":"About 45 minutes, with time kept back at the end for checking accuracy."},{"q":"What must a consistent point of view keep constant across the whole piece?","a":"The narrative perspective (who is telling it) and the register, matched to audience and purpose throughout."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Register","back":"How formal or informal a writer's word choice and sentence patterns are, shaped by their audience and purpose.","code":"3.1.2 writing effectively for different purposes and audiences","open":false},{"front":"Cohesion","back":"The sense that a text holds together as one connected piece, built through linked ideas and discourse markers rather than isolated sentences.","code":"3.1.2 maintaining coherence and consistency across a text","open":false},{"front":"Discourse marker (example)","back":"However; meanwhile; by the time.","code":"3.1.2 maintaining coherence and consistency across a text","open":true},{"front":"A structural movement a description can use (example)","back":"Wide to narrow; outside to inside; a return to the opening image at the end.","code":"3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","open":true},{"front":"Personification","back":"Giving a non-human thing human qualities, such as intention or judgement, to make an image feel active rather than static.","code":"3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively","open":false},{"front":"A device for sustained imaginative language (example)","back":"An extended metaphor developed across several sentences; personification returned to and varied; a governing image repeated with a change each time.","code":"3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively","open":true},{"front":"Close third-person (limited) narration","back":"A narrator restricted to what one character sees, knows and feels, never showing another character's private thoughts.","code":"3.1.2 maintaining a consistent point of view","open":false},{"front":"A sign of a point-of-view lapse (example)","back":"The narration suddenly reveals another character's private thoughts; the register shifts to a different character's voice without reason.","code":"3.1.2 maintaining a consistent point of view","open":true},{"front":"A sentence form used for effect (example)","back":"A minor sentence for pace; a short sentence after several long ones; a long cumulative sentence built with a semicolon.","code":"3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","open":true},{"front":"Semicolon (use)","back":"Joins two complete, closely related statements, each of which could stand alone as its own sentence.","code":"3.1.2 selecting vocabulary, grammar, form, and structural and organisational features judiciously","open":false},{"front":"AO5 Content and Organisation","back":"Communicate clearly, effectively and imaginatively, matching tone, style and register to audience and purpose; organise ideas using structural and grammatical features for coherence and cohesion.","code":"4.5 AO5","open":false},{"front":"Upper Level 4 AO5 key words","back":"'Register is convincing and compelling', 'assuredly matched to purpose', 'extensive and ambitious vocabulary with sustained crafting of linguistic devices', 'varied and inventive use of structural features'.","code":"4.5 AO5","open":false},{"front":"AO6 Level 4 key words","back":"Sentence demarcation 'consistently secure and consistently accurate'; 'a full range of appropriate sentence forms for effect'; 'secure control of complex grammatical structures'.","code":"4.2 Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing)","open":false},{"front":"A move worth borrowing from something you have read (example)","back":"A structural shape, such as narrowing from a wide view to one small detail; a governing image sustained and varied across a whole piece; a way of opening a paragraph that points back at the one before it.","code":"3.1.2 using information provided by others to write in different forms","open":true},{"front":"Governing image","back":"A single image chosen early in a piece and developed or returned to across it, rather than replaced by a new image every sentence.","code":"3.1.2 using language imaginatively and creatively","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The passages written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Passage A — 'The Last Ferry' (fiction, 21st-century style, an opening): The forecast had promised nothing worse than showers, so nobody on the harbour wall believed it until the sky over Gull Point turned the colour of a healing bruise. Maya counted eleven boats swinging on their moorings, then stopped counting because the twelfth had already broken free. Gulls that had screamed all morning fell suddenly silent, as if someone had thrown a switch, and the silence was worse than the noise had been. She pulled her hood up and felt the first drops land on the back of her neck like small, deliberate taps.\n\nThe ferry was meant to leave at four. At half past three the harbourmaster came out of his hut, looked once at the horizon, and went back inside without saying a word to anyone, which told Maya everything the tannoy would later confirm in a voice trying too hard to sound calm. Rain arrived not gradually but all at once, a curtain drawn across the bay in a single motion, and within a minute the far headland had vanished as completely as if it had never existed.\n\nShe should have gone home an hour ago. Instead she stood at the rail with her hands white around the cold metal, watching the water climb the harbour steps one at a time, and found that she did not want to look away. Something about the sea deciding, all by itself, that the day was over made her feel smaller than she had felt in years, and, for reasons she could not have explained to her mother, considerably calmer too.","Passage B — 'Low Water' (fiction, 20th-century style, a shift of perspective): For three days the wind came off the sea without once dropping, and the whole village arranged itself around that single fact: shutters latched, boats hauled above the tideline, washing left unpegged on the line because nobody could be bothered pinning it against a losing fight. Smoke from the chimneys flattened sideways before it had risen a foot. Even the chapel bell, which normally rang itself a little in weather like this, hung silent and heavy on its rope.\n\nInside the end cottage, none of that mattered to Thomas Pryce. He sat at the table with his hands flat on either side of an unopened letter, listening to the wind find every gap in the frame it had been finding for forty years, and thought only of the words he had not yet read. His wife had left the letter propped against the teapot that morning and gone to her sister's, which he understood, without her needing to say so, to mean she already knew what it contained.\n\nHe turned it over twice without opening it. Outside, the storm did what storms always did on this stretch of coast: it took whatever was loose and decided, all on its own, where it belonged. Thomas envied it that certainty. When he finally slid his thumb under the flap, the paper tore raggedly, and the sound of it was, for a moment, louder than the wind.","Passage C — 'Harbour Light' (fiction, 21st-century style, an ending): By the time the last of the water drained from the square, the town had the scoured, astonished look of somewhere that had survived something. Amir stood in the doorway of the shop his grandfather had built and watched two boys wade past dragging a door that belonged to nobody they knew, laughing in the reckless way people laugh when the worst has already happened and failed to finish them off.\n\nHe thought he ought to feel devastated, and in an hour, probably, he would. For now there was only this: the sky clearing itself in ragged patches of a blue so ordinary it seemed almost rude, and the harbour light coming on early, out of habit, over a harbour that for once had nothing left to warn anyone about. Someone somewhere was already sweeping. He could hear the scrape of it, steady as a heartbeat, and he understood that this, not the calm before, was the sound the town actually ran on.\n\nHe picked up the broom leaning against his own doorframe and went to join them."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"How the practice tasks in this room are tiered — read this with the questions","items":["Every task in this room carries the same 40 marks, because Paper 1 Question 5 is one task at one tariff: 24 marks for content and organisation and 16 for technical accuracy, with no easier version of it anywhere on the paper. So the difficulty number beside each task is not a mark-scheme tier. It says how much you are being asked to control at once.","Difficulty 1: one governing demand — a single governing image developed across the piece, one structural movement, or one point of view held from first line to last.","Difficulty 2: exam standard — a whole piece with its purpose, its point of view, its sensory order and its paragraph links all under control at the same time.","Difficulty 3: stretch — a frame structure that closes on what it opened with, a narrator whose attention moves between other people without the perspective itself moving, or meaning carried by action alone and never stated.","This ladder is the room's, not the board's: the paper itself gives you one task and expects all of it at once, in about 45 minutes. Work up the ladder now so that, by the time you sit it, a difficulty 3 task reads like a difficulty 1."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Upper Level 4 (Paper 1 Question 5: 'Write a description suggested by this scenario: a hillside reservoir on the morning after three days of rain.')","items":["The reservoir had come up over its own edges in the night and drawn a new shoreline nobody had asked for: fence posts standing in water to their shoulders, a gate opening onto nothing, a stone wall running out into the grey and giving up when it ran out of confidence. The overflow had been going since before dawn, a steady white noise you stopped hearing after a minute and then heard again every time it changed pitch. Everything the hill had been holding for three days was on its way down, and the reservoir was taking all of it, calmly, the way a small room takes guests it never agreed to have.","Why this is Upper Level 4 (22–24 for AO5): Content — \"Register is convincing and compelling for audience\" (controlled and watchful, pitched for peers judging a competition entry, with no narrator intruding); \"Assuredly matched to purpose\" (it describes a state rather than telling a story: no character, no sequence of events, nothing that needs resolving); \"Extensive and ambitious vocabulary with sustained crafting of linguistic devices\" (one personified image is developed right through — the wall \"giving up\", the hill \"holding\" the water, the reservoir \"taking\" guests — rather than three different devices tried once each). Organisation — \"Varied and inventive use of structural features\" (a colon opening a three-item list, then a sentence built on a sound, then a widening out to the whole hillside); \"Writing is compelling, incorporating a range of convincing and complex ideas\" (the new shoreline, the noise you stop hearing, water arriving from somewhere else). For AO6 (13–16): sentence demarcation is secure throughout, the punctuation range runs from the colon to the paired commas of \"calmly\", no two of the three sentences are built the same way — one turns on a colon into a three-item list, one hangs an appositive noun phrase off its main clause, one is compound with an adverbial held apart by commas — and the ambitious vocabulary is spelled accurately. Nothing states a feeling: the mood is carried by the image alone."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same task, visibly weaker: AO5 lower Level 2 and AO6 Level 2)","items":["The reservoir was very full after all the rain. It had gone over the edges and there was water where the grass should be, a fence was in the water and a gate was in the water as well. It was noisy because of the overflow, the noise was loud all the time. It looked strange and diferent to normal. When it rains like that the water has to go somewhere and it went here. The hill had alot of water on it.","What this meets on AO5 (lower Level 2, 7–9): \"Attempts to match register to audience\" (plain, but never inappropriate) and \"Some linked and relevant ideas\" (the sentences stay on the reservoir and follow a sensible order). What it misses: there is no governing image, only a list of things that are wet, so it cannot reach lower Level 3's \"Vocabulary clearly chosen for effect and appropriate use of linguistic devices\", and there is no structural movement for the Organisation column to reward beyond \"Attempts to use structural features\".","Why this is AO6 Level 2 (5–8), descriptor by descriptor: \"Sentence demarcation is mostly secure and sometimes accurate\" — capital letters and full stops are mostly in place, but two sentences are joined by a comma where a full stop is needed (\"...where the grass should be, a fence was in the water\" and \"...because of the overflow, the noise was loud all the time\"). \"Some control of a range of punctuation\" — full stops and commas only: no colon, semicolon, dash or bracket anywhere. \"Attempts a variety of sentence forms\" — simple and compound sentences throughout, with two reaches for a complex sentence, both of them subordinate clauses hung off a compound one (\"...there was water where the grass should be...\" and \"When it rains like that...\"). \"Some accurate spelling of more complex words\" — \"reservoir\" and \"overflow\" are right, \"diferent\" and \"alot\" are wrong. Two changes would lift it a level: put full stops where the two comma splices are, and vary the sentence openings, because five of the six begin with \"It\" or \"The\".","The technical-accuracy difference in one line: the Upper Level 4 paragraph is doing things with punctuation (a colon that introduces, commas that hold a word apart for emphasis), while this one is only avoiding trouble with it — and not quite managing that."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Paper 1 Question 5 (descriptive or narrative writing): the task, the timing and the AO5/AO6 descriptors","items":["Where it sits: Paper 1, Section B, the whole of the Writing section — \"Section B: Writing – descriptive or narrative writing... Writing (40 marks) (25%): 1 extended writing question (24 marks for content, 16 marks for technical accuracy).\"","The task frame: \"You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age.\" Either a description suggested by a scenario, prompt or image, or the opening part of a story, drawn from \"a choice of scenario, written prompt or visual image that is related to the topic of the reading text in section A\".","Timing: 45 minutes advised for the whole of Section B, with time kept back at the end to check technical accuracy, since AO6 alone carries 16 of the 40 marks.","Form — description: no requirement for plot, dialogue or character names; the task rewards a controlled point of view (a narrator noticing the scene) and a deliberate structural movement across the piece.","Form — narrative opening: \"the opening part of a story\", so only a situation needs to be established, not a complete plot; register, purpose and point of view must all be decided in the first few lines and then held.","AO5 Upper Level 4 (22–24 marks): \"Register is convincing and compelling for audience; Assuredly matched to purpose; Extensive and ambitious vocabulary with sustained crafting of linguistic devices\" and \"Varied and inventive use of structural features; Writing is compelling, incorporating a range of convincing and complex ideas; Fluently linked paragraphs with seamlessly integrated discourse markers.\"","AO5 Lower Level 4 (19–21 marks): \"Register is convincingly matched to audience; Convincingly matched to purpose; Extensive vocabulary with conscious crafting of linguistic devices\" and \"Varied and effective structural features; Writing is highly engaging with a range of developed complex ideas; Consistently coherent use of paragraphs with integrated discourse markers.\"","AO5 Upper Level 3 (16–18 marks): \"Register is consistently matched to audience; Consistently matched to purpose; Increasingly sophisticated vocabulary and phrasing, chosen for effect with a range of successful linguistic devices\" and \"Effective use of structural features; Writing is engaging, using a range of clear connected ideas; Coherent paragraphs with integrated discourse markers.\"","AO5 Lower Level 3 (13–15 marks): \"Register is generally matched to audience; Generally matched to purpose; Vocabulary clearly chosen for effect and appropriate use of linguistic devices\" and \"Usually effective use of structural features; Writing is engaging, with a range of connected ideas; Usually coherent paragraphs with range of discourse markers.\"","AO5 Upper Level 2 (10–12 marks): \"Some sustained attempt to match register to audience; Some sustained attempt to match purpose; Conscious use of vocabulary with some use of linguistic devices\" and \"Some use of structural features; Increasing variety of linked and relevant ideas; Some use of paragraphs and some use of discourse markers.\"","AO5 Lower Level 2 (7–9 marks): \"Attempts to match register to audience; Attempts to match purpose; Begins to vary vocabulary with some use of linguistic devices\" and \"Attempts to use structural features; Some linked and relevant ideas; Attempt to write in paragraphs with some discourse markers, not always appropriate.\"","AO5 Upper Level 1 (4–6 marks): \"Simple awareness of register/audience; Simple awareness of purpose; Simple vocabulary; simple linguistic devices\" and \"Evidence of simple structural features; One or two relevant ideas, simply linked; Random paragraph structure.\"","AO5 Lower Level 1 (1–3 marks): \"Occasional sense of audience; Occasional sense of purpose; Simple vocabulary\" and \"Limited or no evidence of structural features; One or two unlinked ideas; No paragraphs.\"","AO5 Level 0: \"Students will not have offered any meaningful writing to assess.\"","AO6 Level 4 (13–16 marks): \"Sentence demarcation is consistently secure and consistently accurate; Wide range of punctuation is used with a high level of accuracy; Uses a full range of appropriate sentence forms for effect; Uses Standard English consistently and appropriately with secure control of complex grammatical structures; High level of accuracy in spelling, including ambitious vocabulary; Extensive and ambitious use of vocabulary.\"","AO6 Level 3 (9–12 marks): \"Sentence demarcation is mostly secure and mostly accurate; Range of punctuation is used, mostly with success; Uses a variety of sentence forms for effect; Mostly uses Standard English appropriately with mostly controlled grammatical structures; Generally accurate spelling, including complex and irregular words; Increasingly sophisticated use of vocabulary.\"","AO6 Level 2 (5–8 marks): \"Sentence demarcation is mostly secure and sometimes accurate; Some control of a range of punctuation; Attempts a variety of sentence forms; Some use of Standard English with some control of agreement; Some accurate spelling of more complex words; Varied use of vocabulary.\"","AO6 Level 1 (1–4 marks): \"Occasional use of sentence demarcation; Some evidence of conscious punctuation; Simple range of sentence forms; Occasional use of Standard English with limited control of agreement; Accurate basic spelling; Simple use of vocabulary.\"","AO6 Level 0: \"Students' spelling, punctuation etc. is sufficiently poor to prevent understanding or meaning.\""]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 1 Question 5: 'You are going to enter a creative writing competition. Your entry will be judged by a panel of people of your own age. Either: Write a description suggested by this scenario: a pier in the hour the storm reaches it. Or: Write the opening part of a story about someone who stays behind when the rest of the street leaves. (24 marks for content and organisation and 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]'","items":["Decoded task, taking the first option: a description, not a narrative — no plot and no dialogue are required, but a controlled point of view is (an unnamed watcher on the front, say) and so is a deliberate structural movement. Audience: a panel of people your own age judging a competition, so the register wants to be crafted and confident rather than chatty or alarmed.","The thread that runs through it — the writing task's equivalent of an evaluation thread, and the thing to decide before drafting a word: a pier is a structure put up on purpose to stand out in the weather, and tonight it is being asked to prove it. Write that sentence at the top of your plan. Every paragraph then has a job: to advance that idea one step, and to be cut if it does not.","Timing: about 45 minutes for the whole of Section B — roughly 5 minutes planning the thread and the shape, about 35 drafting, and the last 5 checking technical accuracy, since AO6 carries 16 of the 40 marks on its own.","Paragraph 1: a wide establishing view of the pier from the land, introducing the governing image that carries the thread (the pier as something built on purpose to be stood on, now standing in something that was not part of the plan).","Paragraph 2: narrow to two or three concrete details out along the deck — a locked kiosk, a bolted gate, a strip of decking lifting — and advance the thread by developing that one image rather than adding new ones.","Paragraph 3: a human presence reacting without becoming a named main character, linked to paragraph 2 with a discourse marker of time ('By the time...', 'Within the hour...'), so the paragraph reads as a consequence of the one before it.","What the last paragraph must do: settle the thread. Return to the opening image and show it altered by what the piece has since noticed — the pier still there, or not, and the watcher's sense of it changed either way. This is what \"fluently linked paragraphs\" and \"a range of convincing and complex ideas\" (Upper Level 4) reward, as against a piece that simply stops when the time does.","Technical accuracy check before you finish: at least one complex sentence, one deliberately short sentence for effect, and one colon or semicolon used correctly — the range AO6 Level 4 asks for (\"Uses a full range of appropriate sentence forms for effect\"; \"Wide range of punctuation is used with a high level of accuracy\")."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T10:35:24Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.84,"notes":"All sixteen objections in the first verdict are genuinely met — the two broken keys (questions 2 and 6) now match their models, the \"quarter of Paper 1\" arithmetic is right, the two untrue statements about the room's own passages in worked examples 3 and 4 are gone, the model paragraph and plan no longer pre-answer question 0, the Level 2 model has been rewritten so its AO6 Level 2 label is argued from real comma splices and misspellings, the difficulty ladder is now declared as the room's own in a new extra, and the plan names its evaluation thread; on a fresh read all 150 quoted spans are verbatim in the printed passages, levels.md or the specification map, every countable claim recounts true, all thirteen tasks carry the correct 40-mark (24 + 16) Paper 1 Question 5 tariff and every grid band is reproduced in the mark scheme's words, so what is left is worked example 1, which promises \"wide, narrow, wide again\" and demonstrates only the first two movements."}}},"paper2":{"id":"AQA-8700","topic":"paper2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room is the whole of Paper 2 as a single sitting: two linked non-fiction passages, one written in a 19th-century voice and one in a 21st-century voice on the same theme, and the paper's full run of five questions set on them, worth 4, 8, 12, 16 and 40 marks. A student who has only ever practised one question type at a time has never rehearsed the order of work the real paper demands: reading two unfamiliar texts cold, moving between AO1, AO2, AO3, AO5 and AO6 inside 105 minutes, and judging their own writing against the same levels an examiner will use. This room drills that whole sequence on one pair of passages.","idea":[{"code":"4.3 Aim of the paper","h":"What Paper 2 is testing","t":"Paper 2 asks how writers present a viewpoint, and then asks the student to present their own. Section A gives two linked sources from different time periods and genres so the student can consider how each writer builds a perspective on the same theme; Section B then asks the student to write their own text to a specified audience, purpose and form on that same theme. Every question in this room, Q1 to Q5, is examined through this one idea: reading how someone else argues a view, then arguing your own. A top-level answer never treats either source as information to be reported: it names the viewpoint the writer holds, shows the words that build it, and, in Section B, commits to a viewpoint of its own and holds it from the first line to the last."},{"code":"4.3 Assessment structure","h":"How the 80 marks are built","t":"Paper 2 is 1 hour 45 minutes and 80 marks: 40 for Section A (Reading) and 40 for Section B (Writing), an even split between reading and writing. Section A is one short-form question (4 marks), two longer-form questions (8 and 12 marks) and one extended question (16 marks). Section B is a single extended writing task worth 24 marks for content and organisation and 16 for technical accuracy. Reading is marked on AO1, AO2 and AO3; AO4 does not apply on this paper. A top-level answer lets that split show in how it spends its words: the short-form question is answered by checking statements against the text and writing no prose at all, the extended reading question by a comparison built idea by idea across both sources, and Section B by a planned piece that is read back before the end."},{"code":"4.3 Content (the reading sources)","h":"What the two sources will be","t":"Section A's two sources are always non-fiction or literary non-fiction, unseen, and always drawn from different time periods: one from the 19th century and one from the 20th or 21st century, so that the paper can ask how the same kind of theme is treated across time. The genres named in the specification are journalism, articles, reports, essays, travel writing, accounts, sketches, letters, diaries, autobiography and biographical passages. This room's Passage A is a 19th-century letter and Passage B is a 21st-century newspaper column, both on attitudes to a young person's growth through sport. A top-level answer uses the gap between the two periods rather than merely noticing it: it reads the older source's manners of address, sentence length and assumptions as evidence of the attitude itself, and sets them beside the newer source's, so that every comparison is anchored in what the words of each time actually do."},{"code":"4.3 Content (the writing task)","h":"How the writing task is set","t":"Section B is a single task, unlike Paper 1's choice of two: it will specify audience, purpose and form, and will use an opinion, statement or scenario to provoke a response, always on the theme introduced in Section A. There is no choice, so the whole of the 45 minutes advised for Section B goes on planning and writing one piece, matched precisely to the named form, and checking it at the end for the technical accuracy that carries 16 of its 40 marks."},{"code":"Paper 2 Question 1","h":"Question 1: choosing the four true statements","t":"Question 1 is the only question on Paper 2 that is point-marked rather than levelled: eight statements are printed about the first part of Source A, and one mark is given for each of the four that are true and correctly shaded, to a maximum of four marks. It tests only the first bullet of AO1, identifying and interpreting explicit and implicit information, and only within the named part of the text, so a true statement drawn from later in the passage earns nothing."},{"code":"Paper 2 Question 2","h":"Question 2: summarising the differences","t":"Question 2 asks for a summary of the differences between two figures or ideas, drawn from both Source A and Source B together, worth 8 marks and levelled on AO1, and the mark scheme notes that it 'assesses both bullet points': interpreting explicit and implicit information and ideas, and selecting and synthesising evidence from different texts. A Level 1 answer only paraphrases each text in turn; a Level 4 answer makes perceptive inferences from both texts and states the differences between them, not two separate retellings."},{"code":"Paper 2 Question 3","h":"Question 3: language to influence a reader","t":"Question 3 asks how one named source only uses language to try to influence a particular reader, worth 12 marks and levelled on AO2: the larger of the two language questions across the two papers, and the biggest single AO2 tariff on the qualification, because it rewards depth on a handful of choices rather than a longer list. The answer must stay inside the one named source, quote precisely, name the method and explain its effect on the reader it is aimed at."},{"code":"Paper 2 Question 4","h":"Question 4: comparing attitudes across both sources","t":"Question 4 is Paper 2's extended reading question, worth 16 marks and levelled on AO3, the only place on the whole GCSE that AO3 is examined. It asks the student to compare the two writers' different attitudes to a theme and the methods they use to convey them, using both sources. A top answer is structured idea by idea, both writers in every paragraph; two separate summaries with no cross-reference sit at Level 1."},{"code":"Paper 2 Question 5","h":"Question 5: writing to present a viewpoint","t":"Question 5 is the whole of Section B, 40 marks for a single piece of writing in a named form for a named audience, given a provocative statement to respond to. It is marked on two grids: AO5 Content and Organisation out of 24, each of its four levels split into an upper and lower range, and AO6 Technical Accuracy out of 16 in four unsplit levels. Holding the named form and audience from the first line to the last, and taking a clear, sustained position on the statement, is what separates a Level 4 answer from a Level 3 one."}],"examples":[{"title":"Locating the four true statements (Question 1, Passage A)","setup":"Read again the first part of Passage A, from 'My dear Father' to 'before you unmake it.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. A) Walter's father has written to say Walter will be taken from the eleven if his examination marks fall short of expectations. B) Walter says his father's letter filled him with no dismay at all. C) Walter denies that his Latin has been indifferent this half. D) Dr. Mowbray has confirmed that Walter's Latin has been indifferent this half. E) Walter says his father has been deceived by him before. F) Walter describes his father's letter as 'your letter of Tuesday'. G) Walter asks his father to unmake what the eleven has made of him. H) Walter says he will not pretend otherwise to a father who has never once been deceived by him.","steps":["Read statement A against the opening sentence: the father has written that Walter is to be 'taken from the eleven directly my examination marks are known, should they fall short' — A matches this exactly, so A is true.","Read statement B against 'I confess it filled me with no small dismay' — Walter states the opposite of B, so B is false.","Read statement C against 'I do not dispute that my Latin has been indifferent this half' — Walter admits it rather than denying it, so C is false.","Read statement D against the same sentence: 'Dr. Mowbray himself has said as much' confirms the Latin was indifferent, so D is true.","Read statement E against 'a father who has never once been deceived by me' — this reverses what the text says, so E is false.","Read statement F against the letter's first clause, 'I have your letter of Tuesday' — the wording is there in the text, so F is true.","Read statement G against 'I would beg you to consider what the eleven has made of me before you unmake it' — Walter is begging his father not to unmake it, so G reverses the request and is false.","Read statement H against 'I will not pretend otherwise to a father who has never once been deceived by me' — the letter says exactly this, so H is true.","Count the statements that survived every check, shade only those, and stop at four: A, D, F and H."],"cues":["Which sentence of the letter names both 'the eleven' and Walter's examination marks?","Does Walter say the letter upset him, or left him unmoved?","Does Walter defend his Latin this half, or admit to it?","What has Dr. Mowbray himself said as much about?","Has Walter's father ever been deceived by him, according to the letter?","What words does Walter use for his father's letter in his very first clause?","In the last sentence, is Walter asking for the unmaking, or asking his father to hold it off?","What does Walter promise not to do, in the sentence about a father never deceived?","Which letters are left once every one of the eight has been checked against the text?"]},{"title":"Summarising the differences between Walter and Ravi (Question 2, 8 marks)","setup":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between Walter and Ravi. From Passage A: Walter has been told he is to be 'taken from the eleven directly my examination marks are known'; he writes that 'It is the field, Father, and not the form-room, that has taught me to bear a blow without whimpering'; and he ends by asking his father to 'let me keep my place until the examination is over'. From Passage B: Ravi 'handed in his rugby boots on a wet Tuesday in October', and his medals come from races that, 'as far as I can tell, nobody but Ravi and his running app were watching him run.'","steps":["State the first difference as one statement covering both texts: Walter belongs to a team that judges him publicly, while Ravi trains and races largely alone.","Support it with a detail from each text: Walter's fear of being 'taken from the eleven' against Ravi's races that 'nobody but Ravi and his running app were watching him run.'","Infer what this shows, rather than just restating it: Walter needs the eleven's good opinion of him; Ravi no longer needs an audience at all.","State a second difference: Walter must persuade an adult authority to let him carry on, while Ravi has already acted on his own decision without asking permission.","Support it: Walter's plea 'let me keep my place until the examination is over' against the fact that Ravi simply 'handed in his rugby boots' and began running.","Check the summary has drawn from both Passages in every statement, not summarised each text in turn, and judge which difference is more perceptive to end on."],"cues":["Does Walter's sense of achievement depend on other people watching, or on something else?","Which two details, one from each passage, could sit inside the same sentence about being watched?","What does Ravi's use of the app suggest about who he wants to notice his times?","Who is asking permission in each passage, and who has already acted?","What single sentence links a detail from Passage A with a detail from Passage B, rather than reporting them separately?","Which difference between the two boys is the more perceptive one to end the summary on?"]},{"title":"Analysing Walter's language to influence his father (Question 3, 12 marks, Passage A only)","setup":"You now need to refer only to Passage A, the letter by Walter written to his father. How does Walter use language to try to influence his father? Focus on: 'I do not dispute that my Latin has been indifferent this half... I will not pretend otherwise to a father who has never once been deceived by me. But I would beg you to consider what the eleven has made of me before you unmake it.'","steps":["Locate the concession: Walter admits his Latin 'has been indifferent this half' before asking for anything, which is a deliberate move, not an accident.","Name the method: this is a rhetorical concession, admitting a weak point early so the father cannot use it against him later in the letter.","Quote the appeal that follows it: 'a father who has never once been deceived by me' — direct address plus an appeal to his own honesty as a witness.","Explain the effect on the reader: it positions Walter as trustworthy exactly at the moment he is about to ask for something, making the father more likely to believe what comes next.","Track the metaphor 'what the eleven has made of me before you unmake it': the paired opposites 'made' and 'unmake' cast his father's decision as an act of destruction, not discipline.","Judge the cumulative effect: each choice works to convert the father from a judge weighing marks into a witness weighing character, which is the whole of Walter's argument."],"cues":["Why would a writer admit a weakness before making a request, rather than after?","What is the name for admitting a weak point before your reader can raise it themselves?","What is Walter asking the reader to believe about him, at the exact moment he admits the Latin was poor?","What do the words 'made' and 'unmake' have in common, and what does pairing them suggest about the father's decision?","What is Walter's letter trying to change in his father's mind by its final sentence?","Standing back from the individual choices, what single change in the father's judgement is Walter working towards?"]},{"title":"Comparing attitudes to sport and growing up (Question 4, 16 marks, Passage A and Passage B)","setup":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to organised sport and a young person's growth into adulthood. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts.","steps":["Choose one idea both passages touch, rather than working through each text separately: what a young person needs from sport in order to grow up.","State Walter's attitude and its method: he believes collective discipline builds character, conveyed through the formal, pleading register of a letter to an authority figure — 'a gentleman is made on the field before ever he is made at a desk'.","State the columnist's attitude and its method in the same paragraph: the writer is uncertain whether solitary achievement can teach the same lessons, conveyed through the reflective, self-questioning register of a newspaper column — 'I am no longer sure which of us was right about what a boy needs from a game.'","Compare the forms directly: Walter's private letter must persuade one reader who holds power over him; the columnist's public article can afford to stay undecided in front of many readers who hold no power over the writer at all.","Add a second point of comparison: Walter's world measures success publicly ('the eleven'), while Ravi's world measures it privately ('a small green tick'), and note what each writer's chosen detail reveals about a changed idea of achievement.","Judge which writer's uncertainty or certainty is more perceptively conveyed, and check every paragraph has referred to both texts, not summarised one after the other."],"cues":["What single idea appears in both passages, that a paragraph could open on?","What is each writer's attitude, stated in one sentence, and what quotation proves it?","Whose writing is trying to change someone's mind, and whose is reflecting after the decision is already made?","How does the form each writer chooses, letter or column, suit the power each writer has over their reader?","What does each writer's chosen image of success, 'the eleven' or 'a small green tick', say about how each generation measures achievement?","Has every paragraph referred to both texts together, or drifted into summarising one text at a time?"]},{"title":"Planning the viewpoint article (Question 5, 40 marks)","setup":"Plan this Section B task, and stop at the plan: the work here is the article's shape and its evidence, not the finished article. The task is: 'Team sport teaches lessons in life that no individual activity can match.' Write an article for a broadsheet newspaper in which you explain your point of view on this statement. (24 marks for content and organisation / 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","steps":["Decode the task before writing a word: the form is a broadsheet newspaper article, the audience is adult readers who expect an argued case rather than a rant, and the purpose is to explain a point of view on the claim that no individual activity can match team sport.","Settle the position in one sentence and keep it: team sport does teach one thing a solitary activity cannot, which is how to be of use to other people when they are struggling, but the word 'no' in the statement overreaches, so the article agrees in part and says precisely which part.","Choose the opening: a title in question form, 'Do We Still Need a Team to Grow Up?', then a first paragraph that puts one named observation in front of the reader — a friend whose son swapped Saturday rugby for solo running — before the position is stated outright in the second sentence.","Give the other side its own paragraph, second rather than last: the private satisfaction of a personal best that belongs to nobody else, conceded plainly, so that the reader trusts the case that follows it.","Build the middle on concrete lessons instead of general praise: one paragraph on losing in front of other people and shaking hands afterwards, one on carrying a colleague through a bad week, each written as something adult life actually asks for, and a third on club runs and relay legs, which shows the statement's 'no' is too absolute.","Decide the last paragraph now, not when the time runs out: it returns to the statement's claim, narrows it to the single lesson the article has defended, and ends on one sharp line rather than a new idea."],"cues":["Which three things does the task fix that the writer may not change, and what exactly is the claim being argued about?","Must the statement be swallowed whole or rejected outright, or can a position be narrower than either?","What should a broadsheet reader meet in the first two sentences: the argument, or a person?","Where in an argument does a concession do most work — before the case is built, or after it?","What turns 'team sport builds character' from an assertion into an argument a reader can test?","What must the final paragraph return to, and what must it not introduce?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In Question 1, must a true statement be drawn from anywhere in Source A, or only from the named part?","a":"Only from the named part of the text; a true statement drawn from elsewhere earns nothing."},{"q":"Does a Level 4 answer to the summary question retell each source in turn, or state the differences between them?","a":"It states the differences between them, drawing an inference from both texts in each statement, not retelling either text alone."},{"q":"On the 12-mark language question, should the answer discuss both sources or only the one named in the question?","a":"Only the one named source; the question is deliberately set on a single text."},{"q":"What structure earns the higher levels on the 16-mark comparison question: text by text, or idea by idea?","a":"Idea by idea, with both writers discussed together in every paragraph."}]},"room":{"facts":["Paper 2 is 1 hour 45 minutes and 80 marks: 40 for Section A Reading (Q1 4 marks, Q2 8 marks, Q3 12 marks, Q4 16 marks) and 40 for Section B Writing (Q5, 24 marks AO5 plus 16 marks AO6).","The specimen paper advises about 15 minutes reading both Sources and all five questions, about 45 minutes on Section A, and about 45 minutes on Section B; the order of work is to read everything first, then answer Q1 to Q4 in order, then plan and write Q5, leaving time at the end to check it.","Eight of the ten questions across both papers are marked by levels of response, worked as a ladder from the lowest level upward until the answer's descriptor is matched, not by best fit; only the two Question 1s are point-marked, and Paper 2's Question 1 gives one mark for each of the four TRUE statements correctly shaded.","Section A of Paper 2 assesses AO1 (Q1 and Q2), AO2 (Q3) and AO3 (Q4); AO4, evaluation, does not apply on this paper, unlike Paper 1.","Section B is marked on two separate grids: AO5 Content and Organisation out of 24, with each of its four levels split into an upper and a lower range, and AO6 Technical Accuracy out of 16 in four unsplit levels; AO6 carries 20% of the whole GCSE across both papers."],"questions":[{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage A, from 'My dear Father' to 'before you unmake it.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A) Walter's father has written to say Walter will be taken from the eleven if his examination marks fall short of expectations. B) Walter says his father's letter filled him with no dismay at all. C) Walter denies that his Latin has been indifferent this half. D) Dr. Mowbray has confirmed that Walter's Latin has been indifferent this half. E) Walter says his father has been deceived by him before. F) Walter describes his father's letter as 'your letter of Tuesday'. G) Walter asks his father to unmake what the eleven has made of him. H) Walter says he will not pretend otherwise to a father who has never once been deceived by him. [4 marks]","a":"A, D, F and H are true.","sol":"A is true: the father has written that Walter is to be 'taken from the eleven directly my examination marks are known, should they fall short of your expectations.' B is false: Walter says the letter 'filled me with no small dismay,' the opposite of no dismay. C is false: Walter admits 'I do not dispute that my Latin has been indifferent this half,' rather than denying it. D is true: 'Dr. Mowbray himself has said as much,' confirming the indifferent Latin. E is false: Walter calls his father 'a father who has never once been deceived by me,' the reverse of E. F is true: Walter opens 'I have your letter of Tuesday'. G is false: Walter asks his father to consider what the eleven 'has made of me before you unmake it,' not to unmake it. H is true: Walter writes 'I will not pretend otherwise to a father who has never once been deceived by me.'","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Work through the statements in order and check each one only against the first part of the letter, not the whole passage.","For each statement, find the exact sentence it is claiming to describe, and read that whole sentence before deciding.","Match the wording and not the general idea: a statement can fail on a single word that reverses the meaning of the sentence it is drawn from."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 1"]},{"q":"Read again the second part of Passage A, from 'When I came to this school' to 'come to believe him entirely.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A) Walter came to this school two years before writing the letter. B) Walter describes himself, on arrival, as a poor puny creature given to sulking in corners. C) Walter says it is the form-room, not the field, that has taught him to bear a blow without whimpering. D) Walter says the field has taught him to think first of himself and only second of his fellows. E) Mr. Aldous tells new boys that a gentleman is made at a desk before ever he is made on the field. F) Walter says he has come to believe Mr. Aldous's opinion within the last year. G) Walter says he arrived at the school already confident and unafraid of hurt. H) Walter credits the field, not the classroom, with teaching him to think of his fellows before himself. [4 marks]","a":"A, B, F and H are true.","sol":"A is true: 'I came to this school two years since'. B is true: Walter calls his earlier self 'a poor puny creature, given to sulking in corners'. C is false: Walter says 'It is the field, Father, and not the form-room' that has taught him this, the reverse of C. D is false: Walter says the field taught him 'to think first of my fellows and only second of myself,' the reverse order to D. E is false: Aldous says 'a gentleman is made on the field before ever he is made at a desk,' the reverse of E. F is true: Walter has, 'this twelvemonth, come to believe him entirely,' and a twelvemonth is a year. G is false: Walter describes himself as 'nursing every small hurt as though it were a mortal wound,' the opposite of confident and unafraid. H is true, for the same reason C is false: the field, not the classroom, taught him to put his fellows first.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Work through the statements in order and check each one only against this second part of the letter.","Where the letter names two things together, find the sentence that names them and note the order it puts them in.","Match the wording and not the general idea: a statement that swaps an order the text gives, or reverses which of two things is favoured, is false."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 1"]},{"q":"Read again the first part of Passage B, from 'My son handed in his rugby boots' to 'watching him run.' Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A) Ravi handed in his rugby boots on a wet Tuesday in October. B) Ravi has talked about rugby often since giving it up. C) Ravi is fourteen years old. D) For six years, Ravi's Saturday mornings involved a minibus and a muddy touchline. E) The writer describes their own shouted encouragement on the touchline as always exactly right. F) Ravi's Saturdays now begin with a route mapped out the night before. G) The writer says a large crowd watches Ravi run his races. H) Ravi has won a small collection of medals from his races. [4 marks]","a":"A, D, F and H are true.","sol":"A is true: 'My son handed in his rugby boots on a wet Tuesday in October'. B is false: the text says Ravi 'has not mentioned the sport since.' C is false: 'Ravi is fifteen,' not fourteen. D is true: 'for six years Saturday mornings meant a minibus, a muddy touchline'. E is false: the writer calls that shouting 'slightly wrong,' not always exactly right. F is true: Ravi's Saturdays now begin with 'a route mapped out the night before'. G is false: the text says 'nobody but Ravi and his running app were watching him run.' H is true: Ravi has 'a small collection of medals from races'.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Work through the statements in order and check each one only against this first part of Passage B.","For each statement, find the exact sentence it is claiming to describe, and read that whole sentence before deciding.","Match the wording and not the general idea: check every part of a statement against its sentence, since the rest of a statement can be accurate while one detail in it is not."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 1"]},{"q":"Read again the final part of Passage B, from 'But I have watched him' to the end. Choose four statements below which are TRUE. Shade the boxes of the ones that you think are true. Choose a maximum of four statements. A) Ravi has shaved four minutes off a five-kilometre time this spring. B) Nobody carried Ravi off the field on their shoulders for his personal best. C) The app tells Ravi about his personal best with a small green tick. D) The writer says Ravi needs an eleven to tell him he has done well. E) The writer ends by saying they are now certain which of them was right about what a boy needs from a game. F) The numbers on the screen tell Ravi he has done well loudly and publicly. G) The writer describes the satisfaction on Ravi's face as particular and private. H) Ravi asks the app about his times only once. [4 marks]","a":"A, B, C and G are true.","sol":"A is true: 'I have watched him shave four minutes off a five-kilometre time this spring'. B is true: 'Nobody carried him off the field on their shoulders for it.' C is true: the app tells him, 'in a small green tick, that this is a personal best.' D is false: the text says Ravi 'does not need an eleven to tell him he has done well,' the reverse of D. E is false: the passage ends 'I am no longer sure which of us was right about what a boy needs from a game,' which is the reverse of certainty. F is false: the numbers 'do that quietly enough,' not loudly and publicly. G is true: the writer names 'the particular, private satisfaction on his face'. H is false: the numbers tell him well 'every single time he asks them to,' which is repeatedly, not once.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Work through the statements in order and check each one only against this final part of Passage B.","For each statement, find the exact sentence it is claiming to describe, and read that whole sentence before deciding.","Match the wording and not the general idea: hold each statement beside the sentence it is drawn from and check it clause by clause, because one altered word makes the whole statement false."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 1"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between Walter and Ravi. [8 marks]","a":"Walter belongs to a team that judges him publicly and must persuade an authority to let him stay; Ravi trains largely alone and has already acted on his own decision without needing permission.","sol":"Walter's sense of achievement depends on the eleven and on being watched: he fears being 'taken from the eleven' and must beg his father to 'let me keep my place until the examination is over,' showing he needs an authority's permission and a team's good opinion. Ravi's achievement, by contrast, needs no audience at all: his races are ones where 'nobody but Ravi and his running app were watching him run,' and he simply 'handed in his rugby boots' and began running without asking anyone. Where Walter measures success by his place in a public institution, Ravi measures it privately, through 'a small green tick' on a screen: 'Nobody carried him off the field on their shoulders for it. Nobody had to.' The two boys are separated not only by a century but by whether they need other people to confirm they have done well.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Think about who is being judged by other people in each passage, and who is judging only themself.","Find one detail from Passage A and one from Passage B that could sit in the same sentence about needing, or not needing, permission.","Write in linked statements that combine a detail from each passage, not two separate summaries of each text."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 2"]},{"q":"You need to refer to Passage A and Passage B for this question. Use details from both Passages. Write a summary of the differences between how Walter's father and Ravi's parent respond to their son's choice of sport. [8 marks]","a":"Walter's father holds direct authority and threatens to remove Walter from the eleven; Ravi's parent has already lost that power and can only watch the decision and reconsider a long-held view.","sol":"Walter's father acts from a position of clear authority: Walter reports his father's decision in his own words, that he is to be 'taken from the eleven directly my examination marks are known, should they fall short,' a decision his father alone can make and enforce. Ravi's parent, by contrast, has no such power over Ravi's choice: the column admits 'I worried, at first, that he was giving something up rather than choosing something new,' but the choice itself was already made and acted on before the parent comments on it at all. Walter's father is positioned to judge and decide; Ravi's parent is positioned only to watch, worry and reconsider, ending 'I am no longer sure which of us was right about what a boy needs from a game.' The two responses show a shift from a parent who controls a son's place in an institution to a parent who can only react to a decision a son has made for himself.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which parent has the power to enforce a decision, and which parent can only watch one being made.","Find a detail in Passage A that shows authority being threatened, and a detail in Passage B that shows a parent's opinion changing.","Write statements that combine a detail from each passage, showing how the idea of a parent's power itself differs between them."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 2"]},{"q":"You now need to refer only to Passage A, the letter by Walter written to his father. How does Walter use language to try to influence his father? [12 marks]","a":"Direct address and an early concession build trust; the paired verbs of 'what the eleven has made of me before you unmake it' cast the father's decision as destruction, and the formal closing appeal to duty presses the request home.","sol":"Walter opens with a rhetorical concession, admitting that 'my Latin has been indifferent this half' before he has asked his father for anything at all. The concession is a persuasive technique rather than an accident of honesty: by naming the weakness in his own words, Walter forecloses the obvious objection and repositions himself as an honest witness rather than a boy pleading a weak case. He builds on this immediately with direct address and an appeal to his father's own experience of him, 'a father who has never once been deceived by me,' which makes the request that follows harder to refuse, since refusing it would mean doubting a son's honesty for the first time. The metaphor in 'what the eleven has made of me before you unmake it' pairs the opposite verbs 'made' and 'unmake', casting his father's possible decision as an act of destruction against something carefully built, which makes refusal sound far more drastic than a simple withdrawal from a team. Walter's account of his earlier self as 'a poor puny creature, given to sulking in corners' uses hyperbole via the simile 'as though it were a mortal wound', which swells 'every small hurt' into something fatal and makes the distance he has travelled since seem as large as possible. The formal, restrained register throughout, closing with 'I remain, Father, in all things, Your affectionate and dutiful son,' reminds his father of the filial duty a well-brought-up son owes, quietly implying that a father who values duty should honour a son who asks for it so properly. Together these choices work to shift his father from judging him on 'one week's marks' to judging him on 'the whole of what I have become,' which is exactly the shift Walter needs.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the order Walter puts things in: what does he admit before he asks for anything?","Notice the pair of opposite verbs Walter uses about what the eleven has done to him, and what his father might do.","Consider how the letter's closing words remind the reader of the relationship between the two men, not just the argument."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 3"]},{"q":"You now need to refer only to Passage B, the newspaper column written by Ravi's parent. How does the writer use language to try to influence the reader's view of Ravi's choice to run alone? [12 marks]","a":"Short, blunt sentences and a contrast with public celebration set private, quiet satisfaction against the traditional idea that achievement needs an audience, while an honest, self-questioning register makes the writer's changed view feel earned rather than asserted.","sol":"The writer builds the case for taking Ravi's choice seriously by contrasting public and private reward directly: 'Nobody carried him off the field on their shoulders for it. Nobody had to.' The anaphora on 'Nobody', and the blunt three-word second sentence, dismiss the need for public celebration deliberately rather than sadly, inviting the reader to accept that Ravi's private reward is sufficient. Personification in 'the app tells him, kindly, in a small green tick, that this is a personal best' gives the technology an almost parental voice, the adverb 'kindly' standing in for the praise a team or a crowd might once have given, which makes the modern, solitary reward feel warm rather than cold. The phrase 'the particular, private satisfaction on his face' names exactly what kind of achievement this is, two precise adjectives distinguishing it from a shared, public one and justifying it on its own terms. The writer's openness about a change of mind — 'I worried, at first' near the start, and at the close, 'I am no longer sure which of us was right about what a boy needs from a game.' — works as a persuasive method in itself: by refusing to overstate the case, the writer makes the reader far more willing to accept that Ravi's solitary achievement may be as valuable as the team sport the column still believes in.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Look at how the writer describes what did not happen to Ravi, before describing what did.","Notice the adverb used to describe how the app 'tells' Ravi about his personal best.","Consider what effect the writer's own uncertainty, admitted openly, might have on how far a reader trusts the argument."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 3"]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to organised sport and a young person's growth into adulthood. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts. [16 marks]","a":"Walter believes collective, supervised sport builds character and argues this through a formal, pleading letter; the columnist is uncertain whether solitary achievement can teach the same lessons, and conveys this through a reflective, self-questioning newspaper column.","sol":"Walter's attitude is one of certainty in collective discipline: he reports Mr. Aldous's maxim that 'a gentleman is made on the field before ever he is made at a desk,' and states plainly that he has 'come to believe him entirely'; his method, the private letter, is chosen precisely because it must persuade one powerful reader, his father, so its register stays formal and its argument builds carefully towards a direct request. The columnist's attitude, by contrast, is one of unresolved doubt, and the column's last line says so outright: 'I am no longer sure which of us was right about what a boy needs from a game.' That method, the public newspaper column, can afford such openness, because the writer is not trying to change one reader's decision but reflecting for many readers who hold no power over the writer at all. A second comparison lies in how each writer measures achievement: Walter's world rewards him publicly, through 'the eleven,' while Ravi's world rewards him privately, through 'a small green tick,' and this contrast in imagery conveys a wider shift between a generation that measured growing up by belonging to a group and one that measures it by private, individual data. Both writers let the shape of the chosen form reinforce the attitude: Walter's letter moves in one direction only, towards a request, while the column stops without settling anything, its final declarative admitting uncertainty rather than concluding, so that the form performs the doubt it describes. Read together, the two texts show that an attitude to sport and growing up is inseparable from the form each writer chooses to express it in.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Choose one idea that appears in both passages to build a paragraph around, rather than writing about each text in turn.","Think about what each writer's chosen form, letter or column, lets them do that the other form does not.","Find one image of success in each passage that you could place side by side in the same sentence."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 4"]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to how a young person's achievement should be recognised. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts. [16 marks]","a":"Walter's attitude values public, collective recognition, conveyed through his fear of losing 'the eleven'; the columnist's attitude values private, individual recognition, conveyed through the image of 'a small green tick' on a screen.","sol":"Walter conveys an attitude that achievement must be recognised publicly, through a place within a group: his whole argument rests on the fear of being 'taken from the eleven,' and his plea to 'keep my place until the examination is over' shows that, for him, recognition and belonging are the same thing. The columnist conveys the opposite attitude, that recognition can be private and still be enough, describing 'the particular, private satisfaction on his face when the app tells him, kindly, in a small green tick, that this is a personal best,' language that makes a screen's quiet confirmation sound as valid as any public praise. The methods each writer uses reflect this difference: Walter appeals to an authority figure, Mr. Aldous, as a witness whose public opinion matters, 'a gentleman is made on the field before ever he is made at a desk,' while the columnist deliberately removes any human witness from Ravi's achievement: 'Nobody carried him off the field on their shoulders for it. Nobody had to.' The short, definite sentence 'Nobody had to' works almost as a rebuttal of Walter's whole assumption, suggesting that recognition without an audience can still be complete. Comparing the two, Walter's attitude belongs to a world where a person's worth is confirmed from outside; the columnist's belongs to one where it can be confirmed from within, and the two texts together trace exactly that historical shift.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about who, or what, confirms that each young person has done well in each passage.","Find the moment in each passage where a public audience is either needed, or deliberately said not to be needed.","Consider how the short sentence 'Nobody had to' in Passage B could be read as answering Walter's assumption in Passage A."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 4"]},{"q":"For this question, you need to refer to the whole of Passage A, together with Passage B. Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to a parent's role in a young person's independence. In your answer, you could: compare their different attitudes; compare the methods they use to convey their attitudes; support your ideas with references to both texts. [16 marks]","a":"Walter conveys a father who holds and exercises authority over his choices; the columnist conveys a parent whose authority has already given way to a son's own decision, leaving that parent only to watch.","sol":"Walter conveys his father as a figure who still actively decides his son's path: the whole letter is built to change one decision, 'taken from the eleven directly my examination marks are known,' which only his father has the power to make or unmake. Walter's method, the formal letter of petition, respects that authority even as it argues against its use, closing 'I remain, Father, in all things, Your affectionate and dutiful son'. The columnist conveys a very different balance of power: the son has already 'handed in his rugby boots' and 'has not mentioned the sport since', so the parent's role is reduced to commenting after the fact, admitting 'I worried, at first' rather than intervening. That method, the reflective newspaper column, suits the reduced authority: rather than arguing for a decision, as Walter's letter does, it can only narrate one already made and weigh it honestly, ending in open uncertainty: 'I am no longer sure which of us was right about what a boy needs from a game.' The comparison suggests a historical shift in a parent's role, from a father who is petitioned because he still decides, to a parent who can only watch, worry and eventually accept a young person's own authority over their own choices.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about who makes the final decision in each passage: the parent, or the young person.","Find the moment in each passage where a parent's power is either exercised directly, or shown to have already passed to the child.","Consider how each writer's chosen form, a petition or a reflection, matches how much power that parent actually holds."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 4"]},{"q":"'Team sport teaches lessons in life that no individual activity can match.' Write an article for a broadsheet newspaper in which you explain your point of view on this statement. (24 marks for content and organisation / 16 marks for technical accuracy) [40 marks]","a":"A clear, sustained point of view, held in a broadsheet article's register throughout, structured with a title, an opening that states the position, developed middle paragraphs that concede and then build the case, and a conclusion that returns to the statement.","sol":"Do We Still Need a Team to Grow Up?\n\nA friend of mine told me last month that her son had swapped Saturday rugby for solo running, and confessed she no longer knew whether to be proud or quietly concerned. The question deserves an honest answer, and mine is a narrow one: team sport does teach a lesson that solitary activity struggles to match, though the claim that no individual activity can match it is far too broad to survive a moment's thought.\n\nLet us grant the other side its best argument first, because it is a good one. A runner who takes a minute off her own best owes that improvement to nobody but herself. She has had to get up on cold mornings without a coach shouting at her, and accept a bad week with nobody else to blame. There is a private satisfaction in a number on a screen that no committee awards and no selector can withdraw, and anyone who has felt it will tell you it is not a lesser thing.\n\nBut a team asks for something a stopwatch never will. It asks a player to be of use to somebody whose afternoon is going far worse than their own: to pass when passing is the duller option, and to say something decent in a changing room after a defeat. It asks that player, too, to lose in front of other people and shake hands anyway. These are not small lessons. They are the ones adult life demands most often, in offices and hospitals and families, and no personal best has ever taught anybody how to carry a colleague through a bad fortnight.\n\nCritics will call this sentimental about muddy touchlines and minibuses, and perhaps it is. But sentimentality is not the same as error. A generation trained entirely on private targets risks losing the instinct to notice when somebody else is struggling, simply because nothing in the training has ever required it.\n\nThere is a middle path, too, and the statement ignores it. Plenty of individual sports still gather their competitors: the club run, the relay leg, the crowded start line where strangers wish each other luck. My friend's son, running alone on a Tuesday morning, still lines up beside two hundred strangers on a Sunday. The choice is rarely as stark as that word 'no' suggests.\n\nSo the answer is not to rank one against the other, but to insist that every young person learns, at least once, what it is to depend on a team and be depended upon. Everything else about growing up can change. That lesson has not gone out of date, and it cannot be downloaded.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Decide your point of view on the statement before you begin drafting, and hold it through every paragraph.","Plan a broadsheet register from the first line: a considered opening, developed middle paragraphs, and a conclusion that returns to the statement.","Leave time at the end to check spelling, punctuation and sentence variety, since technical accuracy is 16 of the 40 marks."],"codes":["Paper 2 Question 5"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In Passage A, what single word does Walter pair with 'made' to describe what his father's decision could do to him?","a":"'Unmake' — the letter reads 'what the eleven has made of me before you unmake it.'"},{"q":"What contemporary detail in Passage B tells Ravi he has run a personal best?","a":"The running app tells him 'in a small green tick, that this is a personal best.'"},{"q":"When structuring an answer to the comparison question, should a paragraph deal with one text at a time, or one idea across both texts?","a":"One idea across both texts, with both writers discussed together in the same paragraph."},{"q":"In a Level 4 summary of differences, does each statement draw on one Passage or both?","a":"Both Passages, combined into one statement of difference, not two separate summaries."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Semantic field","back":"A group of words linked by a shared area of meaning, used to build one impression across a passage; example: 'field', 'eleven', 'desk' building a field of school sport and duty.","code":"Paper 2 Question 3","open":false},{"front":"Hyperbole","back":"Deliberate exaggeration for effect, often carried by a simile; example: 'nursing every small hurt as though it were a mortal wound' swells a minor hurt into a fatal one.","code":"Paper 2 Question 3","open":false},{"front":"Register","back":"How formal or informal a writer's word choice and sentence patterns are, shaped by their audience and purpose.","code":"4.3 Content (the reading sources)","open":false},{"front":"Direct address","back":"A writer speaking straight to a named reader; example: 'Father' in Walter's letter.","code":"Paper 2 Question 3","open":false},{"front":"Rhetorical concession","back":"Admitting a weak point before the reader can raise it, to appear honest and disarm objection; example: Walter admitting his Latin was 'indifferent' before asking his father anything.","code":"Paper 2 Question 3","open":false},{"front":"Personification","back":"Giving a non-human thing human qualities or actions; example: 'the app tells him, kindly' gives the app a human, gentle voice.","code":"Paper 2 Question 3","open":false},{"front":"Public-sphere text (example)","back":"A newspaper article; a magazine column; a speech.","code":"4.3 Content (the reading sources)","open":true},{"front":"Private-sphere text (example)","back":"A letter; a diary entry; a personal account.","code":"4.3 Content (the reading sources)","open":true},{"front":"Paper 2 Question 1 stem and marks","back":"'Choose four statements below which are TRUE', 4 marks, point-marked not levelled, one mark per correctly shaded true statement.","code":"Paper 2 Question 1","open":false},{"front":"Paper 2 Question 2 stem and marks","back":"'Write a summary of the differences between...', 8 marks, levelled on AO1.","code":"Paper 2 Question 2","open":false},{"front":"Paper 2 Question 3 stem and marks","back":"'How does [the writer] use language to try to influence...?', 12 marks, levelled on AO2, one named source only.","code":"Paper 2 Question 3","open":false},{"front":"Paper 2 Question 4 stem and marks","back":"'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to...', 16 marks, levelled on AO3.","code":"Paper 2 Question 4","open":false},{"front":"Paper 2 Question 5 marks","back":"40 marks: 24 for content and organisation (AO5), 16 for technical accuracy (AO6).","code":"Paper 2 Question 5","open":false},{"front":"Level 4 comparison key words","back":"Compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way; Analyses how writers' methods are used; Selects a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts; Shows a detailed understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.","code":"Paper 2 Question 4","open":false},{"front":"Level 1 summary warning","back":"'Offers paraphrase rather than inference': restating a text instead of drawing a conclusion from it.","code":"Paper 2 Question 2","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The passages written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Passage A — 'Elmhurst School, the ninth of June, 1863' (non-fiction, 19th-century voice, a letter): Elmhurst School, the ninth of June, 1863\n\nMy dear Father, I have your letter of Tuesday, and I confess it filled me with no small dismay, for you write that I am to be taken from the eleven directly my examination marks are known, should they fall short of your expectations. I do not dispute that my Latin has been indifferent this half; Dr. Mowbray himself has said as much, and I will not pretend otherwise to a father who has never once been deceived by me. But I would beg you to consider what the eleven has made of me before you unmake it.\n\nWhen I came to this school two years since I was, as you well remember, a poor puny creature, given to sulking in corners and nursing every small hurt as though it were a mortal wound. It is the field, Father, and not the form-room, that has taught me to bear a blow without whimpering and to think first of my fellows and only second of myself. Mr. Aldous tells the new boys that a gentleman is made on the field before ever he is made at a desk, and I have, this twelvemonth, come to believe him entirely.\n\nI ask only this: let me keep my place until the examination is over, and judge me then by the whole of what I have become, and not by one week's marks alone. I remain, Father, in all things, Your affectionate and dutiful son, Walter","Passage B — 'Going It Alone' (literary non-fiction, 21st-century voice, a newspaper column): My son handed in his rugby boots on a wet Tuesday in October and has not mentioned the sport since. Ravi is fifteen, and for six years Saturday mornings meant a minibus, a muddy touchline, and me shouting something enthusiastic and slightly wrong about ‘getting stuck in.’ Now his Saturdays begin with a phone propped against the kettle, a route mapped out the night before, and a small collection of medals from races that, as far as I can tell, nobody but Ravi and his running app were watching him run.\n\nI worried, at first, that he was giving something up rather than choosing something new. A team teaches you things a stopwatch cannot: how to lose in front of other people, and how to be useful to somebody who is having a worse day than you are. I still believe that, and I have said so, possibly too often, over dinner.\n\nBut I have watched him shave four minutes off a five-kilometre time this spring, and I have seen the particular, private satisfaction on his face when the app tells him, kindly, in a small green tick, that this is a personal best. Nobody carried him off the field on their shoulders for it. Nobody had to. He does not need an eleven to tell him he has done well; the numbers on the screen do that quietly enough, every single time he asks them to.\n\nI am no longer sure which of us was right about what a boy needs from a game."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Paper 2 Question 3: 'How does Walter use language to try to influence his father?', on Passage A)","items":["Walter opens with a rhetorical concession, admitting that 'my Latin has been indifferent this half' before he has asked his father for anything at all. The concession is a persuasive technique rather than an accident of honesty: by naming the weakness in his own words, Walter forecloses the obvious objection and repositions himself as an honest witness rather than a boy pleading a weak case. He builds on this immediately with direct address and an appeal to his father's own experience of him, 'a father who has never once been deceived by me,' where the emphatic adverbial 'never once' admits no exception, so that to refuse him now would mean doubting a son's honesty for the first time. The relative clause does quiet work of its own: attached to the noun 'father', 'who has never once been deceived by me' arrives as a settled fact about the man rather than a claim Walter is making about himself, and a reader finds it far harder to argue with a description than with a boast. The cumulative effect on his reader is to move the argument off a single set of marks and onto Walter's character, which is the very ground on which he goes on to ask to be judged, 'by the whole of what I have become'."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Walter says 'my Latin has been indifferent this half' and this shows he knows it was not good. He also says 'a father who has never once been deceived by me' which shows he wants his dad to trust him. This makes his father maybe believe him more when he asks to stay in the eleven. Walter uses these words to try and persuade his father. This meets the Level 2 descriptor 'attempts to comment on the effect of language' and 'selects some appropriate textual detail', since the two quotations are relevant and a simple effect is named. It misses the Level 4 descriptors because the comment does not analyse why Walter places the admission before the request, no subject terminology such as 'concession' or 'direct address' is used, and the quotations are simply reported one after another rather than shown working together towards one effect on the reader."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Paper 2, the whole paper (Questions 1 to 4, Section A Reading)","items":["Question 1 — 'Read again the first part of Source A... Choose four statements below which are TRUE.' 4 marks, AO1, point-marked (1 mark per correctly shaded true statement, max 4). The 15 minutes the specimen paper advises for reading the Sources and all five questions is additional to the 45 minutes advised for Section A; inside that 45, Question 1's four marks come to about 5 minutes pro rata. Not levelled.","Question 2 — 'Use details from both Sources. Write a summary of the differences between...' 8 marks, AO1, about 9 minutes pro rata within the 45 advised for Section A. Level 4 Perceptive summary (7-8): 'Makes perceptive inferences from both texts; Makes judicious references/use of textual detail relevant to the focus of the question; Statements show perceptive differences between texts.' Level 3 Clear, relevant summary (5-6): 'Makes clear inferences from both texts; Selects clear references/textual detail relevant to the focus of the question; Statements show clear differences between texts.' Level 2 Some attempts at summary (3-4): 'Attempts some inference(s) from one/both texts; Selects some appropriate references/textual detail from one/both texts; Statements show some difference(s) between texts.' Level 1 Simple, limited summary (1-2): 'Offers paraphrase rather than inference; Makes simple reference/textual details from one/both texts; Statements show simple difference between texts.'","Question 3 — 'How does [the writer] use language to try to influence...?' 12 marks, AO2, one named source only, about 13 minutes pro rata within the 45 advised for Section A. Level 4 Detailed, perceptive analysis (10-12): 'Analyses the effects of the writer's choices of language; Selects a judicious range of textual detail; Makes sophisticated and accurate use of subject terminology.' Level 3 Clear, relevant explanation (7-9): 'Explains clearly the effects of the writer's choices of language; Selects a range of relevant textual detail; Makes clear and accurate use of subject terminology.' Level 2 Some understanding and comment (4-6): 'Attempts to comment on the effect of language; Selects some appropriate textual detail; Makes some use of subject terminology, mainly appropriately.' Level 1 Simple, limited comment (1-3): 'Offers simple comment on the effects of language; Selects simple references or textual details; Makes simple use of subject terminology, not always appropriately.'","Question 4 — 'Compare how the two writers convey their different attitudes to...' 16 marks, AO3, about 18 minutes pro rata within the 45 advised for Section A. Level 4 Perceptive, detailed (13-16): 'Compares ideas and perspectives in a perceptive way; Analyses how writers' methods are used; Selects a range of judicious supporting detail from both texts; Shows a detailed understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.' Level 3 Clear, relevant (9-12): 'Compares ideas and perspectives in a clear and relevant way; Explains clearly how writers' methods are used; Selects relevant detail to support from both texts; Shows a clear understanding of the different ideas and perspectives in both texts.' Level 2 Some, attempts (5-8): 'Attempts to compare ideas and perspectives; Makes some comment on how writers' methods are used; Selects some appropriate textual detail/references, not always supporting from one or both texts; Identifies some different ideas and perspectives.' Level 1 Simple, limited (1-4): 'Makes simple cross reference of ideas and perspectives; Makes simple identification of writers' methods; Makes simple references/textual details from one or both texts; Shows simple awareness of ideas and/or perspectives.'","Order of work across Section A: read both Sources and all five questions first, in the 15 minutes the paper advises for reading, which comes before the 45 minutes advised for Section A; then answer Question 1 through Question 4 in that order, spending roughly 5, 9, 13 and 18 of those 45 minutes on them, since each question narrows or widens which part of the text may be used."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Paper 2 Question 5 (Section B Writing) and subject terminology","items":["Question 5 — a single writing task, form, audience and purpose specified by the paper, provoked by a statement, opinion or scenario related to Section A's theme. 40 marks: 24 for AO5 Content and Organisation, 16 for AO6 Technical Accuracy. About 45 minutes advised for the whole of Section B, including planning and checking time.","AO5 Content and Organisation, upper Level 4 (22-24): 'Register is convincing and compelling for audience; Assuredly matched to purpose; Extensive and ambitious vocabulary with sustained crafting of linguistic devices; Varied and inventive use of structural features; Writing is compelling, incorporating a range of convincing and complex ideas; Fluently linked paragraphs with seamlessly integrated discourse markers.'","AO6 Technical Accuracy, Level 4 (13-16): 'Sentence demarcation is consistently secure and consistently accurate; Wide range of punctuation is used with a high level of accuracy; Uses a full range of appropriate sentence forms for effect; Uses Standard English consistently and appropriately with secure control of complex grammatical structures; High level of accuracy in spelling, including ambitious vocabulary; Extensive and ambitious use of vocabulary.'","Rhetorical concession — admitting a weak point in advance to appear honest and disarm the reader's objection before it is raised.","Semantic field — a group of words linked by one shared area of meaning, used across a passage to build a single impression.","Direct address — a writer speaking straight to a named reader, drawing that reader personally into the text."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — Paper 2 Question 5: 'Team sport teaches lessons in life that no individual activity can match. Write an article for a broadsheet newspaper in which you explain your point of view on this statement.' (40 marks)","items":["Decoded task: form is a newspaper article; audience is broadsheet readers, who expect a considered, structured argument rather than a rant; purpose is to explain a personal point of view on the given statement, not to describe or narrate.","Move 1, the title and opening: a title that signals the article's angle, then an opening paragraph that states the writer's position clearly, using a personal anecdote or observation to establish register immediately, as in 'A friend of mine told me last month that her son had swapped Saturday rugby for solo running.'","Move 2, a fair concession: one paragraph that acknowledges the strongest point on the other side of the statement, such as the private satisfaction of an individual personal best, before returning to the writer's own position.","Move 3, the central argument: one or two paragraphs building the writer's case with a specific kind of evidence each, for example what a team teaches that a stopwatch cannot, developed with concrete detail rather than vague assertion.","Move 4, the wider consequence: a paragraph that lifts the argument beyond the individual case to what is lost or gained more broadly if the statement is true, giving the article weight beyond one anecdote.","Timing: about 5 minutes to plan the position and the paragraph order, about 30 minutes to draft, and the remaining minutes of the 45 advised for Section B kept back to check spelling, punctuation and sentence variety, since technical accuracy carries 16 of the 40 marks.","What the last paragraph must do: return explicitly to the statement being argued, restate the writer's position in different words from the opening, and end on one sharp, memorable line rather than introducing a new idea."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T10:25:09Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Every fault in the first verdict is genuinely fixed — the three misquotations are gone ('unmake' now quoted correctly, 'Nobody carried him off the field' restored in full, and the claim that the column 'ends on a question' replaced by 'its final declarative admitting uncertainty') — and on a fresh reading of 164 quoted fragments, all 138 taken from the passages are word for word and every tariff, AO and level label matches the Paper 2 mark scheme, leaving only two attribution slips and three presentational points well inside publishing standard."}}},"spoken-language":{"id":"AQA-8700","topic":"spoken-language","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"The Spoken Language endorsement is the one assessment on this course that carries no marks at all and still appears on your certificate. It is graded Pass, Merit, Distinction or Not Classified on a competency basis, which means something unusual: you do not collect credit for the good parts and lose it on the weak ones. You must hit every criterion in a grade before any of the next grade counts, so a brilliant talk with a mumbled, one-word answer to a question is capped by the answer. This room teaches the four things the criteria actually measure — how you select and organise material, how you plan for a named purpose and audience, how you speak in Standard English in a formal setting, and how you listen and respond to questions and feedback — and it teaches them against the criteria themselves, because they are published and you are allowed to prepare against them.","idea":[{"h":"Choosing what goes in, and the order it goes in","t":"The specification asks for 'selecting and organising information and ideas effectively and persuasively for prepared spoken presentations'. Selecting means cutting: ten minutes holds one claim and three or four reasons, not everything you know about the subject. Organising means an order a listener can follow without a page in front of them — a hook, the claim, the reasons in rising order of strength, the request. The grades turn on exactly this ladder: a Pass 'makes an attempt to organise and structure his or her presentation'; a Merit 'organises and structures his or her presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience'; a Distinction does it 'using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience'. A Distinction performance therefore plans the strategies as well as the sections: where the story goes, where the audience is addressed directly, where the opening image is brought back at the end.","code":"3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)"},{"h":"Planning is a requirement, not advice","t":"The subject content asks for 'planning effectively for different purposes and audiences', and section 4.4 turns that into a rule: 'presentations must be planned and organised. Students should be advised that that lack of preparation is likely to prevent access to the criteria for the higher grades.' Two further conditions sit alongside it. 'Students must identify the subject for their presentations in advance and agree it with their teacher', so the subject is settled before any drafting; and students 'may use pre-prepared notes, powerpoint etc. to assist them during their presentations but this is not a requirement', so notes are a support you choose, never a script you read. A Distinction performance plans two things at once — the purpose (what the audience should think or do by the end) and the audience (what they already believe, and what they will ask) — and lets those two decisions settle the order, the vocabulary and the length.","code":"3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (planning for purpose and audience)"},{"h":"What the performance itself has to be","t":"The assessed event is 'a prepared spoken presentation on a specific topic' and 'as a guide, the duration should be no more than ten minutes'. AO7 states the objective in one line: 'Demonstrate presentation skills in a formal setting.' Formal is the fixed requirement; the shape is not, because 'presentations must be formal but may take a wide variety of forms, including talks, debates, speeches and dialogues'. A talk explains and persuades; a speech works on an audience through rhetoric and rhythm; a debate answers an opponent; a dialogue, such as an interview, carries its content in the exchange. Choosing the form is a real decision, because it fixes how you can engage the audience. A top performance picks the form the subject deserves and then uses it deliberately: a speech that never lifts its voice, or an interview whose answers are monologues, has chosen a form and then ignored it.","code":"3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (making presentations and speeches)"},{"h":"The part after you stop talking","t":"'Responding to spoken language: listening to and responding appropriately to any questions and feedback.' This is not an optional extra: 'as part of, or following, the presentation students must listen to and respond appropriately to questions and feedback', and the aim of the assessment includes 'asking questions themselves to elicit clarification'. AO8 covers it. The criteria climb clearly: a Pass 'listens to questions/feedback and provides an appropriate response in a straight forward manner'; a Merit listens 'responding formally and in some detail'; a Distinction 'responds perceptively and if appropriate elaborates with further ideas and information'. Perceptive means hearing what is really being asked — the objection underneath the question — and answering that. A top performance also checks before it answers, asking the questioner what they mean when a question could be taken two ways, and then adds something new rather than repeating a sentence from the talk.","code":"3.1.3 responding to spoken language"},{"h":"Standard English, spoken","t":"The subject content requires 'expressing ideas using Standard English whenever and wherever appropriate', and AO9 asks you to 'use spoken Standard English effectively in speeches and presentations'. The general criteria define what that means here, and the definition is narrower than people expect: a learner must 'be intelligible' and 'generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation'. It is about grammatical standard forms and formal register, not about accent, and not about sounding like a written essay. In practice it means standard subject–verb agreement and standard negatives, fillers and slang removed, and vocabulary chosen for the room you are standing in. A top performance sounds like a person speaking carefully rather than a person reading: short sentences, standard forms, and a repertoire of precise words used because they are the right ones, not because they are long.","code":"3.1.3 spoken Standard English"},{"h":"What the assessment is for","t":"'The aim of the assessment is to allow students to demonstrate their speaking and listening skills by: giving a presentation in a formal context; responding appropriately to questions and to feedback, asking questions themselves to elicit clarification; using spoken Standard English. The assessment will be separately endorsed and will cover AO7, AO8 and AO9 for spoken language.' Those three bullets are the whole assessment in order, and they are also a warning: two of the three happen after the prepared part is over, so preparation that stops at the last line of the script has prepared for a third of the thing. It is compulsory — 'for the award of the GCSE in English Language students must offer all three assessments'. A strong candidate prepares the questions as carefully as the talk, and treats the exchange afterwards as the second half of the performance.","code":"4.4 Aim of the assessment"},{"h":"The six key requirements, checked one by one","t":"'Students must undertake a prepared spoken presentation on a specific topic.' The key requirements are: presentations 'must be formal but may take a wide variety of forms, including talks, debates, speeches and dialogues'; 'students must identify the subject for their presentations in advance and agree it with their teacher'; 'presentations must be planned and organised'; students 'may use pre-prepared notes, powerpoint etc. to assist them during their presentations but this is not a requirement'; 'as part of, or following, the presentation students must listen to and respond appropriately to questions and feedback'; and 'where the audience is the teacher only, the presentation and dialogue must be designed in such a way that it could have a potentially wider audience than just one person (eg it replicates a television interview)'. A strong candidate treats this list as a checklist to clear before rehearsing, because a performance that breaks one of them cannot be rescued by delivery.","code":"4.4 Content (key requirements)"},{"h":"Competency, not a mark out of ten","t":"'No marks will be assigned to a student's performance – it will be assessed holistically as a grade, using a competency basis on criteria which are provided below. Competency means that a student must hit all the criteria in one grade before moving on to the next. Students who do not reach the Pass standard must be recorded as Not Classified.' The consequence is the single most useful thing to know about this assessment: your weakest criterion decides your grade. Three Distinction-standard criteria and one Pass-standard answer to a question is a Pass. It is teacher set throughout the course, marked by the teacher, and is a separate endorsement at nought per cent weighting of the GCSE. A candidate who understands this rehearses the part they are worst at, because improving the strongest part of the performance cannot change the grade.","code":"4.4 Assessment (competency basis)"},{"h":"The criteria you have to meet before any grade counts","t":"'To be awarded a Pass, Merit or Distinction a learner must: be audible; use Spoken Standard English which, for the purposes of the spoken language assessment, means that a learner must – be intelligible; generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation.' These sit underneath all three grade columns, each of which opens 'In addition to the general criteria...'. Audible is literal: the person furthest from you hears every word, which is a matter of volume, pace and keeping your head up rather than reading into a page. Intelligible means the listener can follow the sense first time, with no second attempt. A strong candidate rehearses these physically — standing, aloud, at the distance of the real room — because they are the only criteria that cannot be fixed in the writing.","code":"4.4 General criteria"},{"h":"The Pass standard, criterion by criterion","t":"'In addition to the general criteria, to be awarded a Pass a Learner's performance in his or her spoken language assessment must meet all of the following criteria: expresses straightforward ideas/information/feelings; makes an attempt to organise and structure his or her presentation; makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience; listens to questions/feedback and provides an appropriate response in a straight forward manner.' Read the verbs: 'expresses', 'makes an attempt', 'provides an appropriate response'. A Pass gets ideas across, has a recognisable beginning, middle and end, shows some awareness that an audience is there, and answers the question it was asked. Nothing here asks for ambition. A candidate working at this level should aim to secure all four first — because none of Merit counts until they are all present — and then look for the one criterion that can be lifted to the next column.","code":"4.4 Pass"},{"h":"What Merit adds","t":"'In addition to the general criteria, to be awarded a Merit a Learner's performance ... must meet all of the following criteria: expresses challenging ideas/information/feelings using a range of vocabulary; organises and structures his or her presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience; achieves the purpose of his or her presentation; listens to questions/feedback responding formally and in some detail.' Three words do the work. 'Challenging' means ideas that could be argued with, not a description of a subject. 'Clearly and appropriately' turns an attempt at structure into a structure chosen for these listeners. 'Achieves the purpose' is new at Merit and is the criterion students lose: if the talk set out to persuade, the audience must actually be asked for something. A Merit answer to a question is formal and detailed — a developed reply, not a sentence.","code":"4.4 Merit"},{"h":"What Distinction adds","t":"'In addition to the general criteria, to be awarded a Distinction a Learner's performance ... must meet all of the following criteria: expresses sophisticated ideas/information/feelings using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary; organises and structures his or her presentation using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience; achieves the purpose of his or her presentation; listens to questions/feedback, responds perceptively and if appropriate elaborates with further ideas and information.' The two lifts are vocabulary and strategy. A 'sophisticated repertoire' is range used precisely, not long words dropped in. 'An effective range of strategies' means several deliberate engagement devices across the talk — an anecdote that opens and returns, direct address, a rhetorical question, a deliberate contrast, a pause before the request. The last criterion asks for elaboration: a Distinction answer hears the objection underneath the question, answers it, and then offers something the questioner did not have.","code":"4.4 Distinction"},{"h":"How the grade is reported and checked","t":"'The preparation and assessment of Spoken Language is a compulsory requirement of the course of study. It will appear on all students' certificates as a separately reported grade, alongside the overall grade issued. Performance will be assessed against common criteria issued by all exam boards.' The endorsement 'will be reported as a separate grade (Pass, Merit, Distinction or Not Classified) and will not contribute to the result of the GCSE English Language qualification'; 'no marks will be assigned'; and it 'will be assessed on a competency basis using agreed common criteria – to be awarded a grade students must achieve all of the criteria for that grade'. Centres must provide audio-visual recordings of a sample of students, complete and unedited: all students where there are thirty or fewer, thirty where there are more, with a minimum of ten at each grade. A candidate should assume the recording will be watched by someone who was not in the room, and speak to that person too.","code":"5 Non-exam assessment administration"}],"examples":[{"title":"Narrowing a subject before you agree it with your teacher","setup":"You must identify the subject for your presentation in advance and agree it with your teacher. You have written down one word: 'social media'. The presentation must be formal, will last no more than ten minutes, will be given to your tutor group and teacher, and you must take questions on it afterwards. Turn 'social media' into a subject you could agree today.","steps":["Cut the field down to one claim somebody could disagree with: 'recommendation feeds should be explained to us in tutor time before we are old enough to be sold to by them'.","Test the claim against the clock: one claim with three reasons and a request fits comfortably; a tour of an entire industry does not, and would leave every point undeveloped.","Test it against the questions that will follow: you can already answer who would teach the lesson, whose time it would use, and what you would drop to make room, so the subject is defensible.","Write the sentence you will take to your teacher, with the purpose and audience in it: 'I want to persuade this tutor group that recommendation feeds should be taught in tutor time, in three reasons and one request.'"],"cues":["What could a listener actually disagree with here?","Does the claim fit the time you are given?","What will the first hostile question be, and can you answer it?","How would you say the subject, the purpose and the audience in one sentence?"]},{"title":"Finding the engagement strategies in Passage A","setup":"Passage A opens: 'Last summer, my grandfather collapsed in the queue at the post office, and for eleven seconds nobody moved.' Its last paragraph begins 'So here is what I am asking for. Not a campaign, not a petition: one timetabled afternoon a year, for every year group, taught properly and practised again the year after.' and, just before its closing courtesies, says 'If it is ever your grandfather in that queue, you will not wish we had spent that hour on something else.' Name the strategies that link the opening to the ending, and say what each does to the audience.","steps":["Locate the return: the grandfather and the queue appear in the first sentence of the talk and again in its final paragraph, so the talk closes the circle it opened.","Name that strategy: framing, in which the closing image repeats the opening one, and the listener feels the talk arrive somewhere rather than stop.","Name the second strategy in the ending: the pronoun switches from 'my grandfather' to 'your grandfather', a direct address that hands the speaker's story to the audience as their own risk.","Name the third: 'Not a campaign, not a petition' is a contrast that clears away the large, vague things the request is not, so the small, specific request lands unopposed.","Judge it against the criteria: three deliberate devices working together is what 'an effective range of strategies to engage the audience' means at Distinction, as against a single attempt to interest the listener at the start."],"cues":["Which image from the opening comes back at the end?","What is that repetition called, and why use it last?","Whose grandfather is it in the sentence just before the closing courtesies?","What work does the pair of negatives do before the request?","Is this one strategy or a range, and which grade needs a range?"]},{"title":"Repairing a closing line into spoken Standard English","setup":"A classmate's rehearsed closing is: 'So yeah, basically we was thinking the school should like do first aid, and it ain't hard, so there you go.' The general criteria require the speaker to be intelligible and to generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation. Repair the closing so it still sounds spoken rather than read aloud.","steps":["Mark the non-standard grammar first: 'we was' is non-standard subject–verb agreement, and 'ain't' is a non-standard negative form.","Mark the fillers next: 'so yeah', 'basically', 'like' and 'there you go' add no meaning and tell a formal audience that the speaker is still deciding what to say.","Rebuild it: 'So that is what I am asking the school to do: teach first aid to every year group, in timetabled lessons. It is not difficult to learn, and it is not expensive to teach. Thank you for listening.'","Check it is still speech: the sentences are short, the audience is addressed directly and the closing courtesy is kept, so the register is formal without becoming written prose.","Judge it: the criteria ask for language appropriate to a formal setting, which this now is, and for intelligibility, which the standard forms protect."],"cues":["Which two grammatical forms would a listener mark as non-standard?","Which words carry no meaning at all?","How would you say the same request formally?","Has the repair turned it into an essay read out loud?","Which general criteria does the repaired version satisfy?"]},{"title":"Reading the climb in an exchange after the talk","setup":"In Passage C the teacher asks: 'How would you answer a governor who told you the timetable is already full?' The speaker replies 'Could I check what you mean by full — full of subject lessons, or full of tutor time?', is told 'Tutor time', and then answers that tutor time 'is already where we are taught what to do in a fire and what to do when something goes wrong online', that first aid 'belongs in the same category: a rehearsed response to a rare event', and offers to 'Start with Year 10 only, and test us in June'. Say which criteria this exchange meets, and what would have capped it at Pass.","steps":["Identify the first move: the speaker asks a question back before answering, which is the aim's own requirement that students ask questions themselves to elicit clarification.","Identify what the clarification buys: the answer can now be aimed at tutor time specifically instead of covering both possibilities vaguely, which is why it can be detailed.","Identify the perceptive move: the speaker hears the real objection underneath the question, which is not 'is there a gap' but 'does this belong in the curriculum at all', and answers that by putting first aid in the same category as fire drill and online safety.","Identify the elaboration: the offer to start with Year 10 and test in June is new information the questioner did not have, which is what the Distinction criterion means by elaborating with further ideas.","Say what Pass would have looked like: 'I would say there is room in tutor time' is an appropriate response in a straightforward manner, and stops there — true, relevant, and capped."],"cues":["What does the speaker do before answering at all?","Why is the reply more detailed because of that first move?","What is the governor really objecting to?","Which part of the reply is new information?","How would a Pass-standard version of this answer sound?"]},{"title":"Matching the strategies to the audience in Passage B","setup":"Passage B, a speech to a town council's transport consultation, says: 'You have been told that the 46 is quiet. It is quiet at eleven in the morning. It is not quiet at four o'clock, when it carries three schools' worth of us home, standing, in wet coats, apologising to each other for our bags.' It then asks: 'When we say that a service is not viable, who exactly are we saying is not viable?' Explain how these choices are shaped for councillors rather than for a tutor group.","steps":["Start from the audience's position: councillors arrive holding the case against the route, so the speech names their evidence before contesting it.","Name the first strategy: the antithesis of 'quiet at eleven in the morning' against 'not quiet at four o'clock' concedes the claim and then limits it, which is harder to dismiss than denying it outright.","Name the second: the concrete detail of wet coats and bags gives the council's average a body, so an abstraction they can approve is replaced by people they would have to overrule.","Name the third: the rhetorical question turns their own word, 'viable', back on them and asks who it describes, which is a challenge a formal audience can accept because it is put as a question rather than an accusation.","Judge the fit: each device is chosen for listeners who hold power and dislike being lectured, which is what 'clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience' asks for, and together they are the range a Distinction wants."],"cues":["What do these listeners already believe when the speaker stands up?","What is the effect of admitting the route is quiet?","Why mention coats and bags to a committee?","Whose word is 'viable', and what is done with it?","Why does this count as a range of strategies rather than one?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A student gives a well-structured, engaging ten-minute talk and then answers the only question with one short sentence: 'I think it would work.' The answer is audible, formal and relevant, but it is not developed. What is the highest grade available, and why?","a":"A Pass: competency means every criterion in a grade must be met, and an undeveloped sentence meets the Pass criterion 'provides an appropriate response in a straight forward manner' but not Merit's 'responding formally and in some detail', so no Merit or Distinction criterion can count."},{"q":"Which criterion appears at Merit and Distinction but not at Pass?","a":"'Achieves the purpose of his or her presentation.'"},{"q":"What are the two things the general criteria mean by Spoken Standard English?","a":"Being intelligible, and generally using language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation."},{"q":"The specification says a presentation may take a wide variety of forms. Which quality is fixed whatever form you choose?","a":"It must be formal — 'presentations must be formal but may take a wide variety of forms, including talks, debates, speeches and dialogues'."}]},"room":{"facts":["The endorsement is reported on the certificate as a separate grade — Pass, Merit, Distinction or Not Classified — and does not contribute to the GCSE English Language result. No marks are assigned to a performance.","Competency means a student must hit all the criteria in one grade before moving on to the next, so the weakest criterion sets the grade; a student who does not reach the Pass standard is recorded as Not Classified.","The general criteria, required before any grade: be audible; use Spoken Standard English, which for this assessment means be intelligible and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation.","The four Pass criteria: expresses straightforward ideas/information/feelings; makes an attempt to organise and structure his or her presentation; makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience; listens to questions/feedback and provides an appropriate response in a straight forward manner.","The four Merit criteria: expresses challenging ideas/information/feelings using a range of vocabulary; organises and structures his or her presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience; achieves the purpose of his or her presentation; listens to questions/feedback responding formally and in some detail.","The four Distinction criteria: expresses sophisticated ideas/information/feelings using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary; organises and structures his or her presentation using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience; achieves the purpose of his or her presentation; listens to questions/feedback, responds perceptively and if appropriate elaborates with further ideas and information.","The performance must be formal, on a specific topic identified in advance and agreed with the teacher, planned and organised, no more than ten minutes as a guide, and followed by (or including) questions and feedback the student must listen and respond to."],"questions":[{"q":"Two classmates write down a subject to take to the teacher, as the specification requires. The first writes: 'I am going to tell you about the history of the bicycle.' The second writes: 'I want to persuade this tutor group that the school's lost property should be run by Year 10, because at present nobody runs it at all.' Say which of the two is ready to be agreed for a formal presentation of no more than ten minutes with questions afterwards, give the two reasons it is ready, and then rewrite the other so that it is. Graded against two criteria: the Pass criterion 'makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience' and the Merit criterion 'achieves the purpose of his or her presentation', so it carries 2.","a":"The second: one arguable claim, aimed at these listeners, with something the room could be asked to do about it. The first is a field with no claim and no purpose beyond telling, and the rewrite must give it both.","sol":"The second subject is ready, for two reasons. It names a purpose the talk can be measured against — to persuade — and it points at something the room could act on, which is what 'achieves the purpose of his or her presentation' asks for: a talk that merely finishes has achieved nothing. And it is aimed at these particular listeners, because a tutor group loses and hunts for property every week, so every example can be checked by the people hearing it; choosing material this audience can test is the attempt to meet their needs that the Pass criterion wants. The first is a field, not a subject: 'the history of the bicycle' contains no claim, so nobody in the room can agree or disagree with it, nothing decides where it should stop at ten minutes, and the questions afterwards could come from anywhere. Rewritten: 'I want to persuade this tutor group that the school should give half of the staff car park over to bicycle stands, and I will use the ninety years this town has spent building for cars to show why it will not happen unless we ask.' The field has become one claim; the claim carries a request the audience can be asked to support; the history is demoted from subject to evidence; and the listeners are now the people who would use the stands, which is what makes the material theirs rather than mine.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A subject is only ready when a listener could disagree with it; a field of interest is not yet a subject.","Test each one twice: is there a purpose the audience could see achieved, and is the material something this room can check?","To rewrite the weaker one, find the claim hiding inside the field and decide what you would ask the room to do about it."],"codes":["3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (planning for purpose and audience)","4.4 Pass","4.4 Merit"]},{"q":"A classmate's rehearsed reply to an objection, half way through a talk on the school's phone policy, is: 'I mean, loads of teachers reckon phones don't never help in lessons, but like, us lot was saying it depends, innit, so whatever.' Rewrite the reply for the formal setting of the assessment, keeping it speakable rather than written out loud, and name the changes you made. Graded against the two sub-points the general criteria use to define Spoken Standard English — be intelligible, and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation — so it carries 2.","a":"The non-standard forms ('don't never', 'us lot was', the tag 'innit') replaced by standard ones, the colloquial lexis ('loads of', 'reckon') lifted, and the fillers ('I mean', 'like', 'so whatever') cut, without turning a spoken reply into written prose.","sol":"'I know that many teachers believe phones never help in a lesson. My answer is that it depends on the lesson, and I want to give you two cases where it does. Then you can decide whether one rule can fairly cover both.' Four kinds of change have been made. The non-standard grammar has gone: 'don't never' is a double negative, which a formal listener hears as the opposite of what is meant, so it becomes 'never help'; 'us lot was saying' carries a non-standard subject pronoun and non-standard agreement together, and becomes 'My answer is', which also stops a vague group standing in for the speaker; and the tag 'innit' is deleted rather than replaced, because its work was to ask for agreement the speaker had not yet earned. The fillers have gone with it: 'I mean' and 'like' carry no meaning, and 'so whatever' concedes the argument before anybody has answered it. The colloquial lexis has been lifted with them: 'loads of' is an informal quantifier and 'reckon' an informal verb of opinion, so 'loads of teachers reckon' becomes 'many teachers believe' — the same claim, in the register a formal setting expects. What has deliberately been kept is the speech. The sentences are short enough to be said in one breath, the audience is addressed directly as 'you', and the decision is handed back to them at the end — because spoken Standard English means standard forms in a formal register, not an essay read aloud.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Separate the two problems in the line: one is grammar, the other is words that carry no meaning.","Work through it marking every form a listener would hear as non-standard, then every word you could delete without losing sense.","Your rewrite should still sound like someone speaking to a room — short sentences, direct address, and the decision handed back to the audience."],"codes":["3.1.3 spoken Standard English","4.4 General criteria"]},{"q":"Passage A opens 'Last summer, my grandfather collapsed in the queue at the post office, and for eleven seconds nobody moved.' Write the first forty to sixty words of a formal talk of your own, on a subject you could agree with your teacher, using one strategy of your choice to engage the audience from the first sentence, and name the strategy underneath. Graded against one criterion — the Pass criterion 'makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience' — so it carries 1.","a":"An opening that gives the audience a reason to listen (a concrete moment, a surprising fact of the speaker's own, a direct question) rather than announcing the title, with the strategy correctly named.","sol":"'Not one of you has ever seen the inside of the building we walk past every morning. It has been locked for nine years. I have been inside twice, because my aunt has the keys, and I want to tell you what our town is keeping empty — and what a hundred of us could do with it by September.' The strategy is a withheld reveal: the audience is told that something known to them is unknown to them, and the answer is delayed until the speaker has been given their attention. It is aimed at these particular listeners — people who pass the building daily — which is what it means to make an attempt to meet the needs of the audience: the first sentence is chosen for the room it is spoken in, not simply for the subject.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The first sentence of a talk buys attention or loses it; a title announcement buys nothing.","Think about what these listeners already know or walk past, and open where their experience is.","Write the opening, then say in one sentence what the device is called and who it was chosen for."],"codes":["3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)","4.4 Pass"]},{"q":"You have just given a talk arguing that your school should change its uniform policy. A classmate asks: 'How much would your proposal cost?' You do not know the figure. Write the response you would give aloud, in two or three sentences. Graded against one criterion — the Pass criterion 'listens to questions/feedback and provides an appropriate response in a straight forward manner' — so it carries 1.","a":"An honest, direct answer that admits what is not known, answers the part that can be answered, and stays courteous and formal rather than bluffing or ignoring the question.","sol":"'I do not have a costing, and I would rather say so than invent one. What I can say is that the change I am proposing removes one item from the uniform list rather than adding one, so the cost falls on the school's supplier arrangements rather than on families. If it would help, I could ask the business manager for the figure and bring it to the next council meeting.' This is straightforward in the sense the criterion means: the question is heard and answered directly, the gap is admitted rather than covered over, and the part of the question that can be answered is answered. The closing offer keeps the exchange formal and cooperative, and it costs nothing to make.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The criterion rewards responding appropriately, not knowing everything.","Split the question into the part you cannot answer and the part you can, and deal with them in that order.","Two or three sentences: the admission, the part you can answer, and what you will do about the rest."],"codes":["3.1.3 responding to spoken language","4.4 Pass"]},{"q":"A classmate rehearses her presentation to you in an empty classroom. She reads from her phone at speed with her chin down; the last two words of most sentences are lost; she says 'stuff' four times and 'this bit is a bit boring but anyway' once. The specification prints the general criteria as two bullets — be audible, and use Spoken Standard English, which it defines by two sub-points: be intelligible, and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation. Write one piece of practical advice for each of those three tickable requirements. Graded against the three, so it carries 3.","a":"Volume and pace fixed by getting the head up and rehearsing at the real distance; sense protected by slowing down and finishing sentences; register repaired by replacing vague nouns and cutting the apology.","sol":"Audible: lift the phone to chest height and rehearse standing at the back of the room you will actually use, aiming your voice at the furthest chair — reading downwards sends the ends of sentences into the floor, which is why the last two words keep disappearing. Intelligible: slow the pace and put a full stop where the phone has a comma; a listener gets one chance at every sentence, and speed is what takes that chance away, so mark the script with a pause line every few lines and honour it. Appropriate to the formal setting: replace 'stuff' with the precise nouns it is standing in for — 'the evidence', 'the objections', 'the cost' — because a vague word four times over tells the audience the speaker has not decided what she means; and cut 'this bit is a bit boring but anyway' altogether, since apologising for your own material invites the audience to stop listening. If a section really is dull, the fix is to shorten it, not to introduce it.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Three criteria, three separate problems — do not let one piece of advice cover all of them.","Two of the three are about delivery and can only be fixed by rehearsing aloud, standing, at the real distance.","For the third, look at what the vague noun and the apology each tell the audience about the speaker."],"codes":["4.4 General criteria","3.1.3 spoken Standard English"]},{"q":"Plan a five-minute formal talk to your tutor group on a subject you have agreed with your teacher, and set the plan out so that a listener could see all four Pass criteria being met: expresses straightforward ideas/information/feelings; makes an attempt to organise and structure his or her presentation; makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience; listens to questions/feedback and provides an appropriate response in a straight forward manner. Graded against those four criteria, so it carries 4.","a":"A plan with a clear beginning, middle and end, ideas stated plainly, one or two decisions taken for this audience, and two likely questions with straightforward answers prepared.","sol":"Subject, agreed with the teacher: the school should keep the library open until half past four. Purpose: to persuade the tutor group to sign the request to the head. Ideas, stated plainly (the Pass criterion asks for straightforward ideas, not complicated ones): people who cannot work at home have nowhere to go; the room is already heated and staffed until four; the cost is one extra half-hour of supervision. Structure, in four parts: a short opening naming what I want; the three reasons in that order; the objection I expect (staffing) and my answer to it; the request. Meeting the needs of the audience: the examples come from our own year group's timetable, and the talk uses the room we all know rather than statistics about other schools, because this audience can check everything I say against their own experience. Questions prepared: 'who would supervise it?' — 'The two staff who are already in the building until four; I have asked one of them and she is willing to be asked formally.' And 'what if nobody comes?' — 'Then the school stops it after a term; I am asking for a trial, not a permanent change.' Both answers are short, direct and true, which is all the Pass responding criterion requires: an appropriate response in a straightforward manner.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Pass asks you to attempt each of the four things, so make sure your plan shows all four rather than doing one of them well.","Work in the criteria's own order: the ideas, then the shape, then the audience decision, then the questions.","Finish by writing down two questions you would genuinely be asked, and the honest answer to each."],"codes":["4.4 Pass","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (planning for purpose and audience)","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)"]},{"q":"Passage A moves from a personal story, to the claim that 'first aid should be taught to every student in this school, in timetabled lessons, before we leave Year 11', to three reasons, to a request. Rework that same material into a different order for a different audience — the school's governors at an evening meeting — and explain in two or three sentences why the new order suits them. Graded against two criteria: the Merit criteria 'organises and structures his or her presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience' and 'achieves the purpose of his or her presentation', so it carries 2.","a":"The request moved to the front with its cost and scale attached, the reasons ordered as feasibility before principle, and the story kept but shortened and placed as evidence rather than as a hook.","sol":"New order for governors: (1) the request first, in one sentence with its size attached — one timetabled afternoon a year for each year group, taught by an outside trainer, at a cost the finance committee can see; (2) feasibility second — it is a single afternoon, it uses time that already exists, and the skill 'can be taught in a single afternoon'; (3) the principle third — that 'the person standing closest is almost never a doctor', so the skill has to sit with everybody or it sits in the wrong place; (4) the story last and in three sentences, as the evidence that the principle is real; (5) a closing ask for a decision, with a date. Governors are not an audience to be warmed up: they arrive with an agenda, a clock and a budget, and they are being asked to approve something, so the proposal has to be visible in the first thirty seconds or they will spend the talk waiting to find out what they are being asked for. Putting feasibility before principle matches the order in which this audience actually resists — they rarely dispute that first aid matters, they dispute whether it fits — and ending on a request for a decision by a named date is what achieving the purpose means with a committee, because a talk that moves them without asking them for anything has achieved nothing.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what this audience has that a tutor group does not: an agenda, a budget and the power to say yes.","Reorder by the order in which the audience will resist, not by the order in which you thought of your reasons.","For a committee, decide what the final sentence has to obtain from them, and work backwards from it."],"codes":["4.4 Merit","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (planning for purpose and audience)"]},{"q":"Passage B closes, in the three sentences just before its final 'Thank you.', 'Keep the route for one more year. Count it at four o'clock, not at eleven. Let us prove the afternoon to you ourselves, and if we are wrong, I will come back to this room and say so.' Write two questions a listening councillor might ask after that speech, and the responses you would give aloud. Graded against two criteria: the Merit criteria 'listens to questions/feedback responding formally and in some detail' and 'expresses challenging ideas/information/feelings using a range of vocabulary', so it carries 2.","a":"Two genuine, difficult questions (not soft ones), each answered formally, in several sentences, with specific detail rather than a repeated line from the speech.","sol":"Question one: 'You have asked us to count the route at four o'clock. What would you accept as a figure that proves you wrong?' Response: 'That is the right question to ask me, and I should answer it precisely rather than generally. If the four o'clock service carries fewer than a third of its seats on an average school week — counted across a full term, not a single sample day, and excluding the week of exams when we finish early — then I would accept that the route cannot be defended on demand alone, and I would say so publicly. I would ask only that the count is taken at the stop outside the school gates as well as at the terminus, because the terminus figures are what produced the impression that the service is quiet.' Question two: 'If we keep the 46, another route has to be reduced. Which one?' Response: 'I am not going to pretend that is an unfair question, and I am not going to pick a route I do not use and call it expendable. What I would say is that the choice as it is presented — one route against another — is a consequence of the budget being fixed before the consultation rather than after it. If the committee is obliged to choose tonight, I would ask it to look at duplication rather than at whole routes: there are two services covering the same half-mile of the high street within four minutes of each other, and reducing one of those does not strand anybody.' Both answers are formal in register, several sentences long, and give the committee something specific — a threshold, a counting method, a named alternative — which is the difference between detail and repetition.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Set yourself the questions you would least like to be asked; easy questions cannot show a detailed response.","A detailed answer offers something the questioner did not already have — a threshold, a method, a named alternative.","Answer in several sentences in the register of the meeting, not in the register of a conversation."],"codes":["4.4 Merit","3.1.3 responding to spoken language"]},{"q":"A student's presentation currently meets all four Pass criteria and no more. It is a five-minute talk called 'My work experience week'; it describes what she did each day in order; her opening is 'I am going to talk about my work experience'; her vocabulary is plain and repetitive ('it was interesting', 'it was good experience'); and she answers questions in a sentence each. Rewrite the four decisions behind that talk so that it meets all four Merit criteria instead, naming the criterion each decision serves. Graded against the four Merit criteria, so it carries 4.","a":"A challenging claim in place of a chronology, with a range of precise vocabulary; a structure chosen for this audience; a purpose the audience is actually asked to act on; and answers that are formal and developed.","sol":"Decision one, for 'expresses challenging ideas/information/feelings using a range of vocabulary': replace the chronology with a claim that could be argued with — 'a week of work experience taught me more about how adults talk to each other than about the job itself' — and replace 'interesting' and 'good experience' with the precise words the claim needs: negotiation, deference, the unwritten hierarchy of a small office, the difference between being trusted and being supervised. A range of vocabulary means words chosen because they are exact, not because they are long. Decision two, for 'organises and structures his or her presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience': drop Monday-to-Friday and structure by the three things the claim needs — what I expected, what I saw, what I would tell the person going next year — because the listeners are the people going next year, and that shape gives them something to take away. Decision three, for 'achieves the purpose of his or her presentation': decide the purpose is to change how the audience prepares, and end by asking them to do one specific thing — write down two questions to ask on their first morning — so the talk lands somewhere instead of simply finishing. Decision four, for 'listens to questions/feedback responding formally and in some detail': prepare the three questions that will actually come ('was it boring?', 'did they pay you?', 'would you work there?') and answer each in several formal sentences with a concrete example, rather than in one.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the two columns and note which words change: 'straightforward' becomes 'challenging', 'makes an attempt' becomes 'clearly and appropriately'.","One Merit criterion has no Pass equivalent at all — find it, and decide what the talk is actually asking the audience to do.","Take the criteria one at a time and write down the single decision that lifts each one."],"codes":["4.4 Merit","4.4 Pass","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)"]},{"q":"Your presentation will be given to your teacher alone, in a classroom at lunchtime, because you are the only student taking it that week. The specification requires that 'where the audience is the teacher only, the presentation and dialogue must be designed in such a way that it could have a potentially wider audience than just one person (eg it replicates a television interview)'. Describe the form you would choose and the two changes it makes to the way you speak. Graded against two criteria: the Merit criterion 'organises and structures his or her presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience' and the general criteria's 'generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation', so it carries 2.","a":"A form with a built-in wider audience — a broadcast interview, a conference paper, a radio piece — with the register and the mode of address adjusted so nothing depends on the listener being one familiar person.","sol":"I would present it as a recorded radio interview: my teacher takes the interviewer's part, and I answer as the invited guest on a programme with listeners who cannot see either of us. The first change is the mode of address. Nothing can rely on the person in front of me: no 'as you know', no reference to last lesson, no nodding at a shared joke. Every name, place and figure is introduced as though the listener is hearing it for the first time, which is exactly the discipline the requirement is trying to produce. The second change is register and pacing. I speak in complete, self-contained sentences, I signpost aloud where I am going ('there are two objections to that, and the second is the serious one'), and I keep the volume and pace of someone addressing a room rather than someone across a desk — because a conversation with one familiar adult naturally drifts into shorthand, and shorthand is what a wider audience cannot follow. Those are the two changes the criteria are looking at here: designing the address for listeners who are not in the room is how a presentation is organised clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of its audience, and holding the register of a broadcast is how it keeps language appropriate to the formal setting.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The requirement is about design, not about pretending a crowd is there.","Ask what a conversation with one familiar person lets you leave out, and then forbid yourself all of it.","Name a form that has a wider audience built into it, then say what it changes about how you address the room."],"codes":["4.4 Content (key requirements)","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (making presentations and speeches)","4.4 Merit","4.4 General criteria"]},{"q":"You have agreed with your teacher to give a ten-minute formal talk to your class on whether your school should give up one week of the summer term to compulsory community work. Plan the talk and write its opening, then script one exchange from the questions afterwards, so that all four Distinction criteria are met: expresses sophisticated ideas/information/feelings using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary; organises and structures his or her presentation using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience; achieves the purpose of his or her presentation; listens to questions/feedback, responds perceptively and if appropriate elaborates with further ideas and information. Graded against those four criteria, so it carries 4.","a":"A plan built on an idea with a complication in it, several named engagement strategies across the talk, a request the audience is actually asked to act on, and one scripted exchange showing a perceptive answer that elaborates.","sol":"Plan. The idea, with the complication kept in: the phrase 'compulsory community work' is at war with itself, because what makes voluntary work worth anything is that nobody required it — so the case has to be that the school would not be compelling the caring, only the introduction, and that those are different things. Structure, with the strategies marked: (1) open on an object rather than a proposal — the sign-up sheet on the corridor wall with four names on it in a year group of two hundred; (2) put the objection first, in the words the class would use ('you cannot make people care'), so that nobody spends the talk waiting to raise it; (3) three arguments in rising order — that most of us never meet this work at all, that the few who do are already the ones with the contacts, and, hardest to answer, that a week is long enough to be an introduction and too short to be exploitation; (4) direct address to the class, who would be the people giving up the week; (5) the return, to a second sheet pinned up in July with nobody made to sign it; (6) the request, with a date. Opening: 'There is a sheet on the corridor wall outside the hall. It has been there since February, it invites this year group to help at the day centre on the main road, and it has four names on it. There are two hundred of us. I am not going to argue that the other hundred and ninety-six are selfish. I am going to argue something less comfortable: that almost none of them has ever been asked properly, and that a school can repair that in a week.' The purpose is achieved by ending on something this room can do: the student council votes on the proposal on the ninth, and I am asking for our year group's name on the submission before then. Scripted exchange. Questioner: 'If you force people to volunteer, it is not volunteering. So what is the point?' Response: 'The objection underneath that question is that compulsion spoils the thing it is trying to produce, and I agree with it more than you might expect. So let me be exact about what I am asking to be compulsory. Not the caring, which cannot be set as homework; the introduction. The week would be compulsory in the way a fire drill is compulsory — everybody is walked through it once, so that what they do afterwards is a choice rather than an accident. And I would test it that way rather than by how good it made us feel: count how many of us go back in July, when nobody is taking a register. If that number is not higher than four, I will tell the council the week failed, and I will say it in this room.'","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A sophisticated idea usually has a complication inside it: find the part of your own case that is genuinely hard to defend.","Distinction asks for a range of strategies, so mark them on the plan — where the object opens, where the audience is addressed, where the opening returns.","For the exchange, write the objection you would least like to hear, then answer the thing underneath it before you answer the words."],"codes":["4.4 Distinction","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)","3.1.3 responding to spoken language"]},{"q":"A student's rehearsed closing, at the Pass standard, ends a talk arguing that the school should let the field margin grow into a wildflower strip: 'So that is my idea. I think it would be nice for the school and it would be good for bees. Thank you for listening.' Rewrite this closing to the Distinction standard, on the same subject, in about a hundred words. Graded against two criteria: 'expresses sophisticated ideas/information/feelings using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary' and 'organises and structures his or her presentation using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience', so it carries 2.","a":"The vague approval ('nice', 'good for bees') replaced by precise, chosen vocabulary, and at least two deliberate closing strategies — a paradox, a question turned on the school's own word, a sensory promise, a dated request — instead of an announcement that the talk has ended.","sol":"'So I am not asking the school to plant anything. I am asking it to stop cutting one strip, three metres wide, along the fence by the tennis courts — which makes this the only proposal you will hear this year that costs less than doing nothing. In June that strip was mown twice a week to keep it looking cared for. Cared for by whom, and for whom? Give it back to the knapweed and the bees, and by July it will be the noisiest piece of ground on this site. The governors meet on the twelfth. I would like to take nine signatures with me.' The announcement has gone, because a closing that says 'So that is my idea' spends its last seconds on the one thing the audience already knows. The vocabulary is exact rather than approving: 'three metres wide', 'mown twice a week', 'knapweed' — a repertoire used because the words are the right ones, not because they are impressive. And four strategies are at work, each doing a different job: the paradox of a proposal that costs less than doing nothing; the school's own phrase, 'cared for', turned into a question the room has to answer; the sensory promise of 'the noisiest piece of ground on this site', which the audience can hear rather than merely agree with; and a request with a date and a number attached, so that the talk ends by asking rather than by stopping.","m":2,"d":3,"hints":["Every word the audience could have predicted from the title is a word doing no work.","Turn the general approval into something specific and countable, and let the detail carry the claim.","Count the distinct strategies in your rewrite: two or more, working differently, is what a range means."],"codes":["4.4 Distinction","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)","3.1.3 spoken Standard English"]},{"q":"Take one idea of your own and write the same ninety seconds of a presentation twice — about two hundred spoken words each, and the two versions the same length, so that nothing in the comparison comes from one of them simply saying more: once so that it meets all four Merit criteria and no more, and once so that it meets all four Distinction criteria. Then write four sentences, one for each Merit and Distinction pair of criteria, saying what the second version does that the first does not. Graded against eight criteria — the four Merit and the four Distinction — so it carries 8.","a":"Two versions of the same ninety seconds, the first clear, purposeful and detailed, the second lifted on vocabulary, engagement strategies and the depth of the response, with four sentences comparing them criterion by criterion.","sol":"Merit version: 'I want to persuade you that our year group should be allowed to run the school's social media account. There are three reasons. First, we are the audience it is aimed at, so we know what people our age will actually read; the posts that get shared in our year are the ones about people, and the ones nobody opens are the notices. Second, it would be good training — several of us want to work in media, and this is real practice with a real audience rather than a written unit of coursework. Third, it costs the school nothing but supervision: we already have the phones in our pockets, and we would write in tutor time, not in lesson time. I know there are risks. Somebody could post something the school would then have to take down, or name a student who did not want naming, and I am not going to pretend that could not happen. So I would accept a member of staff approving every post before it goes out, and a rule that nobody is named without being asked first. So I am asking the senior team for a trial of one term, starting in September, and I am asking you to sign the request tonight.' Distinction version: 'Every week, our school publishes itself to the world in about fifteen posts, and not one of them is written by the people it is describing. I want to persuade you that our year group should write them. Not because we would be better at it — though I think we would — but because an institution that talks about young people without ever letting them hold the microphone has made a choice, and I do not think it has noticed making it. Three arguments, and the third is the one I would defend hardest. We are the audience: we know which posts are read and which are wallpaper. We are the apprentices: several of us want this for a living, and there is no substitute for an audience that can see you get it wrong. And we are the subject — the difference between being photographed and being asked. The risk is real, and it is not that we would be careless; it is that we would be careless about somebody else. I would accept staff approval on every post; I am not asking for the keys, only for the pen. So: one term, fifteen posts a week, and a decision in July about whether we kept your trust.' Criterion by criterion. Ideas and vocabulary: the Merit version states three sound reasons plainly; the Distinction version organises them round one governing idea — being described versus describing yourself — and uses a repertoire that is precise rather than decorative ('institution', 'apprentices', 'the difference between being photographed and being asked'). Structure and engagement: the Merit version signposts clearly for the audience with 'first, second, third'; the Distinction version adds a range of strategies — an opening statistic of its own making, a tricolon, a concession, a metaphor of the microphone and the pen, and a closing three-part offer. Achieving the purpose: both ask for something, but the Distinction version names the size, the length and the date of the decision, so the request is one the senior team could actually approve. Responding: at Merit I would answer a question about risk formally and in detail; at Distinction I would hear that the question is really about trust rather than risk, answer that, and then elaborate by offering a review point the questioner had not asked for.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Write the Merit version first and deliberately hold it there — clear, purposeful, detailed, and no more.","Lift it by changing two things: the vocabulary's precision and the number of different engagement strategies at work.","Your four comparison sentences should each name one criterion and the single difference that lifts it."],"codes":["4.4 Merit","4.4 Distinction","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)","3.1.3 responding to spoken language"]},{"q":"Your teacher has agreed the motion for a formal class debate, held in the hall: 'This house would lengthen the school day by one hour and abolish homework.' You are proposing the motion in a five-minute speech, and the opposition may rise twice during it to put a point of information. Write the first two hundred words of the speech as you would say them aloud; for each of the two points of information say whether you would take it and give the words you would answer with; write the two-sentence right of reply that answers the strongest thing the opposition said; and set down the three delivery decisions you have made for the hall. Graded against seven requirements — the three the two general-criteria bullets print (be audible; and, under 'use Spoken Standard English', be intelligible and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation) and the four Distinction criteria — so it carries 7.","a":"A proposing speech built on one governing idea with a complication inside it and several engagement strategies at work; a reasoned decision to take one point of information and defer the other; answers that address the objection underneath the words and elaborate; a reply that concedes before it converts; and delivery decisions aimed at the back of the hall rather than at the page.","sol":"The first two hundred words, as spoken. 'Madam Chair, the motion is about an hour, and I am going to ask this house to stop thinking about hours. Last night I did an hour and forty minutes of homework at a kitchen table, with the television on, a younger brother on the other side of it and no printer anywhere in the house. Two streets away, somebody in this room did the same hour at a desk, in a quiet room, with a parent who happened to know the subject. We were set identical work. We were not given identical conditions. That is the whole of my case, and I want to be honest that it is not really an argument about workload at all. Homework is the one part of school we have quietly moved into the home, and homes are not equal, so we have built an inequality into our own marking and then called the result ability. The proposal is simple, and it is not soft. Keep the work. Move the last hour of it back inside the building — supervised, resourced, a teacher in the room and the school library open. Three arguments, and the third is the one I would defend hardest.' The first point of information, and what I would do with it. The opposition rises during my first argument: 'Will the proposer accept that a longer day is hardest on the students who are caring for somebody at home?' I take it, and I take it at once, because a speech built on fairness cannot decline a fairness question without sounding frightened of it. Aloud: 'I will take the point, and I will not make it smaller than it is. What is really being asked is whether my hour is free for everybody, and for some of us it is not. So I would put the exception in the motion rather than argue it away: a registered carer keeps the hour as work to do at home and keeps the supervision on offer at lunchtime instead. What I will not accept is that the answer to an unequal hour at home is to leave it at home.' The second point of information, and what I would do with it. It comes thirty seconds later, in the middle of my third argument: 'With eight hours already gone by the time we are home, how is this anything but a longer day?' I decline it, and I say why, because a silent refusal reads as evasion while a stated one reads as control. Aloud: 'With respect, I will not take it now — the travel is my third argument and I would rather not lose it halfway through. I will answer it in about forty seconds.' And then I do, inside the argument itself: the day ends earlier than the evening does, because the work that currently follows us home is finished before we leave. The right of reply, two sentences, on the strongest thing said against me — that a supervised hour is not a taught hour, so the proposal buys a study hall and calls it a lesson. 'That is the one point tonight I cannot answer by disagreeing with it: a supervised hour is not a taught hour, and I am not going to stand here and claim it is. But it is the difference between work done where help exists and work done where it happens to, and if this house would rather defend an hour of its own evening than that difference, it should say so in the vote.' The three delivery decisions, made for the hall. First, for audibility: a hall eats the ends of sentences, so I aim the last three words of every sentence at the back wall, and I test that by putting one person in the back row with instructions to write down every word they lose. Second, for intelligibility: I mark a pause after each of the three arguments and honour it even when I can feel the room waiting, and I say the two quantities slowly and once each — 'an hour and forty minutes', 'one hour' — because a listener gets a single pass at a number and no chance to look back. Third, for the formal register: the card carries, written out, the phrases I will need the instant I am interrupted — 'I will take the point'; 'With respect, I will not take it now' — because being interrupted is exactly the moment a speaker's register slips, and a phrase I have already said aloud comes out formal without my having to choose it.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["A debate speech is not a talk: its purpose is a vote, so settle first what you want the house to do at the end.","Decide which of the two points of information you can afford to take and which you should openly defer — the choice itself is part of what is graded.","Open on the hardest version of your own case, the part an opponent would attack, rather than on the proposal."],"codes":["4.4 General criteria","4.4 Distinction","3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (planning for purpose and audience)","3.1.3 responding to spoken language","4.4 Aim of the assessment"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What four outcomes can the Spoken Language endorsement be reported as?","a":"Pass, Merit, Distinction or Not Classified."},{"q":"What does assessment on a competency basis mean here?","a":"A student must hit all the criteria in one grade before moving on to the next, so the weakest criterion sets the grade."},{"q":"What must a student do about the subject of the presentation before any drafting begins?","a":"Identify it in advance and agree it with the teacher."},{"q":"As a guide, how long should the prepared spoken presentation be?","a":"No more than ten minutes."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Spoken Standard English, as this assessment defines it","back":"Being intelligible, and generally using language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation. It is about standard forms and formal register, not about accent.","code":"3.1.3 spoken Standard English","open":false},{"front":"The general criteria","back":"Required before any grade: be audible; use Spoken Standard English — be intelligible, and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation.","code":"4.4 General criteria","open":false},{"front":"Competency basis","back":"No marks are assigned; a student must hit all the criteria in one grade before moving on to the next, so the weakest criterion sets the grade.","code":"4.4 Assessment (competency basis)","open":false},{"front":"Not Classified","back":"What is recorded for a student who does not reach the Pass standard.","code":"4.4 Assessment (competency basis)","open":false},{"front":"The four Pass criteria","back":"Expresses straightforward ideas/information/feelings; makes an attempt to organise and structure the presentation; makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience; listens to questions/feedback and responds appropriately in a straight forward manner.","code":"4.4 Pass","open":false},{"front":"The four Merit criteria","back":"Challenging ideas using a range of vocabulary; organises and structures clearly and appropriately to meet the audience's needs; achieves the purpose; listens to questions/feedback responding formally and in some detail.","code":"4.4 Merit","open":false},{"front":"The four Distinction criteria","back":"Sophisticated ideas using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary; structures using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience; achieves the purpose; responds perceptively and, if appropriate, elaborates with further ideas and information.","code":"4.4 Distinction","open":false},{"front":"The criterion that first appears at Merit","back":"Achieves the purpose of his or her presentation — the audience must actually be informed, persuaded or asked for something, not merely spoken to.","code":"4.4 Merit","open":false},{"front":"A form the prepared presentation may take (example)","back":"A talk; a debate; a dialogue such as an interview. Whatever the form, it must be formal.","code":"3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (making presentations and speeches)","open":true},{"front":"The duration guide","back":"A prepared spoken presentation on a specific topic; as a guide, the duration should be no more than ten minutes.","code":"4.4 Content (key requirements)","open":false},{"front":"Agreeing the subject","back":"Students must identify the subject for their presentations in advance and agree it with their teacher; presentations must be planned and organised.","code":"3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (planning for purpose and audience)","open":false},{"front":"Pre-prepared notes and slides","back":"Students may use pre-prepared notes, powerpoint etc. to assist them during their presentation, but this is not a requirement.","code":"4.4 Content (key requirements)","open":false},{"front":"A strategy for engaging an audience (example)","back":"Open with a concrete scene and return to it at the end; address the audience directly as 'you'; put the case against yourself before answering it.","code":"3.1.3 presenting information and ideas (selecting and organising)","open":true},{"front":"Eliciting clarification","back":"Asking the questioner a question of your own to check what they mean before you answer. The aim of the assessment names it as part of responding well.","code":"3.1.3 responding to spoken language","open":false},{"front":"The audience-of-one rule","back":"Where the audience is the teacher only, the presentation and dialogue must be designed so that it could have a potentially wider audience than just one person, for example by replicating a television interview.","code":"4.4 Content (key requirements)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The passages written for this room — read these before the questions","items":["Passage A — 'Why first aid belongs on every timetable' (a model presentation script written for this room: a talk to a Year 10 tutor group, with the teacher present, of about three minutes spoken):\nLast summer, my grandfather collapsed in the queue at the post office, and for eleven seconds nobody moved. I know it was eleven seconds because I have counted them ever since. I was fourteen; I had never been taught what to do; and the reason this story has an ending is that the woman behind the counter had been on a first aid course in March.\nI am not going to argue this morning that first aid matters — you already believe that. I am going to argue something harder: that believing it and being unable to do it are the same thing at the moment it counts. So this is what I want to persuade you of: that first aid should be taught to every student in this school, in timetabled lessons, before we leave Year 11. Three reasons, and then exactly what I am asking for.\nMy first reason is that this is a genuinely learnable skill. Chest compressions and the recovery position can be taught in a single afternoon.\nMy second reason is that the person standing closest is almost never a doctor. If the skill sits only with professionals, it is in the wrong place.\nMy third reason is the hardest to say. Doing nothing is not neutral: for eleven seconds, none of us chose, because none of us knew how.\nSo here is what I am asking for. Not a campaign, not a petition: one timetabled afternoon a year, for every year group, taught properly and practised again the year after. If it is ever your grandfather in that queue, you will not wish we had spent that hour on something else. Thank you. I am happy to take questions.","Passage B — 'Save the 46' (a model presentation script written for this room: a speech given by a student representative at a town council transport consultation, of about three minutes spoken):\nChair, councillors, thank you for hearing me tonight. I am here as a student representative, speaking for the forty or so of us who could not come, because we are all doing the thing the number 46 makes possible: a shift, a music lesson, a walk home that does not take an hour.\nOn paper, a bus route is a line on a map. It is not. It is a sixth form a student can actually reach. It is an evening job that does not have to end at the moment the last bus leaves.\nYou have been told that the 46 is quiet. It is quiet at eleven in the morning. It is not quiet at four o'clock, when it carries three schools' worth of us home, standing, in wet coats, apologising to each other for our bags. The figures the committee has seen are an average, and an average is a very efficient way of losing an afternoon.\nSo I would ask the committee one question this evening. When we say that a service is not viable, who exactly are we saying is not viable?\nI am not asking you to ignore the accounts, but to read them alongside the people they describe. Cut the 46 and you will not save a bus. You will move the cost — on to parents with cars, on to students without them, and on to a town that has quietly made itself harder to grow up in.\nKeep the route for one more year. Count it at four o'clock, not at eleven. Let us prove the afternoon to you ourselves, and if we are wrong, I will come back to this room and say so. Thank you.","Passage C — 'The questions after the talk' (a model question-and-answer exchange written for this room: the minutes immediately following the Passage A talk, showing what listening and responding sound like at the top of the criteria):\nFirst questioner, a classmate: You said one afternoon a year. Would it not all be forgotten by the time anybody actually needed it?\nSpeaker: That is a fair challenge, and it is the reason I said one afternoon a year rather than one afternoon ever. Skills like compressions do fade, so the repetition is the proposal rather than an extra: the second year is not a repeat lesson, it is the first practice.\nSecond questioner, the teacher: How would you answer a governor who told you the timetable is already full?\nSpeaker: Could I check what you mean by full — full of subject lessons, or full of tutor time?\nSecond questioner: Tutor time.\nSpeaker: Then I would answer that tutor time is already where we are taught what to do in a fire and what to do when something goes wrong online, and that first aid belongs in the same category: a rehearsed response to a rare event. I would also offer the governor a compromise. Start with Year 10 only, and test us in June to see whether we can still put somebody into the recovery position.\nThird questioner, a classmate: Why should the school pay for it?\nSpeaker: I do not think it would need to pay a great deal, and I would rather answer the harder version of your question, which is whether this is the school's job at all. I think it is, for the same reason we are taught to swim: school is the only place that reaches all of us."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Distinction standard (the opening of the Passage A talk, 'Why first aid belongs on every timetable')","items":["Last summer, my grandfather collapsed in the queue at the post office, and for eleven seconds nobody moved. I know it was eleven seconds because I have counted them ever since. I was fourteen; I had never been taught what to do; and the reason this story has an ending is that the woman behind the counter had been on a first aid course in March.\nI am not going to argue this morning that first aid matters — you already believe that. I am going to argue something harder: that believing it and being unable to do it are the same thing at the moment it counts. So this is what I want to persuade you of: that first aid should be taught to every student in this school, in timetabled lessons, before we leave Year 11. Three reasons, and then exactly what I am asking for.","The criteria this opening meets. 'Expresses sophisticated ideas/information/feelings using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary': the idea is not 'first aid is important' but the harder claim that 'believing it and being unable to do it are the same thing at the moment it counts', and the vocabulary is exact rather than decorative — 'collapsed', 'counted', 'timetabled', 'genuinely learnable' later on. 'Organises and structures his or her presentation using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience': there are four distinct strategies inside seven sentences — a counted, physical detail ('eleven seconds'), a withheld outcome that is not resolved until the third sentence, a concession that refuses the easy argument ('you already believe that'), and a signpost that tells the audience exactly what is coming ('Three reasons, and then exactly what I am asking for'). It also opens what it will close: the grandfather and the queue return in the last paragraph, so the talk arrives rather than stops.","Why it is still spoken English, not written English. The sentences are short enough to be said in one breath, the semicolons in the third sentence are heard as pauses, and the audience is addressed directly as 'you'. The general criteria ask for language 'appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation' — which this is — not for the vocabulary of an essay.","The two criteria an opening cannot settle. 'Achieves the purpose of his or her presentation' is not decided here: the opening only tells the audience what it is going to be asked for, and the criterion is met in the final paragraph, where the request narrows to 'one timetabled afternoon a year, for every year group' — a size a school could actually agree to, which is what separates a talk that moves an audience from one that asks it for nothing. And 'listens to questions/feedback, responds perceptively and if appropriate elaborates with further ideas and information' cannot be judged from an opening at all; it is decided in the exchange afterwards, which Passage C prints."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Pass standard (the opening of the same presentation, visibly weaker)","items":["I am going to talk to you about first aid. I think first aid is important and I think we should learn it at school. My grandad was ill last summer in the post office and a lady helped him. It was quite scary. So that is why I want the school to teach it.","Which criteria it meets. 'Expresses straightforward ideas/information/feelings': it does — the ideas are clear, they are the speaker's own, and a listener knows what is being claimed. 'Makes an attempt to organise and structure his or her presentation': there is an attempt — an announcement, an opinion, a reason, a conclusion, in that order. 'Makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience': the direct address 'I am going to talk to you' is an attempt, and the setting is one the audience knows. The fourth Pass criterion, listening and responding to questions, cannot be judged from an opening at all; it is decided in the exchange afterwards.","Which criteria it misses, and exactly why. Merit's 'expresses challenging ideas/information/feelings using a range of vocabulary': nothing here could be argued with, and the vocabulary repeats itself in general terms — 'important', 'ill', 'quite scary', 'a lady helped him'. Merit's 'organises and structures... clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience': the order is chronological rather than chosen, and the first sentence spends the opening on the one thing the audience could already have worked out. Merit's 'achieves the purpose': the audience is told what the speaker wants but is never asked for anything. Distinction's 'effective range of strategies to engage the audience': there are none — the incident is summarised ('was ill', 'quite scary') instead of being made into a moment the listener can see, which is the single change that lifts this opening most."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the Spoken Language endorsement: the general criteria and the three grades, in the specification's words","items":["How it is assessed: 'No marks will be assigned to a student's performance – it will be assessed holistically as a grade, using a \"competency\" basis on criteria which are provided below.'","What competency means: 'Competency means that a student must hit all the criteria in one grade before moving on to the next.'","The floor: 'Students who do not reach the Pass standard must be recorded as Not Classified.'","General criteria, printed as two bullets: 'To be awarded a Pass, Merit or Distinction a learner must: be audible; use Spoken Standard English which, for the purposes of the spoken language assessment, means that a learner must – be intelligible; generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation.'","So the specification prints two general criteria, not three, and the second carries two sub-points of its own — be intelligible, and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation. That is why a moderator has three separate things to tick here before any grade column can be opened.","Each grade column opens with the same clause: 'In addition to the general criteria, to be awarded a [Pass / Merit / Distinction] a Learner's performance in his or her spoken language assessment must meet all of the following criteria...'","Pass: 'expresses straightforward ideas/information/feelings'.","Pass: 'makes an attempt to organise and structure his or her presentation'.","Pass: 'makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience'.","Pass: 'listens to questions/feedback and provides an appropriate response in a straight forward manner'.","Merit: 'expresses challenging ideas/information/feelings using a range of vocabulary'.","Merit: 'organises and structures his or her presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience'.","Merit: 'achieves the purpose of his or her presentation'.","Merit: 'listens to questions/feedback responding formally and in some detail'.","Distinction: 'expresses sophisticated ideas/information/feelings using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary'.","Distinction: 'organises and structures his or her presentation using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience'.","Distinction: 'achieves the purpose of his or her presentation'.","Distinction: 'listens to questions/feedback, responds perceptively and if appropriate elaborates with further ideas and information'.","What is assessed: AO7 'Demonstrate presentation skills in a formal setting'; AO8 'Listen and respond appropriately to spoken language, including to questions and feedback on presentations'; AO9 'Use spoken Standard English effectively in speeches and presentations.'","The performance: 'Students must undertake a prepared spoken presentation on a specific topic... As a guide, the duration should be no more than ten minutes', and 'presentations must be formal but may take a wide variety of forms, including talks, debates, speeches and dialogues'.","Preparation: 'students must identify the subject for their presentations in advance and agree it with their teacher'; 'presentations must be planned and organised. Students should be advised that that lack of preparation is likely to prevent access to the criteria for the higher grades'; students 'may use pre-prepared notes, powerpoint etc. to assist them during their presentations but this is not a requirement'.","Responding is a requirement, not an extra: 'as part of, or following, the presentation students must listen to and respond appropriately to questions and feedback'.","The audience-of-one rule: 'where the audience is the teacher only, the presentation and dialogue must be designed in such a way that it could have a potentially wider audience than just one person (eg it replicates a television interview)'.","Reporting: it 'will appear on all students' certificates as a separately reported grade, alongside the overall grade issued', is reported as 'Pass, Merit, Distinction or Not Classified' and 'will not contribute to the result of the GCSE English Language qualification'. Centres provide complete, unedited audio-visual recordings of a sample: all students where there are thirty or fewer, thirty where there are more, with a minimum of ten at each grade."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — a prepared spoken presentation on a subject agreed with the teacher: 'Our town should keep its last late bus' (a formal talk of about eight minutes to the class, with questions afterwards)","items":["The task, decoded. The requirement is a prepared spoken presentation on a specific topic, formal, no more than ten minutes as a guide, on a subject identified in advance and agreed with the teacher, followed by questions and feedback I must listen and respond to. So there are three things to prepare, not one: the talk, the delivery, and the exchange afterwards — and the exchange is graded by the same criteria as the talk.","The subject, as agreed with the teacher, in one sentence: 'I want to persuade this class that the town should keep the last evening bus, and to get them to respond to the consultation before it closes on the twenty-first.' Purpose: to persuade and to obtain an action. Audience: my class and my teacher — people who mostly do not use the bus, which is the difficulty the talk has to solve.","The moves, in order, with the engagement strategy marked against each. (1) A scene, not an announcement: the last bus pulling out of the square with four people on it. (2) The concession: state the council's case in the council's own vocabulary — viability, subsidy, passenger numbers — so the audience knows I have read what they have read. (3) Argument one, convenience, dealt with quickly because it is the weakest and everybody expects it. (4) Argument two, work: the evening shift that cannot end before the last bus does. (5) Argument three, the one I would defend hardest: withdrawing an evening service quietly narrows what a young person in this town is allowed to do. (6) Direct address: the reason this class should care even though most of them will not use it. (7) The return: the four passengers from the opening, now named. (8) The request, with a date.","The evidence, and where each piece goes. The council's own report language goes in move 2, because conceding early buys the right to argue later. The timetable itself — the last departure time against the end of an evening shift — goes in move 4, where it does the most work. The one thing I will not do is invent a figure: if I do not have a number, I say so and give the argument that does not need it.","The vocabulary decisions, made before rehearsal. Precise nouns for the abstractions I will be tempted to blur: 'viability', 'subsidy', 'provision', 'consultation'. Two images I will use once each and not repeat: the square at ten past eleven, and the difference between a fare you can budget for and one that changes with demand. Every 'stuff', 'thing' and 'kind of' cut from the card in advance.","The timing. Eight minutes: roughly one for the opening scene, one for the concession, four for the three arguments (with the longest on the third), one for the direct address and the return, one for the request — and then the questions, which are not part of the eight. Rehearsed aloud three times, standing, in the room itself, with a single card at chest height rather than a script, so my head stays up and the back of the room can hear.","The questions I will be asked, and the preparation for them. 'Isn't this nostalgia?' 'Who pays for it?' 'Why should we care if we don't use it?' For each one I write down the objection underneath the question before I write the answer, because the criteria reward responding perceptively — hearing what is really being asked — and then elaborating with something the questioner did not have. If a question could be taken two ways, I ask which way is meant before I answer it.","What the last move must do. Not summarise. The closing has to convert the talk into the action the purpose named — the consultation closes on the twenty-first, the form takes two minutes, and I will bring the link to tutor time tomorrow — and it should land on the opening image, so the audience hears the talk close a circle rather than run out. A talk that moves an audience and asks them for nothing has not achieved its purpose, and 'achieves the purpose of his or her presentation' is the criterion that appears in both the Merit and the Distinction column."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T10:35:56Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All twelve fixes are genuine and none broke anything: the four replaced questions (0, 1, 10, 11) no longer have their answers printed elsewhere, the trimmed passages now run to 285 and 286 words of script with every quotation elsewhere still word for word, question 7's 'closes, in the three sentences just before its final Thank you' is exactly what Passage B prints, and every one of the fourteen tariffs now counts grading criteria the stem names — but question 13 still asks the student to reproduce the plan extra, one anticipated question verbatim, and question 12's two versions differ in length by a third."}}}} };
